{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822505049,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_f92ec3414cff49748e7f2983","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":400},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1:  \n2:  \n3:  \n4: L3: POST AUDIT ACTIVITIES \n5:  \n6: TOPIC OVERVIEW \n7: A.​ INTRODUCTION: WHAT ARE POST-AUDIT \n8: ACTIVITIES?  \n9: B.​ EXIT INTERVIEW  \n10: a.​\n11: Participants In The Exit Interview  \n12: b.​\n13: Purpose Of The Exit Interview  \n14: c.​\n15: How Auditors Use The Exit \n16: Interview \n17: d.​\n18: What To Prepare Before The \n19: Auditors Leave  \n20: e.​\n21: Advantages And Limitations  \n22: C.​ DATA ANALYSIS  \n23: a.​\n24: The Five Steps To Get You Started \n25: With Data Analysis \n26: D.​ GENERATION OF AUDIT REPORTS  \n27: a.​\n28: Audit Report Format  \n29: E.​ PRESENTATION FINDINGS \n30: a.​\n31: Purpose Of The Presentation Of \n32: Findings   \n33: b.​\n34: How Findings Are Presented  \n35: c.​\n36: What To Prepare Before The \n37: Presentation  \n38: d.​\n39: Advantages And Limitations \n40:  \n41: A \n42: INTRODUCTION: WHAT ARE POST-AUDIT \n43: ACTIVITIES?  \n44:  \n45: WHAT ARE POST-AUDIT ACTIVITIES?  \n46: ●​\n47: Post-audit \n48: activities \n49: are \n50: the \n51: activities \n52: performed \n53: after \n54: the \n55: audit \n56: has \n57: been \n58: conducted to review, analyze, document, \n59: and communicate the results of the audit.  \n60: ○​\n61: They \n62: help \n63: the \n64: organization \n65: understand \n66: the \n67: audit \n68: findings, \n69: address \n70: identified \n71: issues, \n72: and \n73: determine appropriate actions for \n74: improvement.  \n75:  \n76:  \n77: B \n78: EXIT INTERVIEW  \n79:  \n80: EXIT INTERVIEW  \n81: ●​\n82: An exit interview, also called an exit or \n83: closing meeting, is a meeting held at the \n84: conclusion \n85: of \n86: audit \n87: fieldwork, \n88: when \n89: necessary, to discuss audit findings, resolve \n90: questions \n91: or \n92: concerns, \n93: and \n94: address \n95: outstanding issues before the final audit \n96: report is released.  \n97:  \n98: OBJECTIVES \n99: ●​\n100: Clarify  \n101: ○​\n102: Resolve \n103: questions \n104: or \n105: concerns \n106: about the audit results.  \n107: ●​\n108: Discuss  \n109: ○​\n110: Address other issues or outstanding \n111: matters before finalization.  \n112: ●​\n113: Prepare  \n114: ○​\n115: Allow the audit client to provide \n116: responses and comments before the \n117: final report is issued.  \n118:  \n119: WHERE DOES IT FIT? \n120: ●​\n121: Audit Fieldwork  \n122: ●​\n123: Exit Interview  \n124: ●​\n125: Management Responses & Comments  \n126: ●​\n127: Draft Report Review  \n128: ●​\n129: Final Audit Report  \n130:  \n131: B1 \n132: PARTICIPANTS IN THE EXIT INTERVIEW  \n133:  \n134: AUDIT TEAM \n135: ●​\n136: Conducts and facilitates the audit  \n137: ●​\n138: Presents and explains the audit results  \n139: ●​\n140: e.g., Lead Auditor / Audit Team Leader  \n141: ●​\n142: Purpose of Their Participation  \n143: ○​\n144: Present \n145: and \n146: explain \n147: the \n148: audit \n149: findings, facilitate the discussion, and \n150: address questions or concerns about \n151: the audit  \n152:  \n153: MANAGEMENT OF THE AUDITED ENTITY \n154: ●​\n155: e.g., Dean, Chairperson, Director, or managers \n156: responsible for the audited functions  \n157: ●​\n158: Purpose of Their Participation  \n159: ○​\n160: Review \n161: the \n162: findings, \n163: provide \n164: explanations or additional context, \n165: and \n166: respond to issues identified \n167: during the audit  \n168:  \n169: OTHER PARTICIPANTS  \n170: ●​\n171: Other individuals invited by the audit client  \n172: ●​\n173: Purpose of Their Participation  \n174: ○​\n175: Provide \n176: relevant \n177: information, \n178: clarification, or perspective regarding \n179: the audited processes or findings.  \n180:  \n181:  \n182:  \n183:  \n184: PAGE 1 \n185: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n186:  \n187: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n188: ITAS311-T \n189: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n190: \f \n191:  \n192: B2 \n193: PURPOSE OF THE EXIT INTERVIEW  \n194:  \n195: PURPOSE OF THE EXIT INTERVIEW \n196: ●​\n197: The exit interview gives the auditor and the \n198: audited entity an opportunity to discuss \n199: findings, clarify concerns, and establish a \n200: common understanding before the report \n201: is finalized.  \n202:  \n203: PURPOSE OF THE EXIT INTERVIEW \n204: ●​\n205: Clarify Findings  \n206: ○​\n207: Discuss audit findings and resolve \n208: misunderstandings about the audit \n209: process, evidence, or conclusions.  \n210: ●​\n211: Obtain Management Response  \n212: ○​\n213: Allow \n214: management \n215: to \n216: provide \n217: explanations, \n218: additional \n219: context, \n220: and responses to identified issues.  \n221: ●​\n222: Discuss Improvements  \n223: ○​\n224: Discuss \n225: recommendations, \n226: corrective \n227: actions, \n228: responsibilities, \n229: and timelines.  \n230: ●​\n231: Identify Risks & Weaknesses  \n232: ○​\n233: Provide \n234: an \n235: objective \n236: view \n237: of \n238: weaknesses \n239: and \n240: areas \n241: requiring \n242: attention.  \n243:  \n244: B3 \n245: HOW AUDITORS USE THE EXIT \n246: INTERVIEW  \n247:  \n248: HOW AUDITORS USE THE EXIT INTERVIEW  \n249: ●​\n250: During the exit interview, auditors use the \n251: meeting to identify and discuss missing or \n252: outstanding information that may still be \n253: needed before the audit is completed.  \n254:  \n255: TWO KEY QUESTIONS  \n256: ●​\n257: WHO?  \n258: ○​\n259: Who is responsible for providing the \n260: missing information?  \n261: ●​\n262: WHEN?  \n263: ○​\n264: When can the auditor expect the \n265: information to be provided?  \n266:  \n267: EXAMPLE  \n268: ●​\n269: IT department hasn't submitted its latest \n270: access control review or user-permission \n271: logs.  \n272: ○​\n273: During the exit interview, the audit \n274: team \n275: identifies \n276: that \n277: the \n278: IT \n279: department has not submitted its \n280: latest \n281: access-control \n282: review \n283: or \n284: user-permission logs. The auditor asks \n285: who is responsible for providing the \n286: information \n287: and \n288: establishes \n289: a \n290: deadline for submission before the \n291: report is finalized.  \n292:  \n293: B4 \n294: WHAT TO PREPARE BEFORE THE \n295: AUDITORS LEAVE  \n296:  \n297: WHAT TO PREPARE BEFORE THE AUDITORS LEAVE  \n298: ●​\n299: Set a Target Date for the Draft Audit Report  \n300: ○​\n301: Establish when the draft audit report \n302: is expected to be completed.  \n303: ●​\n304: Determine the Approval Timeline  \n305: ○​\n306: Estimate how long the organization's \n307: internal review and approval process \n308: will take.  \n309: ●​\n310: Set a Target Date for the Final Report  \n311: ○​\n312: Agree on when the final audit report \n313: is expected to be issued.  \n314:  \n315: Establishing these dates helps the organization \n316: schedule tasks according to the audit timeline and \n317: keeps everyone accountable for meeting agreed \n318: deadlines.  \n319:  \n320: B5 \n321: ADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS \n322:  \n323: ADVANTAGES \n324: ●​\n325: RESOLVES ISSUES EARLY  \n326: ○​\n327: Allows questions, misunderstandings, \n328: and \n329: missing \n330: information \n331: to \n332: be \n333: addressed \n334: before \n335: the \n336: report \n337: is \n338: finalized.  \n339: ●​\n340: KEEPS EVERYONE ACCOUNTABLE  \n341: ○​\n342: Establishes responsibilities and target \n343: dates for outstanding items and \n344: reporting.  \n345: ●​\n346: IMPROVES ACCURACY  \n347: ○​\n348: Allows the audited entity to provide \n349: additional \n350: context \n351: and \n352: correct \n353: possible misunderstandings or errors.  \n354: ●​\n355: PREVENTS SURPRISES  \n356: ○​\n357: Ensures that findings and next steps \n358: are discussed before the final report is \n359: issued.  \n360:  \n361: LIMITATIONS \n362: ●​\n363: NOT REQUIRED IN EVERY AUDIT  \n364: ○​\n365: The exit interview is held when \n366: necessary rather than being required \n367: for every audit.  \n368: ●​\n369: TIME CONSTRAINTS  \n370: ○​\n371: Limited time may be available to \n372: present findings, answer questions, \n373: and discuss outstanding issues.  \n374:  \n375:  \n376:  \n377: PAGE 2 \n378: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n379:  \n380: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n381: ITAS311-T \n382: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n383: \f \n384:  \n385: ●​\n386: ISSUES MAY REMAIN UNRESOLVED  \n387: ○​\n388: Disagreements between the auditor \n389: and audited entity may still remain. If \n390: differences of opinion remain, they \n391: may simply be documented and \n392: included in the audit report.  \n393: ●​\n394: COMMUNICATION GAPS  \n395: ○​\n396: Technical \n397: audit \n398: findings may be \n399: difficult for some participants to \n400: understand.  \n\n(Showing lines 1-400 of 5328. Use offset=401 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":" \n \n \nL3: POST AUDIT ACTIVITIES \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ INTRODUCTION: WHAT ARE POST-AUDIT \nACTIVITIES?  \nB.​ EXIT INTERVIEW  \na.​\nParticipants In The Exit Interview  \nb.​\nPurpose Of The Exit Interview  \nc.​\nHow Auditors Use The Exit \nInterview \nd.​\nWhat To Prepare Before The \nAuditors Leave  \ne.​","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":" \n \n \nL3: POST AUDIT ACTIVITIES \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ INTRODUCTION: WHAT ARE POST-AUDIT \nACTIVITIES?  \nB.​ EXIT INTERVIEW  \na.​\nParticipants In The Exit Interview  \nb.​\nPurpose Of The Exit Interview  \nc.​\nHow Auditors Use The Exit \nInterview \nd.​\nWhat To Prepare Before The \nAuditors Leave  \ne.​\nAdvantages And Limitations  \nC.​ DATA ANALYSIS  \na.​\nThe Five Steps To Get You Started \nWith Data Analysis \nD.​ GENERATION OF AUDIT REPORTS  \na.​\nAudit Report Format  \nE.​ PRESENTATION FINDINGS \na.​\nPurpose Of The Presentation Of \nFindings   \nb.​\nHow Findings Are Presented  \nc.​\nWhat To Prepare Before The \nPresentation  \nd.​\nAdvantages And Limitations \n \nA \nINTRODUCTION: WHAT ARE POST-AUDIT \nACTIVITIES?  \n \nWHAT ARE POST-AUDIT ACTIVITIES?  \n●​\nPost-audit \nactivities \nare \nthe \nactivities \nperformed \nafter \nthe \naudit \nhas \nbeen \nconducted to review, analyze, document, \nand communicate the results of the audit.  \n○​\nThey \nhelp \nthe \norganization \nunderstand \nthe \naudit \nfindings, \naddress \nidentified \nissues, \nand \ndetermine appropriate actions for \nimprovement.  \n \n \nB \nEXIT INTERVIEW  \n \nEXIT INTERVIEW  \n●​\nAn exit interview, also called an exit or \nclosing meeting, is a meeting held at the \nconclusion \nof \naudit \nfieldwork, \nwhen \nnecessary, to discuss audit findings, resolve \nquestions \nor \nconcerns, \nand \naddress \noutstanding issues before the final audit \nreport is released.  \n \nOBJECTIVES \n●​\nClarify  \n○​\nResolve \nquestions \nor \nconcerns \nabout the audit results.  \n●​\nDiscuss  \n○​\nAddress other issues or outstanding \nmatters before finalization.  \n●​\nPrepare  \n○​\nAllow the audit client to provide \nresponses and comments before the \nfinal report is issued.  \n \nWHERE DOES IT FIT? \n●​\nAudit Fieldwork  \n●​\nExit Interview  \n●​\nManagement Responses & Comments  \n●​\nDraft Report Review  \n●​\nFinal Audit Report  \n \nB1 \nPARTICIPANTS IN THE EXIT INTERVIEW  \n \nAUDIT TEAM \n●​\nConducts and facilitates the audit  \n●​\nPresents and explains the audit results  \n●​\ne.g., Lead Auditor / Audit Team Leader  \n●​\nPurpose of Their Participation  \n○​\nPresent \nand \nexplain \nthe \naudit \nfindings, facilitate the discussion, and \naddress questions or concerns about \nthe audit  \n \nMANAGEMENT OF THE AUDITED ENTITY \n●​\ne.g., Dean, Chairperson, Director, or managers \nresponsible for the audited functions  \n●​\nPurpose of Their Participation  \n○​\nReview \nthe \nfindings, \nprovide \nexplanations or additional context, \nand \nrespond to issues identified \nduring the audit  \n \nOTHER PARTICIPANTS  \n●​\nOther individuals invited by the audit client  \n●​\nPurpose of Their Participation  \n○​\nProvide \nrelevant \ninformation, \nclarification, or perspective regarding \nthe audited processes or findings.  \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 1 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nB2 \nPURPOSE OF THE EXIT INTERVIEW  \n \nPURPOSE OF THE EXIT INTERVIEW \n●​\nThe exit interview gives the auditor and the \naudited entity an opportunity to discuss \nfindings, clarify concerns, and establish a \ncommon understanding before the report \nis finalized.  \n \nPURPOSE OF THE EXIT INTERVIEW \n●​\nClarify Findings  \n○​\nDiscuss audit findings and resolve \nmisunderstandings about the audit \nprocess, evidence, or conclusions.  \n●​\nObtain Management Response  \n○​\nAllow \nmanagement \nto \nprovide \nexplanations, \nadditional \ncontext, \nand responses to identified issues.  \n●​\nDiscuss Improvements  \n○​\nDiscuss \nrecommendations, \ncorrective \nactions, \nresponsibilities, \nand timelines.  \n●​\nIdentify Risks & Weaknesses  \n○​\nProvide \nan \nobjective \nview \nof \nweaknesses \nand \nareas \nrequiring \nattention.  \n \nB3 \nHOW AUDITORS USE THE EXIT \nINTERVIEW  \n \nHOW AUDITORS USE THE EXIT INTERVIEW  \n●​\nDuring the exit interview, auditors use the \nmeeting to identify and discuss missing or \noutstanding information that may still be \nneeded before the audit is completed.  \n \nTWO KEY QUESTIONS  \n●​\nWHO?  \n○​\nWho is responsible for providing the \nmissing information?  \n●​\nWHEN?  \n○​\nWhen can the auditor expect the \ninformation to be provided?  \n \nEXAMPLE  \n●​\nIT department hasn't submitted its latest \naccess control review or user-permission \nlogs.  \n○​\nDuring the exit interview, the audit \nteam \nidentifies \nthat \nthe \nIT \ndepartment has not submitted its \nlatest \naccess-control \nreview \nor \nuser-permission logs. The auditor asks \nwho is responsible for providing the \ninformation \nand \nestablishes \na \ndeadline for submission before the \nreport is finalized.  \n \nB4 \nWHAT TO PREPARE BEFORE THE \nAUDITORS LEAVE  \n \nWHAT TO PREPARE BEFORE THE AUDITORS LEAVE  \n●​\nSet a Target Date for the Draft Audit Report  \n○​\nEstablish when the draft audit report \nis expected to be completed.  \n●​\nDetermine the Approval Timeline  \n○​\nEstimate how long the organization's \ninternal review and approval process \nwill take.  \n●​\nSet a Target Date for the Final Report  \n○​\nAgree on when the final audit report \nis expected to be issued.  \n \nEstablishing these dates helps the organization \nschedule tasks according to the audit timeline and \nkeeps everyone accountable for meeting agreed \ndeadlines.  \n \nB5 \nADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS \n \nADVANTAGES \n●​\nRESOLVES ISSUES EARLY  \n○​\nAllows questions, misunderstandings, \nand \nmissing \ninformation \nto \nbe \naddressed \nbefore \nthe \nreport \nis \nfinalized.  \n●​\nKEEPS EVERYONE ACCOUNTABLE  \n○​\nEstablishes responsibilities and target \ndates for outstanding items and \nreporting.  \n●​\nIMPROVES ACCURACY  \n○​\nAllows the audited entity to provide \nadditional \ncontext \nand \ncorrect \npossible misunderstandings or errors.  \n●​\nPREVENTS SURPRISES  \n○​\nEnsures that findings and next steps \nare discussed before the final report is \nissued.  \n \nLIMITATIONS \n●​\nNOT REQUIRED IN EVERY AUDIT  \n○​\nThe exit interview is held when \nnecessary rather than being required \nfor every audit.  \n●​\nTIME CONSTRAINTS  \n○​\nLimited time may be available to \npresent findings, answer questions, \nand discuss outstanding issues.  \n \n \n \nPAGE 2 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nISSUES MAY REMAIN UNRESOLVED  \n○​\nDisagreements between the auditor \nand audited entity may still remain. If \ndifferences of opinion remain, they \nmay simply be documented and \nincluded in the audit report.  \n●​\nCOMMUNICATION GAPS  \n○​\nTechnical \naudit \nfindings may be \ndifficult for some participants to \nunderstand.  ","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":400,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822504948,"end":1789822505016}},"id":"prt_0b9bc05db001BLZeC4hY5bFGkX","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bbffd2001HwC27AeTmMwL1f"}}
