{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822508456,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_9a321a39e846473ba4bbb339","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","offset":401,"limit":500},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n401:  \n402: C \n403: DATA ANALYSIS  \n404:  \n405: DATA ANALYSIS  \n406: ●​\n407: It means the intelligence generated from \n408: reviewing audit-related information, often \n409: using technology. Like other types of data \n410: analytics, audit analytics typically involve \n411: analyzing large sets of numbers to find \n412: actionable audit insights.  \n413:  \n414: C1 \n415: THE FIVE STEPS TO GET YOU STARTED \n416: WITH DATA ANALYSIS \n417:  \n418: STEP 1: DEFINE QUESTIONS AND GOALS \n419: ●​\n420: The first step in data analysis is to clearly \n421: define \n422: your \n423: questions \n424: and \n425: goals. \n426: Like \n427: creating a hypothesis before an experiment, \n428: you should be asking a targeted question \n429: before searching the data for an answer. For \n430: example, \n431: questions \n432: about \n433: overall \n434: performance can be open-ended and it can \n435: be hard to pinpoint which metrics are needed \n436: for analysis. Instead, it is more advantageous \n437: to ask questions such as \"How have certain \n438: metrics changed over time?\" and \"Do these \n439: metrics correlate with others, and if so, \n440: how strongly?\" These types of questions \n441: have \n442: a specific focus which will help \n443: determine the type of analysis needed and \n444: what data is the most relevant to include.  \n445:  \n446: STEP 2: COLLECT DATA \n447: ●​\n448: Before you can start analyzing, there needs to \n449: be data available for use. Data can include \n450: sales records, customer demographics, lead \n451: tracking, net promoter scores, and more. \n452: When using a business intelligence tool, it is \n453: important to make sure that all of the data is \n454: accessible and the proper connections are \n455: set between your data warehouse and your \n456: BI tool of choice. Ultimately the volume of \n457: data required will depend on the question \n458: you wish to answer. Though you may only \n459: need a subset of the data collected, not \n460: having enough data can skew the results of \n461: your analysis. \n462:  \n463: STEP 3: DATA WRANGLING \n464: ●​\n465: Now that you have all of your data in one \n466: place, it is important to clean the data before \n467: beginning the analysis portion of this \n468: process. A large part of the cleansing process \n469: includes making sure that the data is in a \n470: usable format. This entails searching for \n471: outliers, dealing with null values, and looking \n472: for data that may have been incorrectly \n473: input. Often this can be a lengthy and \n474: arduous process.  \n475:  \n476: STEP 4: DETERMINE ANALYSIS \n477: ●​\n478: Once the relevant data is available and \n479: cleansed, it is time to analyze. Choosing a \n480: method of analysis will heavily depend on \n481: the question or goals defined earlier and the \n482: type of analysis needed. \n483: ●​\n484: Diagnostic analysis uses data to search for \n485: the cause of, and a solution to, an existing \n486: problem. \n487: ●​\n488: Descriptive analysis is a way to describe the \n489: data by summarizing key sections. \n490: ●​\n491: Predictive analysis combines historical data \n492: and statistical modeling to forecast how \n493: certain metrics will perform in the future. \n494:  \n495: STEP 5: INTERPRET RESULTS \n496: ●​\n497: After analyzing your data, it is important to \n498: interpret the results. Simply put, what are \n499: you learning from the results of your \n500: analysis? Combining multiple visualizations \n501: in one report enables the users to tell a story \n502: about their data that leaders can use to take \n503: actions, make changes, or refocus efforts \n504: accordingly. This step is key to gaining \n505: insights from the data and being able to \n506: apply these insights to your business.  \n507: ●​\n508: With these steps, you will be able to use data \n509: analysis to unlock powerful new insights \n510: that can be turned into actionable results. \n511: These insights could lead to more questions \n512: that prompt you to dig even deeper into the \n513: data and start the analysis cycle again.  \n514:  \n515: D \n516: GENERATION OF AUDIT REPORTS  \n517:  \n518: GENERATION OF AUDIT REPORTS  \n519: ●​\n520: The auditor gathers evidence and observes, \n521: tests, compares and confirms until gaining \n522: reasonable assurance and forms an opinion \n523: of whether the financial statements are free \n524: of fraud or error. In sum, this is the purpose \n525:  \n526: PAGE 3 \n527: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n528:  \n529: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n530: ITAS311-T \n531: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n532: \f \n533:  \n534: of an audit report. The auditor then presents \n535: an audit opinion.  \n536:  \n537: PURPOSE \n538: ●​\n539: Formal Communication  \n540: ●​\n541: Documentation of Evidence  \n542: ●​\n543: Initiation of Corrective Action Closure  \n544:  \n545: BENEFITS \n546: ●​\n547: Actionable Roadmap  \n548: ●​\n549: Strategic Oversight  \n550: ●​\n551: Historical Benchmarking  \n552: ●​\n553: Accountability  \n554:  \n555: D1 \n556: AUDIT REPORT FORMAT  \n557:  \n558: 1. TITLE \n559: ●​\n560: The title should mention - 'Independent \n561: Auditor's Report.  \n562:  \n563: 2. ADDRESSEE \n564: ●​\n565: The addressee should mention to whom the \n566: report is being presented.  \n567:  \n568: 3. INTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH  \n569: ●​\n570: A statement that the financial statements \n571: described in the report have been audited.  \n572:  \n573: 4. MANAGEMENT'S RESPONSIBILITY   \n574: ●​\n575: This section of the audit reports format \n576: should \n577: mention \n578: the \n579: Management's \n580: Responsibility for the integrity of the financial \n581: statements, which gives an overview of the \n582: company's financial condition, cash flows, \n583: and \n584: financial \n585: performance. \n586: It \n587: should \n588: mention that the financial statements are \n589: the \n590: responsibility \n591: of \n592: the \n593: organization's \n594: management. It is their responsibility to \n595: formulate and execute necessary financial \n596: controls to ensure the accuracy of the \n597: financial \n598: records. \n599: The \n600: responsibility \n601: also \n602: includes the maintenance of accounting \n603: records to prevent fraud.  \n604:  \n605: 5. AUDITOR'S RESPONSIBILITY   \n606: ●​\n607: The auditor's responsibility is to plan and \n608: execute the audit to procure assurance \n609: regarding \n610: the \n611: financial \n612: statements. \n613: The \n614: Auditor's \n615: responsibility \n616: is \n617: to \n618: depict \n619: an \n620: unbiased \n621: opinion \n622: on \n623: the \n624: financial \n625: statements and issue an audit report. The \n626: report is based on Standards on Auditing. \n627: The Standards require that the auditor \n628: complies with ethical requirements.  \n629:  \n630:  \n631: 6. OPINION   \n632: ●​\n633: The most critical content in an Audit Report \n634: is the Auditor's Opinion. It mentions the \n635: impression \n636: derived \n637: after \n638: auditing \n639: the \n640: financial statements.  \n641:  \n642: 7. BASIS OF THE OPINION   \n643: ●​\n644: It should mention the basis of achieving the \n645: opinion as reported and the facts of the \n646: premise.  \n647:  \n648: 8. OTHER REPORTING RESPONSIBILITY  \n649: ●​\n650: Any other responsibility relating to reporting \n651: exists. \n652: It \n653: may \n654: include \n655: Regulatory \n656: requirements. The auditor has to mention \n657: the same.  \n658:  \n659: 9. SIGNATURE OF THE AUDITOR  \n660: ●​\n661: The auditor needs to sign the audit report, \n662: thereby confirming the report's authenticity  \n663:  \n664: 10. PLACE OF SIGNATURE  \n665: ●​\n666: The name of the city in which the signing of \n667: the report happened.  \n668:  \n669: 11. DATE OF AUDIT REPORT  \n670: ●​\n671: The date on which the audit report is \n672: signed/reported \n673:  \n674: E \n675: PRESENTATION FINDINGS \n676:  \n677: PRESENTATION FINDINGS \n678: ●​\n679: Presentation of findings is the stage where \n680: the audit team formally communicates and \n681: discusses the results of the audit — findings, \n682: conclusions, and recommendations — with \n683: relevant stakeholders once the audit report is \n684: finalized or nearing finalization.  \n685:  \n686: KEY ELEMENTS COVERED IN THE PRESENTATION  \n687:  \n688:  \n689: E1 \n690: PURPOSE OF THE PRESENTATION OF \n691: FINDINGS  \n692:  \n693: PURPOSE OF THE PRESENTATION OF FINDINGS  \n694: ●​\n695: The presentation gives the audit team and \n696: stakeholders \n697: a \n698: shared \n699: moment \n700: to \n701: understand results, decide on action, and \n702: move forward with clear accountability.  \n703:  \n704:  \n705: PAGE 4 \n706: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n707:  \n708: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n709: ITAS311-T \n710: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n711: \f \n712:  \n713: ●​\n714: Communicate Results \n715: ○​\n716: Ensure \n717: stakeholders \n718: clearly \n719: understand \n720: what \n721: the \n722: audit \n723: uncovered. \n724: ●​\n725: Ensure Accountability \n726: ○​\n727: Assign \n728: ownership \n729: of \n730: recommendations \n731: and \n732: agree \n733: on \n734: timelines. \n735: ●​\n736: Support Decision-Making \n737: ○​\n738: Give management the information \n739: needed to prioritize and act. \n740: ●​\n741: Promote Transparency \n742: ○​\n743: Build trust by openly discussing both \n744: strengths and weaknesses. \n745:  \n746: E2 \n747: HOW FINDINGS ARE PRESENTED  \n748:  \n749: STEP 1: PREPARE A CLEAR SUMMARY \n750: ●​\n751: Organize findings by priority or risk level, \n752: not by discovery order. \n753:  \n754: STEP 2: USE VISUAL AIDS \n755: ●​\n756: Charts and risk matrices help non-technical \n757: stakeholders follow quickly. \n758:  \n759: STEP 3: STATE FINDING, IMPACT, FIX \n760: ●​\n761: For each issue: what was found, why it \n762: matters, what to do. \n763:  \n764: STEP 4: OPEN THE FLOOR \n765: ●​\n766: Let management respond and clarify before \n767: conclusions are locked in. \n768:  \n769: STEP 5: DOCUMENT AGREED ACTIONS \n770: ●​\n771: Record decisions, owners, and deadlines \n772: during the meeting. \n773:  \n774: E3 \n775: WHAT TO PREPARE BEFORE THE \n776: PRESENTATION \n777:  \n778: FINALIZE THE REPORT DRAFT \n779: ●​\n780: Ensure \n781: findings, \n782: evidence, \n783: and \n784: recommendations \n785: are \n786: accurate \n787: and \n788: complete. \n789:  \n790: ANTICIPATE QUESTIONS \n791: ●​\n792: Prepare supporting evidence for findings \n793: likely to be challenged. \n794:  \n795: PRIORITIZE FINDINGS \n796: ●​\n797: Rank issues by severity or risk so critical \n798: items are discussed first. \n799:  \n800:  \n801:  \n802: SET AN AGENDA \n803: ●​\n804: Allocate time per topic so discussion doesn't \n805: run over or skip items. \n806:  \n807: E4 \n808: ADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS \n809:  \n810: ADVANTAGES \n811: ●​\n812: ENSURES UNDERSTANDING \n813: ○​\n814: Reduces the chance findings are \n815: misread before action is taken. \n816: ●​\n817: DRIVES ACCOUNTABILITY \n818: ○​\n819: Assigns \n820: clear \n821: ownership \n822: and \n823: deadlines for fixes. \n824: ●​\n825: BUILDS BUY-IN \n826: ○​\n827: Stakeholders act faster on findings \n828: they helped discuss. \n829: ●​\n830: SURFACES CONTEXT \n831: ○​\n832: Management may explain factors the \n833: audit team missed. \n834:  \n835: LIMITATIONS \n836: ●​\n837: TIME-CONSTRAINED \n838: ○​\n839: Complex findings may need to be \n840: condensed, losing nuance. \n841: ●​\n842: STAKEHOLDER RESISTANCE \n843: ○​\n844: Management \n845: may \n846: dispute \n847: or \n848: downplay findings. \n849: ●​\n850: TECHNICAL GAPS \n851: ○​\n852: Non-technical \n853: audiences \n854: may \n855: struggle with audit language. \n856: ●​\n857: DELAYED FOLLOW-THROUGH \n858: ○​\n859: Agreement \n860: in \n861: the \n862: room doesn't \n863: guarantee timely action. \n864:  \n865:  \n866: L4: LOG INFORMATION AND \n867: SECURITY CONTROLS \n868:  \n869: TOPIC OVERVIEW \n870: A.​ INTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n871: B.​ TYPES OF LOG INFORMATION TO \n872: CAPTURE  \n873: C.​ INTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM (IDS)  \n874: D.​ VERIFYING SECURITY CONTROLS  \n875: E.​ PROTECTING LOGS  \n876: a.​\n877: Log Review And Incident \n878: Response  \n879: F.​\n880: INTRUSION DETECTION ANALYSIS \n881: METHODS \n882: G.​ THE CORE DETECTION METHODS  \n883: H.​ SUPPLEMENTARY PROFILING \n884: TECHNIQUES  \n885: I.​\n886: HOST BASED INTRUSION DETECTION \n887:  \n888: PAGE 5 \n889: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n890:  \n891: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n892: ITAS311-T \n893: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n894: \f \n895:  \n896: SYSTEM  \n897: J.​\n898: LAYERED DEFENSE  \n899: K.​ NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n900: L.​\n\n(Showing lines 401-900 of 5328. Use offset=901 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":" \nC \nDATA ANALYSIS  \n \nDATA ANALYSIS  \n●​\nIt means the intelligence generated from \nreviewing audit-related information, often \nusing technology. Like other types of data \nanalytics, audit analytics typically involve \nanalyzing large sets of numbers to find \nactionable audit insights.  \n \nC1 \nTHE FIVE STEPS TO GET YOU STARTED \nWITH DATA ANALYSIS \n \nSTEP 1: DEFINE QUESTIONS AND GOALS \n●​\nThe first step in data analysis is to clearly ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":" \nC \nDATA ANALYSIS  \n \nDATA ANALYSIS  \n●​\nIt means the intelligence generated from \nreviewing audit-related information, often \nusing technology. Like other types of data \nanalytics, audit analytics typically involve \nanalyzing large sets of numbers to find \nactionable audit insights.  \n \nC1 \nTHE FIVE STEPS TO GET YOU STARTED \nWITH DATA ANALYSIS \n \nSTEP 1: DEFINE QUESTIONS AND GOALS \n●​\nThe first step in data analysis is to clearly \ndefine \nyour \nquestions \nand \ngoals. \nLike \ncreating a hypothesis before an experiment, \nyou should be asking a targeted question \nbefore searching the data for an answer. For \nexample, \nquestions \nabout \noverall \nperformance can be open-ended and it can \nbe hard to pinpoint which metrics are needed \nfor analysis. Instead, it is more advantageous \nto ask questions such as \"How have certain \nmetrics changed over time?\" and \"Do these \nmetrics correlate with others, and if so, \nhow strongly?\" These types of questions \nhave \na specific focus which will help \ndetermine the type of analysis needed and \nwhat data is the most relevant to include.  \n \nSTEP 2: COLLECT DATA \n●​\nBefore you can start analyzing, there needs to \nbe data available for use. Data can include \nsales records, customer demographics, lead \ntracking, net promoter scores, and more. \nWhen using a business intelligence tool, it is \nimportant to make sure that all of the data is \naccessible and the proper connections are \nset between your data warehouse and your \nBI tool of choice. Ultimately the volume of \ndata required will depend on the question \nyou wish to answer. Though you may only \nneed a subset of the data collected, not \nhaving enough data can skew the results of \nyour analysis. \n \nSTEP 3: DATA WRANGLING \n●​\nNow that you have all of your data in one \nplace, it is important to clean the data before \nbeginning the analysis portion of this \nprocess. A large part of the cleansing process \nincludes making sure that the data is in a \nusable format. This entails searching for \noutliers, dealing with null values, and looking \nfor data that may have been incorrectly \ninput. Often this can be a lengthy and \narduous process.  \n \nSTEP 4: DETERMINE ANALYSIS \n●​\nOnce the relevant data is available and \ncleansed, it is time to analyze. Choosing a \nmethod of analysis will heavily depend on \nthe question or goals defined earlier and the \ntype of analysis needed. \n●​\nDiagnostic analysis uses data to search for \nthe cause of, and a solution to, an existing \nproblem. \n●​\nDescriptive analysis is a way to describe the \ndata by summarizing key sections. \n●​\nPredictive analysis combines historical data \nand statistical modeling to forecast how \ncertain metrics will perform in the future. \n \nSTEP 5: INTERPRET RESULTS \n●​\nAfter analyzing your data, it is important to \ninterpret the results. Simply put, what are \nyou learning from the results of your \nanalysis? Combining multiple visualizations \nin one report enables the users to tell a story \nabout their data that leaders can use to take \nactions, make changes, or refocus efforts \naccordingly. This step is key to gaining \ninsights from the data and being able to \napply these insights to your business.  \n●​\nWith these steps, you will be able to use data \nanalysis to unlock powerful new insights \nthat can be turned into actionable results. \nThese insights could lead to more questions \nthat prompt you to dig even deeper into the \ndata and start the analysis cycle again.  \n \nD \nGENERATION OF AUDIT REPORTS  \n \nGENERATION OF AUDIT REPORTS  \n●​\nThe auditor gathers evidence and observes, \ntests, compares and confirms until gaining \nreasonable assurance and forms an opinion \nof whether the financial statements are free \nof fraud or error. In sum, this is the purpose \n \nPAGE 3 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nof an audit report. The auditor then presents \nan audit opinion.  \n \nPURPOSE \n●​\nFormal Communication  \n●​\nDocumentation of Evidence  \n●​\nInitiation of Corrective Action Closure  \n \nBENEFITS \n●​\nActionable Roadmap  \n●​\nStrategic Oversight  \n●​\nHistorical Benchmarking  \n●​\nAccountability  \n \nD1 \nAUDIT REPORT FORMAT  \n \n1. TITLE \n●​\nThe title should mention - 'Independent \nAuditor's Report.  \n \n2. ADDRESSEE \n●​\nThe addressee should mention to whom the \nreport is being presented.  \n \n3. INTRODUCTORY PARAGRAPH  \n●​\nA statement that the financial statements \ndescribed in the report have been audited.  \n \n4. MANAGEMENT'S RESPONSIBILITY   \n●​\nThis section of the audit reports format \nshould \nmention \nthe \nManagement's \nResponsibility for the integrity of the financial \nstatements, which gives an overview of the \ncompany's financial condition, cash flows, \nand \nfinancial \nperformance. \nIt \nshould \nmention that the financial statements are \nthe \nresponsibility \nof \nthe \norganization's \nmanagement. It is their responsibility to \nformulate and execute necessary financial \ncontrols to ensure the accuracy of the \nfinancial \nrecords. \nThe \nresponsibility \nalso \nincludes the maintenance of accounting \nrecords to prevent fraud.  \n \n5. AUDITOR'S RESPONSIBILITY   \n●​\nThe auditor's responsibility is to plan and \nexecute the audit to procure assurance \nregarding \nthe \nfinancial \nstatements. \nThe \nAuditor's \nresponsibility \nis \nto \ndepict \nan \nunbiased \nopinion \non \nthe \nfinancial \nstatements and issue an audit report. The \nreport is based on Standards on Auditing. \nThe Standards require that the auditor \ncomplies with ethical requirements.  \n \n \n6. OPINION   \n●​\nThe most critical content in an Audit Report \nis the Auditor's Opinion. It mentions the \nimpression \nderived \nafter \nauditing \nthe \nfinancial statements.  \n \n7. BASIS OF THE OPINION   \n●​\nIt should mention the basis of achieving the \nopinion as reported and the facts of the \npremise.  \n \n8. OTHER REPORTING RESPONSIBILITY  \n●​\nAny other responsibility relating to reporting \nexists. \nIt \nmay \ninclude \nRegulatory \nrequirements. The auditor has to mention \nthe same.  \n \n9. SIGNATURE OF THE AUDITOR  \n●​\nThe auditor needs to sign the audit report, \nthereby confirming the report's authenticity  \n \n10. PLACE OF SIGNATURE  \n●​\nThe name of the city in which the signing of \nthe report happened.  \n \n11. DATE OF AUDIT REPORT  \n●​\nThe date on which the audit report is \nsigned/reported \n \nE \nPRESENTATION FINDINGS \n \nPRESENTATION FINDINGS \n●​\nPresentation of findings is the stage where \nthe audit team formally communicates and \ndiscusses the results of the audit — findings, \nconclusions, and recommendations — with \nrelevant stakeholders once the audit report is \nfinalized or nearing finalization.  \n \nKEY ELEMENTS COVERED IN THE PRESENTATION  \n \n \nE1 \nPURPOSE OF THE PRESENTATION OF \nFINDINGS  \n \nPURPOSE OF THE PRESENTATION OF FINDINGS  \n●​\nThe presentation gives the audit team and \nstakeholders \na \nshared \nmoment \nto \nunderstand results, decide on action, and \nmove forward with clear accountability.  \n \n \nPAGE 4 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nCommunicate Results \n○​\nEnsure \nstakeholders \nclearly \nunderstand \nwhat \nthe \naudit \nuncovered. \n●​\nEnsure Accountability \n○​\nAssign \nownership \nof \nrecommendations \nand \nagree \non \ntimelines. \n●​\nSupport Decision-Making \n○​\nGive management the information \nneeded to prioritize and act. \n●​\nPromote Transparency \n○​\nBuild trust by openly discussing both \nstrengths and weaknesses. \n \nE2 \nHOW FINDINGS ARE PRESENTED  \n \nSTEP 1: PREPARE A CLEAR SUMMARY \n●​\nOrganize findings by priority or risk level, \nnot by discovery order. \n \nSTEP 2: USE VISUAL AIDS \n●​\nCharts and risk matrices help non-technical \nstakeholders follow quickly. \n \nSTEP 3: STATE FINDING, IMPACT, FIX \n●​\nFor each issue: what was found, why it \nmatters, what to do. \n \nSTEP 4: OPEN THE FLOOR \n●​\nLet management respond and clarify before \nconclusions are locked in. \n \nSTEP 5: DOCUMENT AGREED ACTIONS \n●​\nRecord decisions, owners, and deadlines \nduring the meeting. \n \nE3 \nWHAT TO PREPARE BEFORE THE \nPRESENTATION \n \nFINALIZE THE REPORT DRAFT \n●​\nEnsure \nfindings, \nevidence, \nand \nrecommendations \nare \naccurate \nand \ncomplete. \n \nANTICIPATE QUESTIONS \n●​\nPrepare supporting evidence for findings \nlikely to be challenged. \n \nPRIORITIZE FINDINGS \n●​\nRank issues by severity or risk so critical \nitems are discussed first. \n \n \n \nSET AN AGENDA \n●​\nAllocate time per topic so discussion doesn't \nrun over or skip items. \n \nE4 \nADVANTAGES AND LIMITATIONS \n \nADVANTAGES \n●​\nENSURES UNDERSTANDING \n○​\nReduces the chance findings are \nmisread before action is taken. \n●​\nDRIVES ACCOUNTABILITY \n○​\nAssigns \nclear \nownership \nand \ndeadlines for fixes. \n●​\nBUILDS BUY-IN \n○​\nStakeholders act faster on findings \nthey helped discuss. \n●​\nSURFACES CONTEXT \n○​\nManagement may explain factors the \naudit team missed. \n \nLIMITATIONS \n●​\nTIME-CONSTRAINED \n○​\nComplex findings may need to be \ncondensed, losing nuance. \n●​\nSTAKEHOLDER RESISTANCE \n○​\nManagement \nmay \ndispute \nor \ndownplay findings. \n●​\nTECHNICAL GAPS \n○​\nNon-technical \naudiences \nmay \nstruggle with audit language. \n●​\nDELAYED FOLLOW-THROUGH \n○​\nAgreement \nin \nthe \nroom doesn't \nguarantee timely action. \n \n \nL4: LOG INFORMATION AND \nSECURITY CONTROLS \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ INTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \nB.​ TYPES OF LOG INFORMATION TO \nCAPTURE  \nC.​ INTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM (IDS)  \nD.​ VERIFYING SECURITY CONTROLS  \nE.​ PROTECTING LOGS  \na.​\nLog Review And Incident \nResponse  \nF.​\nINTRUSION DETECTION ANALYSIS \nMETHODS \nG.​ THE CORE DETECTION METHODS  \nH.​ SUPPLEMENTARY PROFILING \nTECHNIQUES  \nI.​\nHOST BASED INTRUSION DETECTION \n \nPAGE 5 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nSYSTEM  \nJ.​\nLAYERED DEFENSE  \nK.​ NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \nL.​","lineStart":401,"lineEnd":900,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822508364,"end":1789822508427}},"id":"prt_0b9bc1b6b001SKsOFbcvsk4vCj","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc10520015DPZCOwcUScYEc"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1789822508464,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"id":"prt_0b9bc1d8f001aT98tvURyI2P1Q","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_0b9bc10520015DPZCOwcUScYEc","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":22055,"input":2599,"output":256,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":19200}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789822509484,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"id":"prt_0b9bc21a8001Zr3W289S1HO6gq","messageID":"msg_0b9bc1da2001Zz80eGbwtkT6Hp","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1789822511661,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"id":"prt_0b9bc2712001Hgt0Yoo3ZACcmu","messageID":"msg_0b9bc1da2001Zz80eGbwtkT6Hp","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1789822510866,"end":1789822511657}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822511757,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1f99a2d6f3f24395a9f4837a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":600,"offset":901},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n901: HOST ISOLATION  \n902: a.​\n903: When And Why Host Isolation Is \n904: Used  \n905: b.​\n906: Main Objectives Of Isolation  \n907: c.​\n908: Verifying Host Isolation  \n909: M.​ SYSTEM HARDENING  \n910: N.​ MONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY \n911: SYSTEM  \n912:  \n913: A \n914: INTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n915:  \n916: INTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n917: ●​\n918: Security logs record activities occurring on \n919: computers, \n920: networks, \n921: applications, \n922: and \n923: security devices. \n924: ●​\n925: Logs help organizations:  \n926: ○​\n927: Detect unauthorized access.  \n928: ○​\n929: Investigate security incidents.  \n930: ○​\n931: Verify security controls.  \n932: ○​\n933: Support audits and compliance.  \n934: ●​\n935: Important log questions include:  \n936: ○​\n937: Who performed the action?  \n938: ○​\n939: What happened?  \n940: ○​\n941: When did it happen?  \n942: ○​\n943: Where did it originate?  \n944:  \n945: B \n946: TYPES OF LOG INFORMATION TO \n947: CAPTURE \n948:  \n949: TYPES OF LOG INFORMATION TO CAPTURE \n950: 1.​\n951: Authentication logs  \n952: ●​\n953: Successful and failed logins.  \n954: ●​\n955: Password changes.  \n956: ●​\n957: Multi-factor authentication events.  \n958: ●​\n959: Account lockouts.  \n960: 2.​\n961: Authorization logs  \n962: ●​\n963: Access to restricted files.  \n964: ●​\n965: Privilege changes.  \n966: ●​\n967: Administrative actions.  \n968: 3.​\n969: Network logs  \n970: ●​\n971: Firewall connections.  \n972: ●​\n973: Blocked traffic.  \n974: ●​\n975: VPN activity.  \n976: ●​\n977: Network scans.  \n978: 4.​ System and application logs  \n979: ●​\n980: Operating-system errors.  \n981: ●​\n982: Software installations.  \n983: ●​\n984: Configuration changes.  \n985: ●​\n986: Application \n987: failures \n988: and \n989: user \n990: transactions. \n991:  \n992: IMPORTANT LOG DETAILS  \n993: ●​\n994: Each security event should contain enough \n995: information for investigation.  \n996:  \n997: ●​\n998: National \n999: Institute \n1000: of \n1001: Standards \n1002: and \n1003: Technology (NIST) identifies the timestamp, \n1004: event type, event source, and sensor or \n1005: reporting system as important information \n1006: for IDPS events.  \n1007:  \n1008: C \n1009: INTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM (IDS)  \n1010:  \n1011: INTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM (IDS)  \n1012: ●​\n1013: An Intrusion Detection System (IDS) monitors \n1014: activity and detects possible attacks or \n1015: policy violations. An IDS generally generates \n1016: alerts \n1017: rather \n1018: than \n1019: blocking \n1020: activity \n1021: automatically.  \n1022: ●​\n1023: IDS can monitor:  \n1024: ○​\n1025: Network traffic.  \n1026: ○​\n1027: System activity.  \n1028: ○​\n1029: User behavior.  \n1030: ○​\n1031: Application events.  \n1032: ○​\n1033: Authentication attempts.  \n1034: ●​\n1035: Main IDS types:  \n1036: ○​\n1037: Network-based IDS (NIDS): Monitors \n1038: network traffic.  \n1039: ○​\n1040: Host-based \n1041: IDS \n1042: (HIDS): \n1043: Monitors \n1044: individual computers or servers.  \n1045: ●​\n1046: IDS technologies help organizations detect \n1047: and investigate suspicious activity.  \n1048:  \n1049: IDS LOG INFORMATION  \n1050: ●​\n1051: Alert identification number. \n1052: ●​\n1053: Date and time of detection. \n1054: ●​\n1055: Source and destination IP addresses. \n1056: ●​\n1057: Ports and network protocol. \n1058: ●​\n1059: Detection rule or signature. \n1060: ●​\n1061: Suspected attack type. \n1062: ●​\n1063: Severity level. \n1064: ●​\n1065: Affected host, account, or application. \n1066: ●​\n1067: IDS sensor that generated the alert. \n1068: ●​\n1069: Response or action taken. \n1070:  \n1071:  \n1072:  \n1073:  \n1074: PAGE 6 \n1075: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n1076:  \n1077: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n1078: ITAS311-T \n1079: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n1080: \f \n1081:  \n1082: DETECTION METHODS \n1083: ●​\n1084: Signature-based: \n1085: Detects \n1086: known \n1087: attack \n1088: patterns.  \n1089: ●​\n1090: Anomaly-based: Identifies activity that differs \n1091: from normal behavior.  \n1092: ●​\n1093: Protocol analysis: Detects improper use of \n1094: network protocols.  \n1095: ●​\n1096: Event correlation: Combines multiple events \n1097: to identify a larger attack.  \n1098:  \n1099: D \n1100: VERIFYING SECURITY CONTROLS  \n1101:  \n1102: Security controls should be tested to confirm that \n1103: they work as intended.  \n1104: ●​\n1105: Review system and IDS configurations. \n1106: ●​\n1107: Confirm \n1108: that \n1109: logging \n1110: is \n1111: enabled \n1112: on \n1113: important devices. \n1114: ●​\n1115: Perform an authorized failed-login or test \n1116: intrusion. \n1117: ●​\n1118: Verify that the activity creates a log entry. \n1119: ●​\n1120: Confirm that the IDS generates the correct \n1121: alert. \n1122: ●​\n1123: Check whether alerts reach the security \n1124: team or SIEM. \n1125: ●​\n1126: Validate timestamps and required log fields. \n1127: ●​\n1128: Review false positives and missed events. \n1129: ●​\n1130: Document test results and corrective actions \n1131:  \n1132: Testing should be authorized and performed in a \n1133: controlled environment.  \n1134:  \n1135: E \n1136: PROTECTING LOGS  \n1137:  \n1138: PROTECTING LOGS  \n1139: ●​\n1140: Store logs in a centralized log-management \n1141: platform or SIEM. \n1142: ●​\n1143: Restrict log access to authorized personnel. \n1144: ●​\n1145: Encrypt \n1146: logs \n1147: during \n1148: transmission \n1149: and \n1150: storage. \n1151: ●​\n1152: Separate log storage from the systems \n1153: generating the logs. \n1154: ●​\n1155: Monitor attempts to modify or delete logs. \n1156: ●​\n1157: Use \n1158: hashes, \n1159: digital \n1160: signatures, \n1161: or \n1162: append-only storage to protect integrity. \n1163: ●​\n1164: Establish retention and disposal policies. \n1165: ●​\n1166: Monitor whether log sources stop sending \n1167: data. \n1168: ●​\n1169: Protect logs because they may contain \n1170: sensitive \n1171: user, \n1172: system, \n1173: and \n1174: security \n1175: information. \n1176: Log \n1177: management \n1178: includes \n1179: generating, \n1180: transmitting, storing, accessing, and disposing of \n1181: log data.  \n1182:  \n1183: E1 \n1184: LOG REVIEW AND INCIDENT RESPONSE  \n1185:  \n1186: LOG REVIEW AND INCIDENT RESPONSE  \n1187: ●​\n1188: Logs should be reviewed continuously or \n1189: according to a documented schedule.  \n1190:  \n1191: SECURITY TEAMS SHOULD INVESTIGATE: \n1192: ●​\n1193: Repeated failed logins. \n1194: ●​\n1195: Privilege escalation. \n1196: ●​\n1197: Disabled security services. \n1198: ●​\n1199: Malware detection. \n1200: ●​\n1201: Unexpected administrator activity. \n1202: ●​\n1203: Large or unusual data transfers. \n1204: IDS alerts should be correlated with firewall, \n1205: authentication, endpoint, and application logs.  \n1206:  \n1207: WHEN SUSPICIOUS ACTIVITY IS IDENTIFIED: \n1208: 1.​\n1209: Validate the alert. \n1210: 2.​\n1211: Identify affected systems. \n1212: 3.​\n1213: Preserve relevant evidence. \n1214: 4.​\n1215: Contain the activity. \n1216: 5.​\n1217: Investigate and document the incident. \n1218: 6.​\n1219: Improve security controls based on the \n1220: findings. \n1221: Regular log review helps organizations detect \n1222: threats earlier and improve their security posture.  \n1223:  \n1224: F \n1225: INTRUSION DETECTION ANALYSIS \n1226: METHODS \n1227:  \n1228: INTRUSION DETECTION ANALYSIS METHODS \n1229: ●​\n1230: are the core methodologies that Intrusion \n1231: Detection Systems (IDS) and security analysts \n1232: use to inspect network traffic, system \n1233: events, \n1234: and \n1235: log \n1236: files \n1237: to \n1238: discover \n1239: unauthorized or malicious activities \n1240:  \n1241: G \n1242: THE CORE DETECTION METHODS  \n1243:  \n1244: THE CORE DETECTION METHODS  \n1245: ●​\n1246: Signature-Based Detection  \n1247: ○​\n1248: Matches data against a database of \n1249: known threat indicators.  \n1250: ●​\n1251: Anomaly-Based Detection  \n1252: ○​\n1253: Flags any activities that deviate from \n1254: an established baseline of normal \n1255: behavior.  \n1256: ●​\n1257: Stateful Protocol Analysis  \n1258: ○​\n1259: Evaluates traffic based on standard \n1260: protocol \n1261: specifications \n1262: to \n1263: find \n1264: structural deviations.  \n1265:  \n1266:  \n1267:  \n1268: PAGE 7 \n1269: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n1270:  \n1271: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n1272: ITAS311-T \n1273: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n1274: \f \n1275:  \n1276: 1. SIGNATURE-BASED ANALYSIS  \n1277: ●​\n1278: Also \n1279: known \n1280: as \n1281: Misuse \n1282: Detection, \n1283: this \n1284: method scans network packets and system \n1285: logs for specific patterns, byte sequences, or \n1286: malicious \n1287: instruction \n1288: code—known \n1289: as \n1290: \"signatures\"—that match recorded threats.  \n1291: ●​\n1292: Pros: Highly accurate for known threats; \n1293: generates very few false positives.  \n1294: ●​\n1295: Cons: \n1296: Entirely \n1297: blind \n1298: to \n1299: new, \n1300: zero-day \n1301: vulnerabilities and morphing malware.  \n1302:  \n1303: 2. ANOMALY-BASED ANALYSIS  \n1304: ●​\n1305: This method relies on machine learning or \n1306: statistical modeling to build a rigid baseline \n1307: profile \n1308: of \n1309: standard \n1310: system \n1311: or \n1312: network \n1313: operations. Once established, the system logs \n1314: any \n1315: deviation from this baseline as a \n1316: potential attack.  \n1317: ●​\n1318: Pros: Capable of detecting brandnew, novel, \n1319: or zeroday threats.  \n1320: ●​\n1321: Cons: High rate of false positives caused by \n1322: legitimate, unusual user spikes or system \n1323: changes.  \n1324:  \n1325: 3. STATEFUL PROTOCOL ANALYSIS  \n1326: ●​\n1327: Also known as Specification-Based analysis, \n1328: this method compares network activity \n1329: against universal vendor profiles of how \n1330: protocols should structurally behave. It tracks \n1331: the state of network pairings over time. This \n1332: is different from anomaly detection which \n1333: learns \"normal\" behavior from history. \n1334: ●​\n1335: Pros: Easily catches protocol manipulation, \n1336: illegal commands, and scanning attempts.  \n1337: ●​\n1338: Cons: Consumes heavy system resource \n1339: overhead \n1340: to \n1341: continuously \n1342: track \n1343: state \n1344: conditions across layers.  \n1345:  \n1346: H \n1347: SUPPLEMENTARY PROFILING \n1348: TECHNIQUES  \n1349:  \n1350: Beyond the primary engines, advanced modern \n1351: frameworks implement specific profiling layers:  \n1352: ●​\n1353: Threshold Monitoring:  \n1354: ○​\n1355: Alerts \n1356: defenders \n1357: when \n1358: pre-set \n1359: operational limits are broken (e.g., \n1360: failed login attempts).  \n1361: ●​\n1362: Resource Profiling:  \n1363: ○​\n1364: Tracks system-wide consumption \n1365: metrics \n1366: like \n1367: CPU, \n1368: RAM, \n1369: and \n1370: bandwidth \n1371: to \n1372: find \n1373: sneaky \n1374: background malware.  \n1375: ●​\n1376: Executable Profiling:  \n1377: ○​\n1378: Continuously analyzes how active \n1379: software programs invoke system \n1380: calls and read system memory.  \n1381: I \n1382: HOST BASED INTRUSION DETECTION \n1383: SYSTEM  \n1384:  \n1385: HOST BASED INTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM  \n1386: ●​\n1387: Host-based \n1388: Intrusion \n1389: Detection \n1390: System \n1391: (HIDS) is a cybersecurity tool that monitors \n1392: an individual device or host for suspicious \n1393: activity, unauthorized file changes, and \n1394: malicious behavior.  \n1395:  \n1396:  \n1397: HOW DOES IT WORK?  \n1398: ●​\n1399: Data Collection:  \n1400: ○​\n1401: The HIDS agent gathers raw data \n1402: directly from the host, including \n1403: system logs, application event logs, \n1404: authentication records, and kernel \n1405: activities.  \n1406: ●​\n1407: File Integrity Monitoring (FIM):  \n1408: ○​\n1409: It \n1410: tracks \n1411: critical \n1412: system \n1413: files, \n1414: configuration \n1415: settings, \n1416: and \n1417: executables by comparing current \n1418: states against baseline snapshots to \n1419: catch unauthorized changes.  \n1420: ●​\n1421: Analysis and Matching:  \n1422: ○​\n1423: The system evaluates the collected \n1424: data using two main techniques: \n1425: Signature-based \n1426: and \n1427: Anomaly-based.  \n1428: ●​\n1429: Alerting and Logging:  \n1430: ○​\n1431: When a rule violation or threat is \n1432: found, the HIDS logs the event \n1433: details and immediately sends an \n1434: alert to security administrators for \n1435: response.  \n1436:  \n1437: COMPARISON TO NIDS  \n1438:  \n1439:  \n1440:  \n1441: PAGE 8 \n1442: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n1443:  \n1444: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n1445: ITAS311-T \n1446: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n1447: \f \n1448:  \n1449: J \n1450: LAYERED DEFENSE  \n1451:  \n1452: LAYERED DEFENSE  \n1453: ●​\n1454: Layered defense, also known as defense in \n1455: depth, is a security strategy that uses \n1456: multiple security controls at different levels \n1457: of an information system.  \n1458: ●​\n1459: Instead of depending on a single security \n1460: mechanism, \n1461: organizations \n1462: create \n1463: several \n1464: defensive layers.  \n1465: ●​\n1466: If one layer fails or is bypassed, another layer \n1467: can still detect, prevent, or limit the attack.  \n1468:  \n1469: Security Layer  \n1470: What it protects  \n1471: Examples  \n1472: 1. Physical Security  \n1473: Physical access to \n1474: equipment  \n1475: Locks, guards, access \n1476: cards, cameras  \n1477: 2. Network Security  \n1478: Network connections \n1479: and traffic  \n1480: Firewalls, \n1481: segmentation, NAC  \n1482: 3. Detection  \n1483: Identifies suspicious \n1484: activity  \n1485: IDS/IPS, traffic analysis  \n1486: 4. Endpoint/Host \n1487: Security  \n1488: Individual computers \n1489: and servers  \n1490: Antivirus/EDR, HIDS, \n1491: host firewall  \n1492: 5. Application Security  \n1493: Applications and their \n1494: functions  \n1495: Authentication, \n1496: authorization, input \n1497: validation  \n1498: 6. Data Security  \n1499: Information itself  \n1500: Encryption, backups, \n\n(Showing lines 901-1500 of 5328. Use offset=1501 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"HOST ISOLATION  \na.​\nWhen And Why Host Isolation Is \nUsed  \nb.​\nMain Objectives Of Isolation  \nc.​\nVerifying Host Isolation  \nM.​ SYSTEM HARDENING  \nN.​ MONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY \nSYSTEM  \n \nA \nINTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n \nINTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n●​\nSecurity logs record activities occurring on \ncomputers, \nnetworks, ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":"HOST ISOLATION  \na.​\nWhen And Why Host Isolation Is \nUsed  \nb.​\nMain Objectives Of Isolation  \nc.​\nVerifying Host Isolation  \nM.​ SYSTEM HARDENING  \nN.​ MONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY \nSYSTEM  \n \nA \nINTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n \nINTRODUCTION TO SECURITY LOGGING  \n●​\nSecurity logs record activities occurring on \ncomputers, \nnetworks, \napplications, \nand \nsecurity devices. \n●​\nLogs help organizations:  \n○​\nDetect unauthorized access.  \n○​\nInvestigate security incidents.  \n○​\nVerify security controls.  \n○​\nSupport audits and compliance.  \n●​\nImportant log questions include:  \n○​\nWho performed the action?  \n○​\nWhat happened?  \n○​\nWhen did it happen?  \n○​\nWhere did it originate?  \n \nB \nTYPES OF LOG INFORMATION TO \nCAPTURE \n \nTYPES OF LOG INFORMATION TO CAPTURE \n1.​\nAuthentication logs  \n●​\nSuccessful and failed logins.  \n●​\nPassword changes.  \n●​\nMulti-factor authentication events.  \n●​\nAccount lockouts.  \n2.​\nAuthorization logs  \n●​\nAccess to restricted files.  \n●​\nPrivilege changes.  \n●​\nAdministrative actions.  \n3.​\nNetwork logs  \n●​\nFirewall connections.  \n●​\nBlocked traffic.  \n●​\nVPN activity.  \n●​\nNetwork scans.  \n4.​ System and application logs  \n●​\nOperating-system errors.  \n●​\nSoftware installations.  \n●​\nConfiguration changes.  \n●​\nApplication \nfailures \nand \nuser \ntransactions. \n \nIMPORTANT LOG DETAILS  \n●​\nEach security event should contain enough \ninformation for investigation.  \n \n●​\nNational \nInstitute \nof \nStandards \nand \nTechnology (NIST) identifies the timestamp, \nevent type, event source, and sensor or \nreporting system as important information \nfor IDPS events.  \n \nC \nINTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM (IDS)  \n \nINTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM (IDS)  \n●​\nAn Intrusion Detection System (IDS) monitors \nactivity and detects possible attacks or \npolicy violations. An IDS generally generates \nalerts \nrather \nthan \nblocking \nactivity \nautomatically.  \n●​\nIDS can monitor:  \n○​\nNetwork traffic.  \n○​\nSystem activity.  \n○​\nUser behavior.  \n○​\nApplication events.  \n○​\nAuthentication attempts.  \n●​\nMain IDS types:  \n○​\nNetwork-based IDS (NIDS): Monitors \nnetwork traffic.  \n○​\nHost-based \nIDS \n(HIDS): \nMonitors \nindividual computers or servers.  \n●​\nIDS technologies help organizations detect \nand investigate suspicious activity.  \n \nIDS LOG INFORMATION  \n●​\nAlert identification number. \n●​\nDate and time of detection. \n●​\nSource and destination IP addresses. \n●​\nPorts and network protocol. \n●​\nDetection rule or signature. \n●​\nSuspected attack type. \n●​\nSeverity level. \n●​\nAffected host, account, or application. \n●​\nIDS sensor that generated the alert. \n●​\nResponse or action taken. \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 6 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nDETECTION METHODS \n●​\nSignature-based: \nDetects \nknown \nattack \npatterns.  \n●​\nAnomaly-based: Identifies activity that differs \nfrom normal behavior.  \n●​\nProtocol analysis: Detects improper use of \nnetwork protocols.  \n●​\nEvent correlation: Combines multiple events \nto identify a larger attack.  \n \nD \nVERIFYING SECURITY CONTROLS  \n \nSecurity controls should be tested to confirm that \nthey work as intended.  \n●​\nReview system and IDS configurations. \n●​\nConfirm \nthat \nlogging \nis \nenabled \non \nimportant devices. \n●​\nPerform an authorized failed-login or test \nintrusion. \n●​\nVerify that the activity creates a log entry. \n●​\nConfirm that the IDS generates the correct \nalert. \n●​\nCheck whether alerts reach the security \nteam or SIEM. \n●​\nValidate timestamps and required log fields. \n●​\nReview false positives and missed events. \n●​\nDocument test results and corrective actions \n \nTesting should be authorized and performed in a \ncontrolled environment.  \n \nE \nPROTECTING LOGS  \n \nPROTECTING LOGS  \n●​\nStore logs in a centralized log-management \nplatform or SIEM. \n●​\nRestrict log access to authorized personnel. \n●​\nEncrypt \nlogs \nduring \ntransmission \nand \nstorage. \n●​\nSeparate log storage from the systems \ngenerating the logs. \n●​\nMonitor attempts to modify or delete logs. \n●​\nUse \nhashes, \ndigital \nsignatures, \nor \nappend-only storage to protect integrity. \n●​\nEstablish retention and disposal policies. \n●​\nMonitor whether log sources stop sending \ndata. \n●​\nProtect logs because they may contain \nsensitive \nuser, \nsystem, \nand \nsecurity \ninformation. \nLog \nmanagement \nincludes \ngenerating, \ntransmitting, storing, accessing, and disposing of \nlog data.  \n \nE1 \nLOG REVIEW AND INCIDENT RESPONSE  \n \nLOG REVIEW AND INCIDENT RESPONSE  \n●​\nLogs should be reviewed continuously or \naccording to a documented schedule.  \n \nSECURITY TEAMS SHOULD INVESTIGATE: \n●​\nRepeated failed logins. \n●​\nPrivilege escalation. \n●​\nDisabled security services. \n●​\nMalware detection. \n●​\nUnexpected administrator activity. \n●​\nLarge or unusual data transfers. \nIDS alerts should be correlated with firewall, \nauthentication, endpoint, and application logs.  \n \nWHEN SUSPICIOUS ACTIVITY IS IDENTIFIED: \n1.​\nValidate the alert. \n2.​\nIdentify affected systems. \n3.​\nPreserve relevant evidence. \n4.​\nContain the activity. \n5.​\nInvestigate and document the incident. \n6.​\nImprove security controls based on the \nfindings. \nRegular log review helps organizations detect \nthreats earlier and improve their security posture.  \n \nF \nINTRUSION DETECTION ANALYSIS \nMETHODS \n \nINTRUSION DETECTION ANALYSIS METHODS \n●​\nare the core methodologies that Intrusion \nDetection Systems (IDS) and security analysts \nuse to inspect network traffic, system \nevents, \nand \nlog \nfiles \nto \ndiscover \nunauthorized or malicious activities \n \nG \nTHE CORE DETECTION METHODS  \n \nTHE CORE DETECTION METHODS  \n●​\nSignature-Based Detection  \n○​\nMatches data against a database of \nknown threat indicators.  \n●​\nAnomaly-Based Detection  \n○​\nFlags any activities that deviate from \nan established baseline of normal \nbehavior.  \n●​\nStateful Protocol Analysis  \n○​\nEvaluates traffic based on standard \nprotocol \nspecifications \nto \nfind \nstructural deviations.  \n \n \n \nPAGE 7 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n1. SIGNATURE-BASED ANALYSIS  \n●​\nAlso \nknown \nas \nMisuse \nDetection, \nthis \nmethod scans network packets and system \nlogs for specific patterns, byte sequences, or \nmalicious \ninstruction \ncode—known \nas \n\"signatures\"—that match recorded threats.  \n●​\nPros: Highly accurate for known threats; \ngenerates very few false positives.  \n●​\nCons: \nEntirely \nblind \nto \nnew, \nzero-day \nvulnerabilities and morphing malware.  \n \n2. ANOMALY-BASED ANALYSIS  \n●​\nThis method relies on machine learning or \nstatistical modeling to build a rigid baseline \nprofile \nof \nstandard \nsystem \nor \nnetwork \noperations. Once established, the system logs \nany \ndeviation from this baseline as a \npotential attack.  \n●​\nPros: Capable of detecting brandnew, novel, \nor zeroday threats.  \n●​\nCons: High rate of false positives caused by \nlegitimate, unusual user spikes or system \nchanges.  \n \n3. STATEFUL PROTOCOL ANALYSIS  \n●​\nAlso known as Specification-Based analysis, \nthis method compares network activity \nagainst universal vendor profiles of how \nprotocols should structurally behave. It tracks \nthe state of network pairings over time. This \nis different from anomaly detection which \nlearns \"normal\" behavior from history. \n●​\nPros: Easily catches protocol manipulation, \nillegal commands, and scanning attempts.  \n●​\nCons: Consumes heavy system resource \noverhead \nto \ncontinuously \ntrack \nstate \nconditions across layers.  \n \nH \nSUPPLEMENTARY PROFILING \nTECHNIQUES  \n \nBeyond the primary engines, advanced modern \nframeworks implement specific profiling layers:  \n●​\nThreshold Monitoring:  \n○​\nAlerts \ndefenders \nwhen \npre-set \noperational limits are broken (e.g., \nfailed login attempts).  \n●​\nResource Profiling:  \n○​\nTracks system-wide consumption \nmetrics \nlike \nCPU, \nRAM, \nand \nbandwidth \nto \nfind \nsneaky \nbackground malware.  \n●​\nExecutable Profiling:  \n○​\nContinuously analyzes how active \nsoftware programs invoke system \ncalls and read system memory.  \nI \nHOST BASED INTRUSION DETECTION \nSYSTEM  \n \nHOST BASED INTRUSION DETECTION SYSTEM  \n●​\nHost-based \nIntrusion \nDetection \nSystem \n(HIDS) is a cybersecurity tool that monitors \nan individual device or host for suspicious \nactivity, unauthorized file changes, and \nmalicious behavior.  \n \n \nHOW DOES IT WORK?  \n●​\nData Collection:  \n○​\nThe HIDS agent gathers raw data \ndirectly from the host, including \nsystem logs, application event logs, \nauthentication records, and kernel \nactivities.  \n●​\nFile Integrity Monitoring (FIM):  \n○​\nIt \ntracks \ncritical \nsystem \nfiles, \nconfiguration \nsettings, \nand \nexecutables by comparing current \nstates against baseline snapshots to \ncatch unauthorized changes.  \n●​\nAnalysis and Matching:  \n○​\nThe system evaluates the collected \ndata using two main techniques: \nSignature-based \nand \nAnomaly-based.  \n●​\nAlerting and Logging:  \n○​\nWhen a rule violation or threat is \nfound, the HIDS logs the event \ndetails and immediately sends an \nalert to security administrators for \nresponse.  \n \nCOMPARISON TO NIDS  \n \n \n \nPAGE 8 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nJ \nLAYERED DEFENSE  \n \nLAYERED DEFENSE  \n●​\nLayered defense, also known as defense in \ndepth, is a security strategy that uses \nmultiple security controls at different levels \nof an information system.  \n●​\nInstead of depending on a single security \nmechanism, \norganizations \ncreate \nseveral \ndefensive layers.  \n●​\nIf one layer fails or is bypassed, another layer \ncan still detect, prevent, or limit the attack.  \n \nSecurity Layer  \nWhat it protects  \nExamples  \n1. Physical Security  \nPhysical access to \nequipment  \nLocks, guards, access \ncards, cameras  \n2. Network Security  \nNetwork connections \nand traffic  \nFirewalls, \nsegmentation, NAC  \n3. Detection  \nIdentifies suspicious \nactivity  \nIDS/IPS, traffic analysis  \n4. Endpoint/Host \nSecurity  \nIndividual computers \nand servers  \nAntivirus/EDR, HIDS, \nhost firewall  \n5. Application Security  \nApplications and their \nfunctions  \nAuthentication, \nauthorization, input \nvalidation  \n6. Data Security  \nInformation itself  \nEncryption, backups, ","lineStart":901,"lineEnd":1500,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822511651,"end":1789822511720}},"id":"prt_0b9bc2784001ZKQp7UElJZiFdt","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc1da2001Zz80eGbwtkT6Hp"}}
