{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822513729,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5b673187721447bab3e39fe2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":600,"offset":1501},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1501: access controls  \n1502: 7. Monitoring & \n1503: Logging  \n1504: Visibility and \n1505: investigation  \n1506: Logs, SIEM, alerts, audit \n1507: trails  \n1508:  \n1509: K \n1510: NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n1511:  \n1512: NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n1513: ●​\n1514: Network Access Control (NAC) is a security \n1515: approach that controls which users and \n1516: devices are allowed to connect to and use \n1517: network resources  \n1518:  \n1519:  \n1520: NAC CAN EVALUATE  \n1521: ●​\n1522: User identity \n1523: ○​\n1524: Username \n1525: ○​\n1526: Authentication credentials \n1527: ○​\n1528: User role \n1529: ●​\n1530: Device identity \n1531: ○​\n1532: Device type \n1533: ○​\n1534: MAC address \n1535: ○​\n1536: Device certificate \n1537: ○​\n1538: Device ownership \n1539: ●​\n1540: Security posture \n1541: ○​\n1542: Operating system \n1543: ○​\n1544: Security updates \n1545: ○​\n1546: Endpoint protection status \n1547: ○​\n1548: Security configuration \n1549: ●​\n1550: Network requirements \n1551: ○​\n1552: Which network the device should \n1553: access \n1554: ○​\n1555: Which resources the user should be \n1556: allowed to reach \n1557:  \n1558: POSSIBLE NAC DECISIONS  \n1559: 1.​\n1560: Allow  \n1561: 2.​\n1562: Deny  \n1563: 3.​\n1564: Restrict  \n1565: 4.​\n1566: Quarantine  \n1567:  \n1568: NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL PROCESS  \n1569: ●​\n1570: NAC \n1571: generally \n1572: follows \n1573: a \n1574: sequence \n1575: of \n1576: identification, \n1577: authentication, \n1578: assessment, \n1579: authorization, and monitoring.  \n1580: ●​\n1581: Identification  \n1582: ○​\n1583: The \n1584: NAC \n1585: system \n1586: determines \n1587: information about the device and \n1588: user.  \n1589: ●​\n1590: Authentication  \n1591: ○​\n1592: The user or device proves its identity  \n1593: ●​\n1594: Security Assessment  \n1595: ○​\n1596: The \n1597: NAC \n1598: system \n1599: can determine \n1600: whether the device meets security \n1601: requirements  \n1602: ●​\n1603: Authorization  \n1604: ○​\n1605: The NAC system determines what \n1606: level of access should be provided.  \n1607: ●​\n1608: Monitoring  \n1609: ○​\n1610: The device's network activity can \n1611: continue \n1612: to \n1613: be \n1614: monitored \n1615: and \n1616: logged.  \n1617:  \n1618: VERIFYING NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n1619: ●​\n1620: Network Access Control must be tested \n1621: regularly to determine whether its policies \n1622: are actually being enforced.  \n1623: ●​\n1624: Authorized User  \n1625: ○​\n1626: An authorized employee should be \n1627: able \n1628: to \n1629: access \n1630: the \n1631: resources \n1632: required for their role.  \n1633: ●​\n1634: Unauthorized User  \n1635: ○​\n1636: An unauthorized account should not \n1637: receive \n1638: access \n1639: to \n1640: protected \n1641: resources.  \n1642: ●​\n1643: Unknown Device  \n1644: ○​\n1645: A device that is not recognized or \n1646: approved should be tested.  \n1647:  \n1648: PAGE 9 \n1649: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n1650:  \n1651: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n1652: ITAS311-T \n1653: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n1654: \f \n1655:  \n1656: ●​\n1657: Non-Compliant Device  \n1658: ○​\n1659: Test a device that intentionally does \n1660: not \n1661: meet \n1662: the \n1663: organization's \n1664: requirements.  \n1665: ●​\n1666: Disabled Account  \n1667: ○​\n1668: Attempt authentication using an \n1669: account that has been disabled.  \n1670:  \n1671: L \n1672: HOST ISOLATION  \n1673:  \n1674: HOST ISOLATION  \n1675: ●​\n1676: Host isolation is a security technique that \n1677: separates a computer, server, or endpoint \n1678: from other systems when the host is \n1679: suspected \n1680: of \n1681: being \n1682: compromised \n1683: or \n1684: presenting a security risk.  \n1685: ●​\n1686: The primary purpose is containment.  \n1687: ●​\n1688: Instead of immediately shutting down the \n1689: computer, security administrators may isolate \n1690: its \n1691: network \n1692: communication \n1693: so \n1694: that \n1695: investigators can still examine the system.  \n1696:  \n1697: WHY IS HOST ISOLATION NECESSARY?  \n1698: ●​\n1699: A compromised computer could potentially: \n1700: ○​\n1701: Spread malware \n1702: ○​\n1703: Attack other computers \n1704: ○​\n1705: Steal credentials \n1706: ○​\n1707: Communicate \n1708: with \n1709: an \n1710: attacker's \n1711: server \n1712: ○​\n1713: Access sensitive resources \n1714: ○​\n1715: Transfer stolen information \n1716: ○​\n1717: Serve as a starting point for further \n1718: attacks \n1719:  \n1720: L1 \n1721: WHEN AND WHY HOST ISOLATION IS \n1722: USED  \n1723:  \n1724: HOST ISOLATION  \n1725: ●​\n1726: is normally considered when there is a \n1727: reasonable indication that a device may be \n1728: compromised.  \n1729:  \n1730: POSSIBLE INDICATORS  \n1731: ●​\n1732: Malware Detection \n1733: ○​\n1734: Malware or ransomware is detected. \n1735: ●​\n1736: Suspicious Processes \n1737: ○​\n1738: An unknown or malicious process is \n1739: running. \n1740: ●​\n1741: Abnormal Network Connections \n1742: ○​\n1743: The computer communicates with \n1744: suspicious external systems. \n1745: ●​\n1746: Credential Compromise \n1747: ○​\n1748: A user's credentials may have been \n1749: stolen. \n1750: ●​\n1751: Data Exfiltration \n1752: ○​\n1753: An unusual amount of information is \n1754: being \n1755: transferred \n1756: outside \n1757: the \n1758: organization. \n1759: ●​\n1760: Unauthorized System Changes \n1761: ○​\n1762: Important \n1763: system \n1764: files \n1765: or \n1766: configurations have been modified. \n1767: ●​\n1768: IDS/HIDS Alerts \n1769: ○​\n1770: Security \n1771: monitoring \n1772: detects \n1773: suspicious activity originating from \n1774: the host. \n1775:  \n1776: L2 \n1777: MAIN OBJECTIVES OF ISOLATION  \n1778:  \n1779: MAIN OBJECTIVES OF ISOLATION  \n1780: 1.​\n1781: Containment \n1782: ●​\n1783: Prevent the threat from spreading. \n1784: 2.​\n1785: Protection \n1786: ●​\n1787: Protect other systems and sensitive \n1788: information. \n1789: 3.​\n1790: Investigation \n1791: ●​\n1792: Allow security personnel to examine \n1793: the affected host. \n1794: 4.​ Evidence Preservation \n1795: ●​\n1796: Avoid \n1797: unnecessary \n1798: changes \n1799: that \n1800: could destroy useful investigation \n1801: evidence. \n1802: 5.​\n1803: Recovery \n1804: ●​\n1805: Provide time to remove the threat \n1806: and restore the system safely. \n1807:  \n1808:  \n1809: L3 \n1810: VERIFYING HOST ISOLATION  \n1811:  \n1812: VERIFYING HOST ISOLATION  \n1813: ●​\n1814: After isolating a potentially compromised \n1815: host, administrators must verify that the \n1816: isolation is working correctly.  \n1817:  \n1818: VERIFICATION STEPS  \n1819: 1.​\n1820: Identify the Host \n1821: ●​\n1822: Confirm the affected device and \n1823: reason for isolation. \n1824: 2.​\n1825: Apply Isolation \n1826: ●​\n1827: Use NAC, endpoint security, firewall \n1828: rules, or network segmentation. \n1829: 3.​\n1830: Test Connectivity \n1831: ●​\n1832: Check whether the host can still \n1833: access restricted systems. \n1834: 4.​ Review Logs \n1835: ●​\n1836: Examine \n1837: firewall, \n1838: NAC, \n1839: IDS, \n1840: and \n1841: endpoint \n1842: logs \n1843: for \n1844: connection \n1845: attempts. \n1846: 5.​\n1847: Compare Results \n1848: ●​\n1849: Make sure actual behavior matches \n1850: the isolation policy. \n1851:  \n1852: PAGE 10 \n1853: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n1854:  \n1855: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n1856: ITAS311-T \n1857: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n1858: \f \n1859:  \n1860: 6.​\n1861: Document Findings \n1862: ●​\n1863: Record the tests, results, and any \n1864: problems discovered. \n1865:  \n1866: M \n1867: SYSTEM HARDENING  \n1868:  \n1869: SYSTEM HARDENING  \n1870: ●​\n1871: System hardening is the process of securing \n1872: a \n1873: computer \n1874: system \n1875: by \n1876: reducing \n1877: or \n1878: eliminating vulnerabilities that could be \n1879: exploited by attackers. It involves configuring \n1880: the operating system, applications, network \n1881: services, and user accounts according to \n1882: security best practices.  \n1883: ●​\n1884: Without hardening, systems may contain \n1885: unnecessary services, outdated software, \n1886: weak \n1887: passwords, \n1888: or \n1889: excessive \n1890: user \n1891: privileges. These weaknesses can provide \n1892: attackers \n1893: with \n1894: opportunities \n1895: to \n1896: gain \n1897: unauthorized access.  \n1898: ●​\n1899: Reduce \n1900: the number of possible attack \n1901: surfaces \n1902: ●​\n1903: Prevent unauthorized access to systems and \n1904: data  \n1905: ●​\n1906: Protect systems from malware and other \n1907: cyber threats  \n1908: ●​\n1909: Ensure security configurations are properly \n1910: implemented  \n1911: ●​\n1912: Minimize unnecessary services, applications, \n1913: and permissions  \n1914:  \n1915: WHY IS IT IMPORTANT?  \n1916: ●​\n1917: Without hardening, systems may contain \n1918: unnecessary \n1919: services, \n1920: outdated \n1921: software, \n1922: weak passwords, or excessive user privileges. \n1923: These weaknesses can provide attackers with \n1924: opportunities to gain unauthorized access  \n1925: ●​\n1926: Example: A company server running an \n1927: outdated service may contain a known \n1928: vulnerability. An attacker could exploit that \n1929: vulnerability to access the server. Applying \n1930: security updates and disabling unnecessary \n1931: services reduces this risk  \n1932:  \n1933: N \n1934: MONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY \n1935: SYSTEM  \n1936:  \n1937: MONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY SYSTEM  \n1938: ●​\n1939: Detect suspicious behavior \n1940: ●​\n1941: Identify possible security incidents \n1942: ●​\n1943: Discover unauthorized access \n1944: ●​\n1945: Track changes to systems \n1946: ●​\n1947: Provide \n1948: information \n1949: for \n1950: incident \n1951: investigation \n1952: ●​\n1953: Verify that security controls are operating \n1954: correctly \n1955:  \n1956: MONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY SYSTEM  \n1957: ●​\n1958: Security monitoring and testing is the process \n1959: of continuously observing systems and \n1960: regularly \n1961: testing \n1962: security \n1963: controls \n1964: to \n1965: determine whether they are functioning \n1966: correctly and protecting against threats.  \n1967: ●​\n1968: Monitoring \n1969: involves \n1970: collecting \n1971: and \n1972: examining information about system and \n1973: network activity. Security testing evaluates \n1974: whether \n1975: security \n1976: controls \n1977: can \n1978: actually \n1979: protect a system and detect potential \n1980: threats.  \n1981: ●​\n1982: Testing \n1983: should be performed regularly \n1984: because new vulnerabilities and threats can \n1985: appear over time.  \n1986:  \n1987: COMMON SECURITY TESTING METHODS  \n1988: ●​\n1989: Vulnerability Scanning  \n1990: ○​\n1991: Searches systems for known security \n1992: weaknesses  \n1993: ○​\n1994: Identifies outdated software and \n1995: insecure configurations  \n1996: ●​\n1997: Penetration Testing  \n1998: ○​\n1999: Simulates controlled attacks against \n2000: a system  \n2001: ○​\n2002: Determines whether vulnerabilities \n2003: can actually be exploited  \n2004: ●​\n2005: Security Audits  \n2006: ○​\n2007: Review \n2008: security \n2009: policies, \n2010: configurations, and procedures  \n2011: ○​\n2012: Verify \n2013: compliance \n2014: with \n2015: security \n2016: requirements  \n2017: ●​\n2018: Log and Alert Testing  \n2019: ○​\n2020: Generates controlled security events  \n2021: ○​\n2022: Checks whether monitoring tools \n2023: properly record and report them  \n2024:  \n2025: L5: POST AUDIT ACTIVITIES \n2026:  \n2027: TOPIC OVERVIEW \n2028: A.​ EXIT INTERVIEW \n2029: B.​ DATA ANALYSIS \n2030: C.​ AUDIT REPORT \n2031: a.​\n2032: Parts of an Audit Report \n2033: b.​\n2034: Presentation of Audit Findings \n2035: c.​\n2036: Qualities Of A Good Audit Report \n2037: d.​\n2038: Summary \n2039: D.​ PRESENTATION OF FINDINGS \n2040: a.​\n2041: Advantages and Disadvantages \n2042: b.​\n2043: Example \n2044:  \n2045:  \n2046: PAGE 11 \n2047: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n2048:  \n2049: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n2050: ITAS311-T \n2051: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n2052: \f \n2053:  \n2054: A \n2055: EXIT INTERVIEW \n2056:  \n2057: DESCRIPTION \n2058: ●​\n2059: Definition: \n2060: ○​\n2061: The final formal meeting conducted \n2062: between the security audit team and \n2063: key \n2064: organizational \n2065: stakeholders/management \n2066: before \n2067: the official audit report is published.  \n2068: ●​\n2069: Core Concept: \n2070: ○​\n2071: Acts as an initial, verbal debriefing \n2072: session \n2073: designed \n2074: to \n2075: discuss \n2076: the \n2077: execution \n2078: of \n2079: the \n2080: audit, \n2081: initial \n2082: observations, \n2083: and \n2084: preliminary \n2085: findings in a controlled environment.  \n2086: ●​\n2087: Context in Security Auditing: \n2088: ○​\n2089: Represents the transition point from \n2090: active field testing and evidence \n2091: collection to formal reporting and \n2092: remediation planning. \n2093:  \n2094: PURPOSE \n2095: ●​\n2096: Prevent \"No Surprises\": \n2097: ○​\n2098: Ensures executive management and \n2099: system owners are not caught off \n2100: guard by critical findings when the \n\n(Showing lines 1501-2100 of 5328. Use offset=2101 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"access controls  \n7. Monitoring & \nLogging  \nVisibility and \ninvestigation  \nLogs, SIEM, alerts, audit \ntrails  \n \nK \nNETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n \nNETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n●​\nNetwork Access Control (NAC) is a security \napproach that controls which users and \ndevices are allowed to connect to and use \nnetwork resources  \n \n \nNAC CAN EVALUATE  ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":"access controls  \n7. Monitoring & \nLogging  \nVisibility and \ninvestigation  \nLogs, SIEM, alerts, audit \ntrails  \n \nK \nNETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n \nNETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n●​\nNetwork Access Control (NAC) is a security \napproach that controls which users and \ndevices are allowed to connect to and use \nnetwork resources  \n \n \nNAC CAN EVALUATE  \n●​\nUser identity \n○​\nUsername \n○​\nAuthentication credentials \n○​\nUser role \n●​\nDevice identity \n○​\nDevice type \n○​\nMAC address \n○​\nDevice certificate \n○​\nDevice ownership \n●​\nSecurity posture \n○​\nOperating system \n○​\nSecurity updates \n○​\nEndpoint protection status \n○​\nSecurity configuration \n●​\nNetwork requirements \n○​\nWhich network the device should \naccess \n○​\nWhich resources the user should be \nallowed to reach \n \nPOSSIBLE NAC DECISIONS  \n1.​\nAllow  \n2.​\nDeny  \n3.​\nRestrict  \n4.​\nQuarantine  \n \nNETWORK ACCESS CONTROL PROCESS  \n●​\nNAC \ngenerally \nfollows \na \nsequence \nof \nidentification, \nauthentication, \nassessment, \nauthorization, and monitoring.  \n●​\nIdentification  \n○​\nThe \nNAC \nsystem \ndetermines \ninformation about the device and \nuser.  \n●​\nAuthentication  \n○​\nThe user or device proves its identity  \n●​\nSecurity Assessment  \n○​\nThe \nNAC \nsystem \ncan determine \nwhether the device meets security \nrequirements  \n●​\nAuthorization  \n○​\nThe NAC system determines what \nlevel of access should be provided.  \n●​\nMonitoring  \n○​\nThe device's network activity can \ncontinue \nto \nbe \nmonitored \nand \nlogged.  \n \nVERIFYING NETWORK ACCESS CONTROL  \n●​\nNetwork Access Control must be tested \nregularly to determine whether its policies \nare actually being enforced.  \n●​\nAuthorized User  \n○​\nAn authorized employee should be \nable \nto \naccess \nthe \nresources \nrequired for their role.  \n●​\nUnauthorized User  \n○​\nAn unauthorized account should not \nreceive \naccess \nto \nprotected \nresources.  \n●​\nUnknown Device  \n○​\nA device that is not recognized or \napproved should be tested.  \n \nPAGE 9 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nNon-Compliant Device  \n○​\nTest a device that intentionally does \nnot \nmeet \nthe \norganization's \nrequirements.  \n●​\nDisabled Account  \n○​\nAttempt authentication using an \naccount that has been disabled.  \n \nL \nHOST ISOLATION  \n \nHOST ISOLATION  \n●​\nHost isolation is a security technique that \nseparates a computer, server, or endpoint \nfrom other systems when the host is \nsuspected \nof \nbeing \ncompromised \nor \npresenting a security risk.  \n●​\nThe primary purpose is containment.  \n●​\nInstead of immediately shutting down the \ncomputer, security administrators may isolate \nits \nnetwork \ncommunication \nso \nthat \ninvestigators can still examine the system.  \n \nWHY IS HOST ISOLATION NECESSARY?  \n●​\nA compromised computer could potentially: \n○​\nSpread malware \n○​\nAttack other computers \n○​\nSteal credentials \n○​\nCommunicate \nwith \nan \nattacker's \nserver \n○​\nAccess sensitive resources \n○​\nTransfer stolen information \n○​\nServe as a starting point for further \nattacks \n \nL1 \nWHEN AND WHY HOST ISOLATION IS \nUSED  \n \nHOST ISOLATION  \n●​\nis normally considered when there is a \nreasonable indication that a device may be \ncompromised.  \n \nPOSSIBLE INDICATORS  \n●​\nMalware Detection \n○​\nMalware or ransomware is detected. \n●​\nSuspicious Processes \n○​\nAn unknown or malicious process is \nrunning. \n●​\nAbnormal Network Connections \n○​\nThe computer communicates with \nsuspicious external systems. \n●​\nCredential Compromise \n○​\nA user's credentials may have been \nstolen. \n●​\nData Exfiltration \n○​\nAn unusual amount of information is \nbeing \ntransferred \noutside \nthe \norganization. \n●​\nUnauthorized System Changes \n○​\nImportant \nsystem \nfiles \nor \nconfigurations have been modified. \n●​\nIDS/HIDS Alerts \n○​\nSecurity \nmonitoring \ndetects \nsuspicious activity originating from \nthe host. \n \nL2 \nMAIN OBJECTIVES OF ISOLATION  \n \nMAIN OBJECTIVES OF ISOLATION  \n1.​\nContainment \n●​\nPrevent the threat from spreading. \n2.​\nProtection \n●​\nProtect other systems and sensitive \ninformation. \n3.​\nInvestigation \n●​\nAllow security personnel to examine \nthe affected host. \n4.​ Evidence Preservation \n●​\nAvoid \nunnecessary \nchanges \nthat \ncould destroy useful investigation \nevidence. \n5.​\nRecovery \n●​\nProvide time to remove the threat \nand restore the system safely. \n \n \nL3 \nVERIFYING HOST ISOLATION  \n \nVERIFYING HOST ISOLATION  \n●​\nAfter isolating a potentially compromised \nhost, administrators must verify that the \nisolation is working correctly.  \n \nVERIFICATION STEPS  \n1.​\nIdentify the Host \n●​\nConfirm the affected device and \nreason for isolation. \n2.​\nApply Isolation \n●​\nUse NAC, endpoint security, firewall \nrules, or network segmentation. \n3.​\nTest Connectivity \n●​\nCheck whether the host can still \naccess restricted systems. \n4.​ Review Logs \n●​\nExamine \nfirewall, \nNAC, \nIDS, \nand \nendpoint \nlogs \nfor \nconnection \nattempts. \n5.​\nCompare Results \n●​\nMake sure actual behavior matches \nthe isolation policy. \n \nPAGE 10 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n6.​\nDocument Findings \n●​\nRecord the tests, results, and any \nproblems discovered. \n \nM \nSYSTEM HARDENING  \n \nSYSTEM HARDENING  \n●​\nSystem hardening is the process of securing \na \ncomputer \nsystem \nby \nreducing \nor \neliminating vulnerabilities that could be \nexploited by attackers. It involves configuring \nthe operating system, applications, network \nservices, and user accounts according to \nsecurity best practices.  \n●​\nWithout hardening, systems may contain \nunnecessary services, outdated software, \nweak \npasswords, \nor \nexcessive \nuser \nprivileges. These weaknesses can provide \nattackers \nwith \nopportunities \nto \ngain \nunauthorized access.  \n●​\nReduce \nthe number of possible attack \nsurfaces \n●​\nPrevent unauthorized access to systems and \ndata  \n●​\nProtect systems from malware and other \ncyber threats  \n●​\nEnsure security configurations are properly \nimplemented  \n●​\nMinimize unnecessary services, applications, \nand permissions  \n \nWHY IS IT IMPORTANT?  \n●​\nWithout hardening, systems may contain \nunnecessary \nservices, \noutdated \nsoftware, \nweak passwords, or excessive user privileges. \nThese weaknesses can provide attackers with \nopportunities to gain unauthorized access  \n●​\nExample: A company server running an \noutdated service may contain a known \nvulnerability. An attacker could exploit that \nvulnerability to access the server. Applying \nsecurity updates and disabling unnecessary \nservices reduces this risk  \n \nN \nMONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY \nSYSTEM  \n \nMONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY SYSTEM  \n●​\nDetect suspicious behavior \n●​\nIdentify possible security incidents \n●​\nDiscover unauthorized access \n●​\nTrack changes to systems \n●​\nProvide \ninformation \nfor \nincident \ninvestigation \n●​\nVerify that security controls are operating \ncorrectly \n \nMONITORING AND TESTING SECURITY SYSTEM  \n●​\nSecurity monitoring and testing is the process \nof continuously observing systems and \nregularly \ntesting \nsecurity \ncontrols \nto \ndetermine whether they are functioning \ncorrectly and protecting against threats.  \n●​\nMonitoring \ninvolves \ncollecting \nand \nexamining information about system and \nnetwork activity. Security testing evaluates \nwhether \nsecurity \ncontrols \ncan \nactually \nprotect a system and detect potential \nthreats.  \n●​\nTesting \nshould be performed regularly \nbecause new vulnerabilities and threats can \nappear over time.  \n \nCOMMON SECURITY TESTING METHODS  \n●​\nVulnerability Scanning  \n○​\nSearches systems for known security \nweaknesses  \n○​\nIdentifies outdated software and \ninsecure configurations  \n●​\nPenetration Testing  \n○​\nSimulates controlled attacks against \na system  \n○​\nDetermines whether vulnerabilities \ncan actually be exploited  \n●​\nSecurity Audits  \n○​\nReview \nsecurity \npolicies, \nconfigurations, and procedures  \n○​\nVerify \ncompliance \nwith \nsecurity \nrequirements  \n●​\nLog and Alert Testing  \n○​\nGenerates controlled security events  \n○​\nChecks whether monitoring tools \nproperly record and report them  \n \nL5: POST AUDIT ACTIVITIES \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ EXIT INTERVIEW \nB.​ DATA ANALYSIS \nC.​ AUDIT REPORT \na.​\nParts of an Audit Report \nb.​\nPresentation of Audit Findings \nc.​\nQualities Of A Good Audit Report \nd.​\nSummary \nD.​ PRESENTATION OF FINDINGS \na.​\nAdvantages and Disadvantages \nb.​\nExample \n \n \nPAGE 11 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nA \nEXIT INTERVIEW \n \nDESCRIPTION \n●​\nDefinition: \n○​\nThe final formal meeting conducted \nbetween the security audit team and \nkey \norganizational \nstakeholders/management \nbefore \nthe official audit report is published.  \n●​\nCore Concept: \n○​\nActs as an initial, verbal debriefing \nsession \ndesigned \nto \ndiscuss \nthe \nexecution \nof \nthe \naudit, \ninitial \nobservations, \nand \npreliminary \nfindings in a controlled environment.  \n●​\nContext in Security Auditing: \n○​\nRepresents the transition point from \nactive field testing and evidence \ncollection to formal reporting and \nremediation planning. \n \nPURPOSE \n●​\nPrevent \"No Surprises\": \n○​\nEnsures executive management and \nsystem owners are not caught off \nguard by critical findings when the ","lineStart":1501,"lineEnd":2100,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822513674,"end":1789822513705}},"id":"prt_0b9bc2fef001BC1wR9irBI7fgQ","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc2a86001p24bQCF6LIjtLI"}}
