{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822517065,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_7caf79c05bcf437682a3869b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":600,"offset":2701},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n2701: to ensure that important information is \n2702: presented clearly and consistently. Each \n2703: section has a specific purpose, beginning \n2704: with the identification of the report and its \n2705: recipient, followed by information about the \n2706: audit, the responsibilities of management \n2707: and the auditor, and the auditor’s opinion. \n2708: ●​\n2709: The format also identifies the basis for the \n2710: opinion \n2711: and \n2712: other \n2713: required \n2714: reporting \n2715: responsibilities. \n2716: Finally, \n2717: the \n2718: auditor’s \n2719: signature, place of signature, and date are \n2720: included to formally establish the authenticity \n2721: and completion of the audit report. \n2722:  \n2723: C1 \n2724: PARTS OF AN AUDIT REPORT \n2725:  \n2726: PARTS OF AN AUDIT REPORT \n2727: ●​\n2728: Title – Identifies the document as an official \n2729: audit report. \n2730: ●​\n2731: Addressee – States who the audit report is \n2732: intended for. \n2733:  \n2734: PAGE 14 \n2735: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n2736:  \n2737: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n2738: ITAS311-T \n2739: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n2740: \f \n2741:  \n2742: ●​\n2743: Introductory \n2744: Paragraph \n2745: – \n2746: Provides \n2747: the \n2748: background, purpose, and scope of the audit. \n2749: ●​\n2750: Management’s \n2751: Responsibility \n2752: – Explains \n2753: management’s responsibility for the financial \n2754: information, \n2755: systems, \n2756: or \n2757: controls \n2758: being \n2759: audited. \n2760: ●​\n2761: Auditor’s Responsibility – Describes the \n2762: auditor’s duty to conduct the audit and \n2763: evaluate the evidence. \n2764: ●​\n2765: Opinion – Presents the auditor’s conclusion \n2766: based on the audit results. \n2767: ●​\n2768: Basis of the Opinion – Explains the evidence \n2769: and procedures supporting the auditor’s \n2770: opinion. \n2771: ●​\n2772: Other Reporting Responsibility – Includes \n2773: additional matters that the auditor is required \n2774: to report. \n2775: ●​\n2776: Signature of the Auditor – Identifies the \n2777: auditor who conducted or approved the audit \n2778: report. \n2779: ●​\n2780: Place of Signature – Indicates the location \n2781: where the report was signed. \n2782: ●​\n2783: Date of Audit Report – States the date when \n2784: the audit report was completed and issued. \n2785:  \n2786: C2 \n2787: PRESENTATION OF AUDIT FINDINGS \n2788:  \n2789: PRESENTATION OF AUDIT FINDINGS \n2790: ●​\n2791: Audit findings communicate the problems, \n2792: weaknesses, or risks discovered during the \n2793: audit. They should be presented clearly and \n2794: objectively, with each finding supported by \n2795: sufficient evidence. This allows stakeholders \n2796: to understand exactly what was observed \n2797: and why the issue is considered a concern. \n2798: ●​\n2799: A \n2800: finding \n2801: may \n2802: describe the condition, \n2803: criteria, \n2804: cause, \n2805: effect \n2806: or \n2807: risk, \n2808: and \n2809: recommended corrective action. Organizing \n2810: findings in this way helps management \n2811: understand the significance of each issue \n2812: and \n2813: determine the appropriate actions \n2814: needed to address or reduce the identified \n2815: risks. \n2816: ●​\n2817: Finding Components: \n2818: ○​\n2819: Condition – What was found or \n2820: observed \n2821: ○​\n2822: Criteria – What should have been in \n2823: place \n2824: ○​\n2825: Cause – Why the issue occurred \n2826: ○​\n2827: Effect/Risk – Possible impact \n2828: ○​\n2829: Recommendation – Action needed \n2830: to address the issue \n2831:  \n2832:  \n2833:  \n2834: C3 \n2835: QUALITIES OF A GOOD AUDIT REPORT \n2836:  \n2837: QUALITIES OF A GOOD AUDIT REPORT \n2838: ●​\n2839: A \n2840: good \n2841: audit \n2842: report \n2843: should \n2844: present \n2845: information that is accurate, objective, clear, \n2846: constructive, timely, and confidential. These \n2847: qualities help ensure that the report is \n2848: reliable \n2849: and \n2850: understandable \n2851: to \n2852: both \n2853: technical personnel and management, while \n2854: preventing \n2855: unsupported \n2856: claims \n2857: or \n2858: unnecessary bias in presenting the audit \n2859: results. \n2860: ●​\n2861: The \n2862: recommendations \n2863: should \n2864: also \n2865: be \n2866: practical \n2867: and \n2868: actionable \n2869: so \n2870: that \n2871: management can use them to address \n2872: identified weaknesses. Since audit reports \n2873: may contain sensitive information about \n2874: systems and security controls, appropriate \n2875: confidentiality and distribution practices \n2876: should also be maintained. \n2877:  \n2878: ○​\n2879: Accurate – Supported by evidence \n2880: ○​\n2881: Objective – Free from personal bias \n2882: ○​\n2883: Clear – Easy to understand \n2884: ○​\n2885: Constructive \n2886: – \n2887: Provides \n2888: practical \n2889: solutions \n2890: ○​\n2891: Timely – Issued promptly \n2892: ○​\n2893: Confidential – Sensitive information \n2894: is protected \n2895:  \n2896: C4 \n2897: SUMMARY \n2898:  \n2899: SUMMARY \n2900: ●​\n2901: The generation of an audit report transforms \n2902: the evidence and findings gathered during \n2903: the audit into a formal document. It provides \n2904: stakeholders with a structured presentation \n2905: of what was examined, what was discovered, \n2906: and whether the organization’s controls and \n2907: requirements were properly implemented. \n2908: ●​\n2909: The report ultimately communicates the \n2910: auditor’s \n2911: conclusion \n2912: or \n2913: opinion \n2914: and \n2915: identifies \n2916: areas \n2917: that \n2918: may \n2919: require \n2920: improvement. \n2921: Through \n2922: documented \n2923: findings and recommendations, the audit \n2924: report helps management understand risks \n2925: and \n2926: provides \n2927: a \n2928: basis \n2929: for \n2930: appropriate \n2931: corrective actions. \n2932: ●​\n2933: Audit Evidence → Analysis → Findings → \n2934: Opinion/Conclusion → Audit Report \n2935:  \n2936:  \n2937:  \n2938:  \n2939: PAGE 15 \n2940: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n2941:  \n2942: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n2943: ITAS311-T \n2944: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n2945: \f \n2946:  \n2947: D \n2948: PRESENTATION OF FINDINGS \n2949:  \n2950: CONCEPT \n2951: ●​\n2952: The Presentation of Findings is the pivotal \n2953: final communication phase in the post-audit \n2954: lifecycle where auditors formally deliver, \n2955: explain, and contextualize verified audit \n2956: results to management, process owners, and \n2957: governance committees. Rather than simply \n2958: handing over raw data, this process translates \n2959: complex technical or operational evidence \n2960: into clear business risks and practical \n2961: opportunities for improvement. It serves as \n2962: the bridge between investigative analysis \n2963: and real-world corrective action, ensuring \n2964: organizational alignment on critical issues. \n2965: ○​\n2966: Core Purpose: Transforms complex \n2967: audit \n2968: evidence \n2969: into \n2970: actionable \n2971: business insights. \n2972: ○​\n2973: Strategic Role: Connects technical \n2974: analysis \n2975: with \n2976: executive \n2977: decision-making and remediation. \n2978:  \n2979: DESCRIPTION \n2980: ●​\n2981: This activity typically takes place in a formal \n2982: closing meeting, executive presentation, or \n2983: board review using visual slide decks and \n2984: summary \n2985: exhibits. \n2986: The \n2987: discussion \n2988: is \n2989: designed to be constructive, transparent, \n2990: and forward-looking, moving away from \n2991: fault-finding toward strengthening internal \n2992: controls, \n2993: regulatory \n2994: compliance, \n2995: and \n2996: operational resilience. \n2997: ○​\n2998: Core Deliverables: Summarizes key \n2999: control gaps, operational risks, and \n3000: policy non-compliances. \n3001: ○​\n3002: Risk Categorization: Prioritizes issues \n3003: using severity ratings (High, Medium, \n3004: Low) to clarify operational impact. \n3005: ○​\n3006: Remediation \n3007: Roadmap: \n3008: Outlines \n3009: proposed \n3010: Corrective \n3011: Action Plans \n3012: (CAP), implementation deadlines, and \n3013: assigned accountability. \n3014:  \n3015: D1 \n3016: ADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES \n3017:  \n3018: ADVANTAGES \n3019: ●​\n3020: Immediate Clarity & Direct Dialogue:  \n3021: ○​\n3022: Allows stakeholders to ask clarifying \n3023: questions \n3024: and \n3025: eliminates \n3026: misunderstandings \n3027: before \n3028: final \n3029: reports are permanently archived. \n3030: ●​\n3031: Faster Stakeholder Buy-in:  \n3032: ○​\n3033: Presenting \n3034: findings \n3035: interactively \n3036: helps executives see the business \n3037: impact \n3038: immediately, \n3039: accelerating \n3040: approval \n3041: for \n3042: budgets \n3043: and \n3044: remediation resources. \n3045: ●​\n3046: Shared Accountability:  \n3047: ○​\n3048: Establishes consensus on the root \n3049: causes \n3050: of \n3051: non-compliance \n3052: and \n3053: clearly assigns responsible parties \n3054: for corrective actions. \n3055: ●​\n3056: Strategic Value Addition:  \n3057: ○​\n3058: Elevates the perception of an audit \n3059: from \n3060: a \n3061: policing \n3062: exercise \n3063: to \n3064: a \n3065: value-driving \n3066: partnership \n3067: that \n3068: strengthens governance. \n3069:  \n3070: DISADVANTAGES \n3071: ●​\n3072: Defensiveness & Conflict:  \n3073: ○​\n3074: Stakeholders \n3075: may \n3076: become \n3077: combative or dispute findings if \n3078: non-conformities reflect poorly on \n3079: their department's performance. \n3080: ●​\n3081: Misinterpretation Risk:  \n3082: ○​\n3083: Condensing \n3084: detailed \n3085: technical \n3086: or \n3087: financial \n3088: data \n3089: into \n3090: summary \n3091: presentation \n3092: slides \n3093: can \n3094: lead \n3095: to \n3096: oversimplification \n3097: or \n3098: taken-out-of-context takeaways. \n3099: ●​\n3100: Time & Resource Intensive:  \n3101: ○​\n3102: Preparing high-stakes visual decks \n3103: and coordinating executive schedules \n3104: requires \n3105: substantial \n3106: auditor \n3107: bandwidth. \n3108: ●​\n3109: Scope Creep during Discussion:  \n3110: ○​\n3111: Open-floor \n3112: discussions can easily \n3113: derail into operational complaints or \n3114: issues beyond the defined scope of \n3115: the audit. \n3116:  \n3117: D2 \n3118: EXAMPLES \n3119:  \n3120: IT & Cybersecurity Audit: \n3121: ●​\n3122: Finding: \"58% of employee workstations are \n3123: missing critical security patches, and default \n3124: administrator credentials were found active \n3125: on network switches.\" \n3126: ●​\n3127: Impact Presentation: Visualizes the risk of \n3128: ransomware \n3129: infiltration \n3130: and \n3131: presents \n3132: a \n3133: mandatory \n3134: 14-day \n3135: automated \n3136: patch \n3137: deployment plan. \n3138:  \n3139: Financial Compliance & Inventory Audit: \n3140: ●​\n3141: Finding: \"A 12% discrepancy exists between \n3142: the ERP physical inventory logs and the \n3143: warehouse cycle counts due to delayed \n3144: manual data entry.\" \n3145:  \n3146: PAGE 16 \n3147: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n3148:  \n3149: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n3150: ITAS311-T \n3151: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n3152: \f \n3153:  \n3154: ●​\n3155: Impact Presentation: Displays loss trends via \n3156: bar charts and proposes a barcode-scanning \n3157: workflow to prevent reconciliation errors. \n3158:  \n3159: Quality & Safety (ISO/HACCP) Audit: \n3160: ●​\n3161: Finding: \"Storage temperatures in Cold Room \n3162: B exceeded the 4°C limit three times during \n3163: the quarter without documented corrective \n3164: actions.\" \n3165: ●​\n3166: Impact Presentation: Highlights regulatory \n3167: non-compliance \n3168: risks \n3169: and \n3170: presents \n3171: an \n3172: automated \n3173: IoT \n3174: temperature-alerting \n3175: framework. \n3176:  \n3177: L6: RISK MANAGEMENT PROCESS \n3178:  \n3179: TOPIC OVERVIEW \n3180: A.​ RISK IDENTIFICATION \n3181: B.​ RISK ASSESSMENT \n3182: C.​ IMPLEMENTING & MONITORING \n3183:  \n3184: A \n3185: RISK IDENTIFICATION \n3186:  \n3187: WHAT IS RISK IDENTIFICATION? \n3188: ●​\n3189: It is the process of finding, recognizing, and \n3190: documenting potential risks that may affect \n3191: a project. \n3192: ●​\n3193: STARTING \n3194: POINT \n3195: of \n3196: risk \n3197: management \n3198: process. \n3199: ●​\n3200: “WHAT \n3201: COULD \n3202: GO \n3203: WRONG \n3204: WHILE \n3205: DEVELOPING OUR SYSTEM?” \n3206:  \n3207: WHY IS RISK IDENTIFICATION IMPORTANT? \n3208: ●​\n3209: Recognize potential problems early \n3210: ●​\n3211: Prepare appropriate responses \n3212: ●​\n3213: Reduce the possibility of response issues \n3214: ●​\n3215: Protect the project’s schedule, budget and \n3216: objectives \n3217: ●​\n3218: Help the team prioritize which risks need \n3219: attention \n3220:  \n3221: COMMON RISK IDENTIFICATION TOOLS AND \n3222: TECHNIQUES \n3223: 1.​\n3224: BRAINSTORMING \n3225: ●​\n3226: Team members identify risks such \n3227: as lack of resources, technical errors, \n3228: or delays. \n3229: ●​\n3230: EXAMPLE: Team members identify \n3231: risks \n3232: such \n3233: as \n3234: lack of resources, \n3235: technical errors, or delays. \n3236:  \n3237:  \n3238:  \n3239: 2.​\n3240: CHECKLISTS \n3241: ●​\n3242: A \n3243: list \n3244: of \n3245: common \n3246: risks \n3247: from \n3248: previous \n3249: projects \n3250: or established \n3251: standards is used to identify possible \n3252: risks. \n3253: ●​\n3254: EXAMPLE: \n3255: A \n3256: software \n3257: project \n3258: checklist \n3259: may \n3260: include \n3261: security \n3262: problems, data loss, software bugs, \n3263: and system downtime. \n3264: 3.​\n3265: SWOT ANALYSIS \n3266: ●​\n3267: SWOT examines: \n3268: ○​\n3269: S – Strengths \n3270: ○​\n3271: W – Weaknesses \n3272: ○​\n3273: O – Opportunities \n3274: ○​\n3275: T – Threats \n3276: 4.​ EXPERT INTERVIEWS \n3277: ●​\n3278: People with specialized knowledge \n3279: or \n3280: experience \n3281: are \n3282: asked \n3283: about \n3284: possible risks. \n3285: ●​\n3286: EXAMPLE: \n3287: A cybersecurity expert \n3288: may identify security vulnerabilities \n3289: that the project team might overlook. \n3290: 5.​\n3291: DOCUMENTATION REVIEW \n3292: ●​\n3293: Existing \n3294: project \n3295: documents, \n3296: contracts, schedules, requirements, \n3297: and reports are reviewed to find \n3298: potential risks. \n3299: 6.​\n3300: HISTORICAL INFORMATION \n\n(Showing lines 2701-3300 of 5328. Use offset=3301 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"to ensure that important information is \npresented clearly and consistently. Each \nsection has a specific purpose, beginning \nwith the identification of the report and its \nrecipient, followed by information about the \naudit, the responsibilities of management \nand the auditor, and the auditor’s opinion. \n●​\nThe format also identifies the basis for the \nopinion \nand \nother \nrequired \nreporting \nresponsibilities. \nFinally, \nthe \nauditor’s \nsignature, place of signature, and date are \nincluded to formally establish the authenticity ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":"to ensure that important information is \npresented clearly and consistently. Each \nsection has a specific purpose, beginning \nwith the identification of the report and its \nrecipient, followed by information about the \naudit, the responsibilities of management \nand the auditor, and the auditor’s opinion. \n●​\nThe format also identifies the basis for the \nopinion \nand \nother \nrequired \nreporting \nresponsibilities. \nFinally, \nthe \nauditor’s \nsignature, place of signature, and date are \nincluded to formally establish the authenticity \nand completion of the audit report. \n \nC1 \nPARTS OF AN AUDIT REPORT \n \nPARTS OF AN AUDIT REPORT \n●​\nTitle – Identifies the document as an official \naudit report. \n●​\nAddressee – States who the audit report is \nintended for. \n \nPAGE 14 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nIntroductory \nParagraph \n– \nProvides \nthe \nbackground, purpose, and scope of the audit. \n●​\nManagement’s \nResponsibility \n– Explains \nmanagement’s responsibility for the financial \ninformation, \nsystems, \nor \ncontrols \nbeing \naudited. \n●​\nAuditor’s Responsibility – Describes the \nauditor’s duty to conduct the audit and \nevaluate the evidence. \n●​\nOpinion – Presents the auditor’s conclusion \nbased on the audit results. \n●​\nBasis of the Opinion – Explains the evidence \nand procedures supporting the auditor’s \nopinion. \n●​\nOther Reporting Responsibility – Includes \nadditional matters that the auditor is required \nto report. \n●​\nSignature of the Auditor – Identifies the \nauditor who conducted or approved the audit \nreport. \n●​\nPlace of Signature – Indicates the location \nwhere the report was signed. \n●​\nDate of Audit Report – States the date when \nthe audit report was completed and issued. \n \nC2 \nPRESENTATION OF AUDIT FINDINGS \n \nPRESENTATION OF AUDIT FINDINGS \n●​\nAudit findings communicate the problems, \nweaknesses, or risks discovered during the \naudit. They should be presented clearly and \nobjectively, with each finding supported by \nsufficient evidence. This allows stakeholders \nto understand exactly what was observed \nand why the issue is considered a concern. \n●​\nA \nfinding \nmay \ndescribe the condition, \ncriteria, \ncause, \neffect \nor \nrisk, \nand \nrecommended corrective action. Organizing \nfindings in this way helps management \nunderstand the significance of each issue \nand \ndetermine the appropriate actions \nneeded to address or reduce the identified \nrisks. \n●​\nFinding Components: \n○​\nCondition – What was found or \nobserved \n○​\nCriteria – What should have been in \nplace \n○​\nCause – Why the issue occurred \n○​\nEffect/Risk – Possible impact \n○​\nRecommendation – Action needed \nto address the issue \n \n \n \nC3 \nQUALITIES OF A GOOD AUDIT REPORT \n \nQUALITIES OF A GOOD AUDIT REPORT \n●​\nA \ngood \naudit \nreport \nshould \npresent \ninformation that is accurate, objective, clear, \nconstructive, timely, and confidential. These \nqualities help ensure that the report is \nreliable \nand \nunderstandable \nto \nboth \ntechnical personnel and management, while \npreventing \nunsupported \nclaims \nor \nunnecessary bias in presenting the audit \nresults. \n●​\nThe \nrecommendations \nshould \nalso \nbe \npractical \nand \nactionable \nso \nthat \nmanagement can use them to address \nidentified weaknesses. Since audit reports \nmay contain sensitive information about \nsystems and security controls, appropriate \nconfidentiality and distribution practices \nshould also be maintained. \n \n○​\nAccurate – Supported by evidence \n○​\nObjective – Free from personal bias \n○​\nClear – Easy to understand \n○​\nConstructive \n– \nProvides \npractical \nsolutions \n○​\nTimely – Issued promptly \n○​\nConfidential – Sensitive information \nis protected \n \nC4 \nSUMMARY \n \nSUMMARY \n●​\nThe generation of an audit report transforms \nthe evidence and findings gathered during \nthe audit into a formal document. It provides \nstakeholders with a structured presentation \nof what was examined, what was discovered, \nand whether the organization’s controls and \nrequirements were properly implemented. \n●​\nThe report ultimately communicates the \nauditor’s \nconclusion \nor \nopinion \nand \nidentifies \nareas \nthat \nmay \nrequire \nimprovement. \nThrough \ndocumented \nfindings and recommendations, the audit \nreport helps management understand risks \nand \nprovides \na \nbasis \nfor \nappropriate \ncorrective actions. \n●​\nAudit Evidence → Analysis → Findings → \nOpinion/Conclusion → Audit Report \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 15 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nD \nPRESENTATION OF FINDINGS \n \nCONCEPT \n●​\nThe Presentation of Findings is the pivotal \nfinal communication phase in the post-audit \nlifecycle where auditors formally deliver, \nexplain, and contextualize verified audit \nresults to management, process owners, and \ngovernance committees. Rather than simply \nhanding over raw data, this process translates \ncomplex technical or operational evidence \ninto clear business risks and practical \nopportunities for improvement. It serves as \nthe bridge between investigative analysis \nand real-world corrective action, ensuring \norganizational alignment on critical issues. \n○​\nCore Purpose: Transforms complex \naudit \nevidence \ninto \nactionable \nbusiness insights. \n○​\nStrategic Role: Connects technical \nanalysis \nwith \nexecutive \ndecision-making and remediation. \n \nDESCRIPTION \n●​\nThis activity typically takes place in a formal \nclosing meeting, executive presentation, or \nboard review using visual slide decks and \nsummary \nexhibits. \nThe \ndiscussion \nis \ndesigned to be constructive, transparent, \nand forward-looking, moving away from \nfault-finding toward strengthening internal \ncontrols, \nregulatory \ncompliance, \nand \noperational resilience. \n○​\nCore Deliverables: Summarizes key \ncontrol gaps, operational risks, and \npolicy non-compliances. \n○​\nRisk Categorization: Prioritizes issues \nusing severity ratings (High, Medium, \nLow) to clarify operational impact. \n○​\nRemediation \nRoadmap: \nOutlines \nproposed \nCorrective \nAction Plans \n(CAP), implementation deadlines, and \nassigned accountability. \n \nD1 \nADVANTAGES AND DISADVANTAGES \n \nADVANTAGES \n●​\nImmediate Clarity & Direct Dialogue:  \n○​\nAllows stakeholders to ask clarifying \nquestions \nand \neliminates \nmisunderstandings \nbefore \nfinal \nreports are permanently archived. \n●​\nFaster Stakeholder Buy-in:  \n○​\nPresenting \nfindings \ninteractively \nhelps executives see the business \nimpact \nimmediately, \naccelerating \napproval \nfor \nbudgets \nand \nremediation resources. \n●​\nShared Accountability:  \n○​\nEstablishes consensus on the root \ncauses \nof \nnon-compliance \nand \nclearly assigns responsible parties \nfor corrective actions. \n●​\nStrategic Value Addition:  \n○​\nElevates the perception of an audit \nfrom \na \npolicing \nexercise \nto \na \nvalue-driving \npartnership \nthat \nstrengthens governance. \n \nDISADVANTAGES \n●​\nDefensiveness & Conflict:  \n○​\nStakeholders \nmay \nbecome \ncombative or dispute findings if \nnon-conformities reflect poorly on \ntheir department's performance. \n●​\nMisinterpretation Risk:  \n○​\nCondensing \ndetailed \ntechnical \nor \nfinancial \ndata \ninto \nsummary \npresentation \nslides \ncan \nlead \nto \noversimplification \nor \ntaken-out-of-context takeaways. \n●​\nTime & Resource Intensive:  \n○​\nPreparing high-stakes visual decks \nand coordinating executive schedules \nrequires \nsubstantial \nauditor \nbandwidth. \n●​\nScope Creep during Discussion:  \n○​\nOpen-floor \ndiscussions can easily \nderail into operational complaints or \nissues beyond the defined scope of \nthe audit. \n \nD2 \nEXAMPLES \n \nIT & Cybersecurity Audit: \n●​\nFinding: \"58% of employee workstations are \nmissing critical security patches, and default \nadministrator credentials were found active \non network switches.\" \n●​\nImpact Presentation: Visualizes the risk of \nransomware \ninfiltration \nand \npresents \na \nmandatory \n14-day \nautomated \npatch \ndeployment plan. \n \nFinancial Compliance & Inventory Audit: \n●​\nFinding: \"A 12% discrepancy exists between \nthe ERP physical inventory logs and the \nwarehouse cycle counts due to delayed \nmanual data entry.\" \n \nPAGE 16 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nImpact Presentation: Displays loss trends via \nbar charts and proposes a barcode-scanning \nworkflow to prevent reconciliation errors. \n \nQuality & Safety (ISO/HACCP) Audit: \n●​\nFinding: \"Storage temperatures in Cold Room \nB exceeded the 4°C limit three times during \nthe quarter without documented corrective \nactions.\" \n●​\nImpact Presentation: Highlights regulatory \nnon-compliance \nrisks \nand \npresents \nan \nautomated \nIoT \ntemperature-alerting \nframework. \n \nL6: RISK MANAGEMENT PROCESS \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ RISK IDENTIFICATION \nB.​ RISK ASSESSMENT \nC.​ IMPLEMENTING & MONITORING \n \nA \nRISK IDENTIFICATION \n \nWHAT IS RISK IDENTIFICATION? \n●​\nIt is the process of finding, recognizing, and \ndocumenting potential risks that may affect \na project. \n●​\nSTARTING \nPOINT \nof \nrisk \nmanagement \nprocess. \n●​\n“WHAT \nCOULD \nGO \nWRONG \nWHILE \nDEVELOPING OUR SYSTEM?” \n \nWHY IS RISK IDENTIFICATION IMPORTANT? \n●​\nRecognize potential problems early \n●​\nPrepare appropriate responses \n●​\nReduce the possibility of response issues \n●​\nProtect the project’s schedule, budget and \nobjectives \n●​\nHelp the team prioritize which risks need \nattention \n \nCOMMON RISK IDENTIFICATION TOOLS AND \nTECHNIQUES \n1.​\nBRAINSTORMING \n●​\nTeam members identify risks such \nas lack of resources, technical errors, \nor delays. \n●​\nEXAMPLE: Team members identify \nrisks \nsuch \nas \nlack of resources, \ntechnical errors, or delays. \n \n \n \n2.​\nCHECKLISTS \n●​\nA \nlist \nof \ncommon \nrisks \nfrom \nprevious \nprojects \nor established \nstandards is used to identify possible \nrisks. \n●​\nEXAMPLE: \nA \nsoftware \nproject \nchecklist \nmay \ninclude \nsecurity \nproblems, data loss, software bugs, \nand system downtime. \n3.​\nSWOT ANALYSIS \n●​\nSWOT examines: \n○​\nS – Strengths \n○​\nW – Weaknesses \n○​\nO – Opportunities \n○​\nT – Threats \n4.​ EXPERT INTERVIEWS \n●​\nPeople with specialized knowledge \nor \nexperience \nare \nasked \nabout \npossible risks. \n●​\nEXAMPLE: \nA cybersecurity expert \nmay identify security vulnerabilities \nthat the project team might overlook. \n5.​\nDOCUMENTATION REVIEW \n●​\nExisting \nproject \ndocuments, \ncontracts, schedules, requirements, \nand reports are reviewed to find \npotential risks. \n6.​\nHISTORICAL INFORMATION ","lineStart":2701,"lineEnd":3300,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822517019,"end":1789822517044}},"id":"prt_0b9bc3e07001Z1iE1oFAJvQ6JP","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc3869001WdpnmE1FKMan29"}}
