{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822518655,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ea04f124dc16443fa1e686ea","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":600,"offset":3301},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n3301: ●​\n3302: The \n3303: team \n3304: examines \n3305: risks \n3306: from \n3307: previous \n3308: or \n3309: similar \n3310: projects \n3311: to \n3312: determine which risks might occur \n3313: again. \n3314:  \n3315: MAIN OUTPUT: RISK REGISTER \n3316: ●​\n3317: A risk register is a document that records the \n3318: risks identified throughout the project. \n3319:  \n3320: THE RISK REGISTER \n3321: Project Name: Common Project Risks \n3322:  \n3323:  \n3324: PROCESS OF IDENTIFICATION \n3325: ●​\n3326: Identify \n3327: ●​\n3328: Document \n3329: ●​\n3330: Record in Risk Register \n3331: ●​\n3332: Proceed to Risk Assessment \n3333:  \n3334:  \n3335:  \n3336: PAGE 17 \n3337: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n3338:  \n3339: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n3340: ITAS311-T \n3341: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n3342: \f \n3343:  \n3344: B \n3345: RISK ASSESSMENT \n3346:  \n3347: WHAT IS RISK ASSESSMENT? \n3348: ●​\n3349: Process \n3350: of \n3351: identifying, \n3352: analyzing, \n3353: and \n3354: evaluating \n3355: risks \n3356: to \n3357: determine \n3358: their \n3359: significance \n3360: ●​\n3361: Helps organizations decide which risks need \n3362: treatment and in what order \n3363: ●​\n3364: Core output: a prioritized understanding of \n3365: risk exposure \n3366: ●​\n3367: Foundation \n3368: for \n3369: risk response planning \n3370: (accept, mitigate, transfer, avoid) \n3371:  \n3372: TWO APPROACHES TO RISK ASSESSMENT \n3373: ●​\n3374: Qualitative Risk Assessment \n3375: ○​\n3376: Uses descriptive, judgment-based \n3377: ratings like Low/Medium/High rather \n3378: than hard numbers. It's fast and easy \n3379: to communicate, relying on expert \n3380: experience and team discussion. \n3381: ●​\n3382: Quantitative Risk Assessment \n3383: ○​\n3384: Uses numerical, data-driven values \n3385: such \n3386: as \n3387: dollar \n3388: amounts \n3389: and \n3390: probabilities. It takes more time and \n3391: data \n3392: but \n3393: supports \n3394: precise \n3395: cost-benefit decisions. \n3396: ●​\n3397: Example \n3398: ○​\n3399: A startup gives a new vendor a quick \n3400: \"Medium\" rating in a team discussion \n3401: (qualitative), while a bank calculates a \n3402: $2.3M expected annual loss before \n3403: approving a large loan (quantitative). \n3404:  \n3405: QUALITATIVE RISK ASSESSMENT \n3406: ●​\n3407: Uses descriptive scales (high, medium, low) \n3408: ●​\n3409: Relies on expert judgment and experience \n3410: ●​\n3411: Requires minimal mathematical calculations \n3412: ●​\n3413: Provides quick assessment results \n3414: ●​\n3415: Works well with limited historical data \n3416:  \n3417:  \n3418: QUANTITATIVE RISK ASSESSMENT \n3419: ●​\n3420: Uses numerical values and probabilities \n3421: ●​\n3422: Requires statistical analysis tools \n3423: ●​\n3424: Demands detailed historical data \n3425: ●​\n3426: Produces specific numerical outcomes \n3427: ●​\n3428: Takes longer to complete \n3429:  \n3430:  \n3431:  \n3432: LIKELIHOOD AND IMPACT ANALYSIS \n3433: ●​\n3434: Likelihood:  \n3435: ○​\n3436: probability a risk event will occur \n3437: ●​\n3438: Impact:  \n3439: ○​\n3440: severity of consequences if it occurs \n3441: ●​\n3442: Both usually rated on a scale (e.g., 1–5 or \n3443: Low/Med/High) \n3444: Risk Level = Likelihood × Impact \n3445:  \n3446:  \n3447: THE RISK MATRIX \n3448: ●​\n3449: Visual grid plotting Likelihood (Y-axis) vs. \n3450: Impact (X-axis) \n3451: ●​\n3452: Cells \n3453: represent \n3454: overall \n3455: risk \n3456: severity \n3457: (color-coded: green/yellow/red) \n3458: ●​\n3459: Helps quickly visualize and communicate \n3460: risk exposure \n3461: ●​\n3462: Simplifies decision-making on where to \n3463: focus resources \n3464:  \n3465:  \n3466:  \n3467:  \n3468:  \n3469:  \n3470: PAGE 18 \n3471: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n3472:  \n3473: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n3474: ITAS311-T \n3475: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n3476: \f \n3477:  \n3478: THE RISK MATRIX \n3479:  \n3480:  \n3481: LIKELIHOOD AND IMPACT ANALYSIS \n3482: ●​\n3483: Rank risks from highest to lowest overall \n3484: severity score \n3485: ●​\n3486: High-severity \n3487: risks \n3488: → \n3489: immediate \n3490: mitigation/action \n3491: ●​\n3492: Low-severity risks → monitor or accept \n3493: ●​\n3494: Ensures resources are allocated efficiently \n3495: to the most critical threats \n3496:  \n3497: C \n3498: IMPLEMENTING & MONITORING \n3499:  \n3500: 4 STEPS OF RISK MANAGEMENT \n3501: 1.​\n3502: Identify Risk \n3503: 2.​\n3504: Assess Risk \n3505: 3.​\n3506: Implement the Risk Response Plan \n3507: 4.​\n3508: Monitor and Control Risk Response \n3509:  \n3510: IMPLEMENT THE RISK RESPONSE PLAN \n3511: ●​\n3512: The process of executing the chosen risk \n3513: response strategies and actions that were \n3514: planned during the risk assessment stage. \n3515: ●​\n3516: Involves \n3517: ○​\n3518: Resource Allocation \n3519: ○​\n3520: Assigning Responsibilities \n3521: ○​\n3522: Setting Timelines \n3523: ○​\n3524: Selecting the Right Tools \n3525:  \n3526: RISK RESPONSE STRATEGIES \n3527: ●​\n3528: Avoid \n3529: ○​\n3530: Eliminate the risk entirely \n3531: ●​\n3532: Mitigate \n3533: ○​\n3534: Reduce the likelihood or impact \n3535: ●​\n3536: Transfer \n3537: ○​\n3538: Shift the risk to a third party \n3539: ●​\n3540: Accept \n3541: ○​\n3542: Acknowledge and monitor without \n3543: action \n3544:  \n3545: THE IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS \n3546: 1.​\n3547: Finalize the chosen response strategy \n3548: 2.​\n3549: Assign roles and responsibilities \n3550: 3.​\n3551: Allocate budget and resources \n3552: 4.​\n3553: Set a timeline / schedule \n3554: 5.​\n3555: Execute the planned actions \n3556: 6.​\n3557: Document the implementation \n3558:  \n3559: SUCCESSFUL IMPLEMENTATION REQUIRES: \n3560: ●​\n3561: Clear ownership \n3562: ○​\n3563: Who is accountable \n3564: ●​\n3565: Adequate resources \n3566: ○​\n3567: budget, tools, manpower \n3568: ●​\n3569: Open communication \n3570: ○​\n3571: stakeholders informed at every stage \n3572:  \n3573: MONITOR AND CONTROL RISK RESPONSE \n3574: ●​\n3575: The ongoing process of tracking identified \n3576: risks, \n3577: reviewing \n3578: the \n3579: effectiveness \n3580: of \n3581: response plans, and identifying new risks as \n3582: they emerge. \n3583: ●​\n3584: Involves \n3585: ○​\n3586: Tracking \n3587: ○​\n3588: Reviewing \n3589: ○​\n3590: Adjusting \n3591: ○​\n3592: Reporting \n3593:  \n3594: MONITORING TECHNIQUES AND TOOLS \n3595: ●​\n3596: Regular risk audits and inspections \n3597: ●​\n3598: Key Risk Indicators (KRIs) and dashboards \n3599: ●​\n3600: Status reports and team check-ins \n3601: ●​\n3602: Feedback loops from stakeholders \n3603:  \n3604: L7: BUSINESS CONTINUITY \n3605: MANAGEMENT \n3606:  \n3607: TOPIC OVERVIEW \n3608: A.​ FOUNDATIONAL DEFINITIONS \n3609: B.​ MAXIMUM TOLERABLE DOWNTIME \n3610: C.​ BUSINESS IMPACT ANALYSIS \n3611: D.​ PLAN REVIEW \n3612: E.​ TESTING THE PLAN \n3613:  \n3614: A \n3615: FOUNDATIONAL DEFINITIONS \n3616:  \n3617: BUSINESS CONTINUITY MANAGEMENT (BCM)  \n3618: ●​\n3619: An organization-wide framework of policies, \n3620: processes, and capabilities that prepare a \n3621: business to prevent, respond to, and recover \n3622: from disruptive incidents  \n3623:  \n3624: BUSINESS CONTINUITY PLAN (BCP) \n3625: ●​\n3626: The documented set of procedures an \n3627: organization follows to keep critical functions \n3628: running during, and immediately after, a \n3629: disruption  \n3630:  \n3631:  \n3632:  \n3633: PAGE 19 \n3634: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n3635:  \n3636: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n3637: ITAS311-T \n3638: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n3639: \f \n3640:  \n3641: DISASTER RECOVERY (DR) \n3642: ●​\n3643: A technical, IT-focused subset of continuity \n3644: planning concerned with restoring systems, \n3645: data, and infrastructure after an outage  \n3646:  \n3647: KEY RECOVERY METRICS \n3648: ●​\n3649: Recovery Time Objective  \n3650: ○​\n3651: The maximum acceptable time to \n3652: restore a business function after a \n3653: disruption occurs.  \n3654: ●​\n3655: Recovery Point Objective  \n3656: ○​\n3657: The maximum acceptable amount \n3658: of data loss, measured as a point in \n3659: time to which data must be restored  \n3660: ●​\n3661: Maximum Tolerable Downtime  \n3662: ○​\n3663: The absolute limit of disruption a \n3664: function \n3665: can \n3666: withstand \n3667: before \n3668: survival of the business is threatened.  \n3669: ●​\n3670: Work Recovery Time  \n3671: ○​\n3672: The time needed after systems are \n3673: restored to verify data and resume \n3674: normal processing  \n3675:  \n3676: MORE ESSENTIAL TERMS  \n3677: ●​\n3678: Incident, Crisis, and Disaster  \n3679: ○​\n3680: Escalating levels of severity: an \n3681: incident is a minor disruption, a crisis \n3682: threatens objectives and reputation, \n3683: and a disaster causes major, often \n3684: prolonged, damage.  \n3685: ●​\n3686: Resilience & Single Point of Failure (SPOF)  \n3687: ○​\n3688: Resilience is the ability to absorb and \n3689: adapt to disruption without losing \n3690: function; \n3691: a \n3692: SPOF \n3693: is \n3694: any \n3695: single \n3696: component whose failure stops the \n3697: entire process  \n3698: ●​\n3699: Continuity of Operations  \n3700: ○​\n3701: The \n3702: sustained \n3703: delivery \n3704: of \n3705: an \n3706: organization's critical services and \n3707: functions throughout a disruption, \n3708: not merely their eventual restoration  \n3709:  \n3710: B \n3711: MAXIMUM TOLERABLE DOWNTIME \n3712:  \n3713: MAXIMUM TOLERABLE DOWNTIME \n3714: ●​\n3715: also called Maximum Tolerable Period of \n3716: Disruption (MTPD) — is the longest period a \n3717: critical \n3718: business \n3719: function \n3720: can \n3721: be \n3722: unavailable \n3723: before \n3724: the \n3725: consequences \n3726: become \n3727: unacceptable, \n3728: and \n3729: potentially \n3730: threaten the survival of the organization itself.  \n3731: ●​\n3732: It is not a target to aim for — it is the outer \n3733: boundary every recovery strategy must stay \n3734: inside  \n3735:  \n3736:  \n3737: ●​\n3738: Why it matters?  \n3739: ○​\n3740: Anchors every other recovery metric \n3741: (RTO, RPO, WRT)  \n3742: ○​\n3743: Differs \n3744: by \n3745: function \n3746: — \n3747: payroll \n3748: tolerates \n3749: more \n3750: downtime \n3751: than \n3752: trading  \n3753: ○​\n3754: Drives investment priorities and \n3755: recovery strategy design  \n3756:  \n3757: WHAT DRIVES MTD? \n3758: ●​\n3759: Financial Impact  \n3760: ○​\n3761: Lost revenue, contractual penalties, \n3762: and \n3763: cash-flow \n3764: strain \n3765: that \n3766: accumulate the longer a function \n3767: stays down  \n3768: ●​\n3769: Reputational Damage  \n3770: ○​\n3771: Erosion of customer, investor, and \n3772: media trust — damage that often \n3773: outlasts the outage itself  \n3774: ●​\n3775: Regulatory & Legal Obligations  \n3776: ○​\n3777: Compliance deadlines and statutory \n3778: duties that create hard limits on \n3779: acceptable disruption  \n3780: ●​\n3781: Operational Dependencies  \n3782: ○​\n3783: Downstream \n3784: processes \n3785: and \n3786: contractual SLAs with partners that \n3787: fail once this function is unavailable \n3788: too long  \n3789:  \n3790: THE COST OF DOWNTIME ESCALATES OVER TIME \n3791: ●​\n3792: Impact rarely rises in a straight line — it \n3793: accelerates. Early hours may be absorbed by \n3794: buffers and goodwill; each additional day \n3795: compounds financial, legal, and reputational \n3796: cost until it approaches the MTD ceiling  \n3797: ●​\n3798: MTD marks the point beyond which the \n3799: curve \n3800: becomes \n3801: unacceptable \n3802: — \n3803: by \n3804: definition, not by degree  \n3805:  \n3806: HOW RTO, RPO & WRT FIT WITHIN MTD \n3807:  \n3808: ●​\n3809: RPO looks backward from the disruption to \n3810: set how much data loss is tolerable. RTO \n3811: measures the recovery effort itself. WRT \n3812: covers \n3813: verification \n3814: before \n3815: normal \n3816: work \n3817: resumes. Together, RPO + RTO + WRT must fit \n3818: comfortably inside the MTD boundary  \n3819:  \n3820: STEPS TO DETERMINE ACCEPTABLE DOWNTIME \n3821: 1.​\n3822: Identify Critical Processes  \n3823: -​\n3824: List the functions the organization \n3825: cannot operate without  \n3826: 2.​\n3827: Consult Stakeholders & Regulators  \n3828: -​\n3829: Gather input from process owners, \n3830: customers, and compliance bodies  \n3831:  \n3832: PAGE 20 \n3833: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n3834:  \n3835: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n3836: ITAS311-T \n3837: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n3838: \f \n3839:  \n3840: 3.​\n3841: Quantify Impact Over Time  \n3842: -​\n3843: Model \n3844: how \n3845: financial, legal, and \n3846: reputational harm grows hour by \n3847: hour  \n3848: 4.​ Set the MTD Threshold  \n3849: -​\n3850: Agree \n3851: the \n3852: point \n3853: beyond \n3854: which \n3855: impact becomes unacceptable  \n3856: 5.​\n3857: Derive RTO & RPO Targets  \n3858: -​\n3859: Work \n3860: backward \n3861: to \n3862: set recovery \n3863: objectives safely inside that ceiling  \n3864:  \n3865: C \n3866: BUSINESS IMPACT ANALYSIS \n3867:  \n3868: WHAT IS BUSINESS IMPACT ANALYSIS \n3869: ●​\n3870: A process to identify and evaluate the \n3871: effects of a disruption to critical business \n3872: operations.  \n3873: ●​\n3874: Key foundation of Business Continuity and \n3875: Disaster Recovery Planning.  \n3876: ●​\n3877: Answers: \"What happens to the business — \n3878: financially, \n3879: operationally, \n3880: legally, \n3881: and \n3882: reputationally \n3883: — \n3884: if \n3885: this \n3886: process \n3887: stops \n3888: working?\"  \n3889: ●​\n3890: Different from Risk Assessment: Risk asks \n3891: \"what could go wrong?\" while BIA asks \n3892: \"what's the impact, and how quickly must we \n3893: recover?\"  \n3894:  \n3895: WHY IT MATTERS?  \n3896: ●​\n3897: Identifies \n3898: mission-critical \n3899: functions \n3900: vs. \n\n(Showing lines 3301-3900 of 5328. Use offset=3901 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"●​\nThe \nteam \nexamines \nrisks \nfrom \nprevious \nor \nsimilar \nprojects \nto \ndetermine which risks might occur \nagain. \n \nMAIN OUTPUT: RISK REGISTER \n●​\nA risk register is a document that records the \nrisks identified throughout the project. \n \nTHE RISK REGISTER ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":"●​\nThe \nteam \nexamines \nrisks \nfrom \nprevious \nor \nsimilar \nprojects \nto \ndetermine which risks might occur \nagain. \n \nMAIN OUTPUT: RISK REGISTER \n●​\nA risk register is a document that records the \nrisks identified throughout the project. \n \nTHE RISK REGISTER \nProject Name: Common Project Risks \n \n \nPROCESS OF IDENTIFICATION \n●​\nIdentify \n●​\nDocument \n●​\nRecord in Risk Register \n●​\nProceed to Risk Assessment \n \n \n \nPAGE 17 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nB \nRISK ASSESSMENT \n \nWHAT IS RISK ASSESSMENT? \n●​\nProcess \nof \nidentifying, \nanalyzing, \nand \nevaluating \nrisks \nto \ndetermine \ntheir \nsignificance \n●​\nHelps organizations decide which risks need \ntreatment and in what order \n●​\nCore output: a prioritized understanding of \nrisk exposure \n●​\nFoundation \nfor \nrisk response planning \n(accept, mitigate, transfer, avoid) \n \nTWO APPROACHES TO RISK ASSESSMENT \n●​\nQualitative Risk Assessment \n○​\nUses descriptive, judgment-based \nratings like Low/Medium/High rather \nthan hard numbers. It's fast and easy \nto communicate, relying on expert \nexperience and team discussion. \n●​\nQuantitative Risk Assessment \n○​\nUses numerical, data-driven values \nsuch \nas \ndollar \namounts \nand \nprobabilities. It takes more time and \ndata \nbut \nsupports \nprecise \ncost-benefit decisions. \n●​\nExample \n○​\nA startup gives a new vendor a quick \n\"Medium\" rating in a team discussion \n(qualitative), while a bank calculates a \n$2.3M expected annual loss before \napproving a large loan (quantitative). \n \nQUALITATIVE RISK ASSESSMENT \n●​\nUses descriptive scales (high, medium, low) \n●​\nRelies on expert judgment and experience \n●​\nRequires minimal mathematical calculations \n●​\nProvides quick assessment results \n●​\nWorks well with limited historical data \n \n \nQUANTITATIVE RISK ASSESSMENT \n●​\nUses numerical values and probabilities \n●​\nRequires statistical analysis tools \n●​\nDemands detailed historical data \n●​\nProduces specific numerical outcomes \n●​\nTakes longer to complete \n \n \n \nLIKELIHOOD AND IMPACT ANALYSIS \n●​\nLikelihood:  \n○​\nprobability a risk event will occur \n●​\nImpact:  \n○​\nseverity of consequences if it occurs \n●​\nBoth usually rated on a scale (e.g., 1–5 or \nLow/Med/High) \nRisk Level = Likelihood × Impact \n \n \nTHE RISK MATRIX \n●​\nVisual grid plotting Likelihood (Y-axis) vs. \nImpact (X-axis) \n●​\nCells \nrepresent \noverall \nrisk \nseverity \n(color-coded: green/yellow/red) \n●​\nHelps quickly visualize and communicate \nrisk exposure \n●​\nSimplifies decision-making on where to \nfocus resources \n \n \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 18 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nTHE RISK MATRIX \n \n \nLIKELIHOOD AND IMPACT ANALYSIS \n●​\nRank risks from highest to lowest overall \nseverity score \n●​\nHigh-severity \nrisks \n→ \nimmediate \nmitigation/action \n●​\nLow-severity risks → monitor or accept \n●​\nEnsures resources are allocated efficiently \nto the most critical threats \n \nC \nIMPLEMENTING & MONITORING \n \n4 STEPS OF RISK MANAGEMENT \n1.​\nIdentify Risk \n2.​\nAssess Risk \n3.​\nImplement the Risk Response Plan \n4.​\nMonitor and Control Risk Response \n \nIMPLEMENT THE RISK RESPONSE PLAN \n●​\nThe process of executing the chosen risk \nresponse strategies and actions that were \nplanned during the risk assessment stage. \n●​\nInvolves \n○​\nResource Allocation \n○​\nAssigning Responsibilities \n○​\nSetting Timelines \n○​\nSelecting the Right Tools \n \nRISK RESPONSE STRATEGIES \n●​\nAvoid \n○​\nEliminate the risk entirely \n●​\nMitigate \n○​\nReduce the likelihood or impact \n●​\nTransfer \n○​\nShift the risk to a third party \n●​\nAccept \n○​\nAcknowledge and monitor without \naction \n \nTHE IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS \n1.​\nFinalize the chosen response strategy \n2.​\nAssign roles and responsibilities \n3.​\nAllocate budget and resources \n4.​\nSet a timeline / schedule \n5.​\nExecute the planned actions \n6.​\nDocument the implementation \n \nSUCCESSFUL IMPLEMENTATION REQUIRES: \n●​\nClear ownership \n○​\nWho is accountable \n●​\nAdequate resources \n○​\nbudget, tools, manpower \n●​\nOpen communication \n○​\nstakeholders informed at every stage \n \nMONITOR AND CONTROL RISK RESPONSE \n●​\nThe ongoing process of tracking identified \nrisks, \nreviewing \nthe \neffectiveness \nof \nresponse plans, and identifying new risks as \nthey emerge. \n●​\nInvolves \n○​\nTracking \n○​\nReviewing \n○​\nAdjusting \n○​\nReporting \n \nMONITORING TECHNIQUES AND TOOLS \n●​\nRegular risk audits and inspections \n●​\nKey Risk Indicators (KRIs) and dashboards \n●​\nStatus reports and team check-ins \n●​\nFeedback loops from stakeholders \n \nL7: BUSINESS CONTINUITY \nMANAGEMENT \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ FOUNDATIONAL DEFINITIONS \nB.​ MAXIMUM TOLERABLE DOWNTIME \nC.​ BUSINESS IMPACT ANALYSIS \nD.​ PLAN REVIEW \nE.​ TESTING THE PLAN \n \nA \nFOUNDATIONAL DEFINITIONS \n \nBUSINESS CONTINUITY MANAGEMENT (BCM)  \n●​\nAn organization-wide framework of policies, \nprocesses, and capabilities that prepare a \nbusiness to prevent, respond to, and recover \nfrom disruptive incidents  \n \nBUSINESS CONTINUITY PLAN (BCP) \n●​\nThe documented set of procedures an \norganization follows to keep critical functions \nrunning during, and immediately after, a \ndisruption  \n \n \n \nPAGE 19 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nDISASTER RECOVERY (DR) \n●​\nA technical, IT-focused subset of continuity \nplanning concerned with restoring systems, \ndata, and infrastructure after an outage  \n \nKEY RECOVERY METRICS \n●​\nRecovery Time Objective  \n○​\nThe maximum acceptable time to \nrestore a business function after a \ndisruption occurs.  \n●​\nRecovery Point Objective  \n○​\nThe maximum acceptable amount \nof data loss, measured as a point in \ntime to which data must be restored  \n●​\nMaximum Tolerable Downtime  \n○​\nThe absolute limit of disruption a \nfunction \ncan \nwithstand \nbefore \nsurvival of the business is threatened.  \n●​\nWork Recovery Time  \n○​\nThe time needed after systems are \nrestored to verify data and resume \nnormal processing  \n \nMORE ESSENTIAL TERMS  \n●​\nIncident, Crisis, and Disaster  \n○​\nEscalating levels of severity: an \nincident is a minor disruption, a crisis \nthreatens objectives and reputation, \nand a disaster causes major, often \nprolonged, damage.  \n●​\nResilience & Single Point of Failure (SPOF)  \n○​\nResilience is the ability to absorb and \nadapt to disruption without losing \nfunction; \na \nSPOF \nis \nany \nsingle \ncomponent whose failure stops the \nentire process  \n●​\nContinuity of Operations  \n○​\nThe \nsustained \ndelivery \nof \nan \norganization's critical services and \nfunctions throughout a disruption, \nnot merely their eventual restoration  \n \nB \nMAXIMUM TOLERABLE DOWNTIME \n \nMAXIMUM TOLERABLE DOWNTIME \n●​\nalso called Maximum Tolerable Period of \nDisruption (MTPD) — is the longest period a \ncritical \nbusiness \nfunction \ncan \nbe \nunavailable \nbefore \nthe \nconsequences \nbecome \nunacceptable, \nand \npotentially \nthreaten the survival of the organization itself.  \n●​\nIt is not a target to aim for — it is the outer \nboundary every recovery strategy must stay \ninside  \n \n \n●​\nWhy it matters?  \n○​\nAnchors every other recovery metric \n(RTO, RPO, WRT)  \n○​\nDiffers \nby \nfunction \n— \npayroll \ntolerates \nmore \ndowntime \nthan \ntrading  \n○​\nDrives investment priorities and \nrecovery strategy design  \n \nWHAT DRIVES MTD? \n●​\nFinancial Impact  \n○​\nLost revenue, contractual penalties, \nand \ncash-flow \nstrain \nthat \naccumulate the longer a function \nstays down  \n●​\nReputational Damage  \n○​\nErosion of customer, investor, and \nmedia trust — damage that often \noutlasts the outage itself  \n●​\nRegulatory & Legal Obligations  \n○​\nCompliance deadlines and statutory \nduties that create hard limits on \nacceptable disruption  \n●​\nOperational Dependencies  \n○​\nDownstream \nprocesses \nand \ncontractual SLAs with partners that \nfail once this function is unavailable \ntoo long  \n \nTHE COST OF DOWNTIME ESCALATES OVER TIME \n●​\nImpact rarely rises in a straight line — it \naccelerates. Early hours may be absorbed by \nbuffers and goodwill; each additional day \ncompounds financial, legal, and reputational \ncost until it approaches the MTD ceiling  \n●​\nMTD marks the point beyond which the \ncurve \nbecomes \nunacceptable \n— \nby \ndefinition, not by degree  \n \nHOW RTO, RPO & WRT FIT WITHIN MTD \n \n●​\nRPO looks backward from the disruption to \nset how much data loss is tolerable. RTO \nmeasures the recovery effort itself. WRT \ncovers \nverification \nbefore \nnormal \nwork \nresumes. Together, RPO + RTO + WRT must fit \ncomfortably inside the MTD boundary  \n \nSTEPS TO DETERMINE ACCEPTABLE DOWNTIME \n1.​\nIdentify Critical Processes  \n-​\nList the functions the organization \ncannot operate without  \n2.​\nConsult Stakeholders & Regulators  \n-​\nGather input from process owners, \ncustomers, and compliance bodies  \n \nPAGE 20 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n3.​\nQuantify Impact Over Time  \n-​\nModel \nhow \nfinancial, legal, and \nreputational harm grows hour by \nhour  \n4.​ Set the MTD Threshold  \n-​\nAgree \nthe \npoint \nbeyond \nwhich \nimpact becomes unacceptable  \n5.​\nDerive RTO & RPO Targets  \n-​\nWork \nbackward \nto \nset recovery \nobjectives safely inside that ceiling  \n \nC \nBUSINESS IMPACT ANALYSIS \n \nWHAT IS BUSINESS IMPACT ANALYSIS \n●​\nA process to identify and evaluate the \neffects of a disruption to critical business \noperations.  \n●​\nKey foundation of Business Continuity and \nDisaster Recovery Planning.  \n●​\nAnswers: \"What happens to the business — \nfinancially, \noperationally, \nlegally, \nand \nreputationally \n— \nif \nthis \nprocess \nstops \nworking?\"  \n●​\nDifferent from Risk Assessment: Risk asks \n\"what could go wrong?\" while BIA asks \n\"what's the impact, and how quickly must we \nrecover?\"  \n \nWHY IT MATTERS?  \n●​\nIdentifies \nmission-critical \nfunctions \nvs. ","lineStart":3301,"lineEnd":3900,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822518592,"end":1789822518622}},"id":"prt_0b9bc434d001QAy4at2w3TftHS","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc3f45001Gh4DjEppk3KUGk"}}
