{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822520306,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_0e8f1b9c1bb54192b3de55e1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":700,"offset":3901},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n3901: deferrable ones \n3902: ●​\n3903: Quantifies financial, operational, legal, and \n3904: reputational impact \n3905: ●​\n3906: Justifies budget for recovery investments \n3907: ●​\n3908: Prevents over- or under-investing in the \n3909: wrong priorities \n3910: ●​\n3911: May be required for regulatory compliance \n3912: ●​\n3913: Builds resilience — helps reduce recovery \n3914: time and unnecessary costs \n3915: ●​\n3916: Aligns \n3917: business, \n3918: IT, and leadership on \n3919: priorities \n3920:  \n3921: KEY OBJECTIVES \n3922: ●​\n3923: Identify critical business processes and their \n3924: dependencies — people, systems, data, and \n3925: vendors involved  \n3926: ●​\n3927: Determine how impact escalates over time, \n3928: from \n3929: minor \n3930: disruption \n3931: to \n3932: severe \n3933: or \n3934: unacceptable impact  \n3935: ●​\n3936: Establish \n3937: clear \n3938: recovery \n3939: priorities \n3940: so \n3941: leadership \n3942: knows \n3943: which \n3944: processes \n3945: to \n3946: prioritize  \n3947: ●​\n3948: Identify resource requirements for recovery \n3949: — staffing, technology, facilities, and vendor \n3950: support  \n3951: ●​\n3952: Surface \n3953: hidden \n3954: interdependencies \n3955: and \n3956: single points of failure across departments  \n3957: ●​\n3958: Provide a shared understanding across the \n3959: organization of what \"critical\" actually means  \n3960:  \n3961: CORE METRICS \n3962: ●​\n3963: RTO (Recovery Time Objective)  \n3964: ○​\n3965: maximum \n3966: acceptable \n3967: time \n3968: a \n3969: system/process can be unavailable \n3970: before it must be restored  \n3971: ●​\n3972: MTD (Maximum Tolerable Downtime)  \n3973: ○​\n3974: maximum time a process can be \n3975: unavailable \n3976: before \n3977: the \n3978: impact \n3979: becomes unacceptable  \n3980: ●​\n3981: RPO (Recovery Point Objective)  \n3982: ○​\n3983: maximum acceptable amount of \n3984: data loss, measured in time  \n3985: ●​\n3986: MBCO \n3987: (Minimum \n3988: Business \n3989: Continuity \n3990: Objective)  \n3991: ○​\n3992: minimum service level the business \n3993: must maintain during a disruption  \n3994:  \n3995: THE BIA PROCESS — STEP BY STEP  \n3996: 1.​\n3997: Define \n3998: scope \n3999: — \n4000: which \n4001: departments, \n4002: processes, and locations are covered  \n4003: 2.​\n4004: Identify critical business functions through \n4005: interviews, \n4006: surveys, \n4007: and workshops with \n4008: process owners  \n4009: 3.​\n4010: Map dependencies — people, technology, \n4011: facilities, data, and third party vendors  \n4012: 4.​\n4013: Assess impact at 7 increasing time intervals \n4014: across \n4015: financial, \n4016: operational, \n4017: legal, \n4018: and \n4019: reputational categories  \n4020: 5.​\n4021: Assign RTO, RPO, and MTD for each critical \n4022: process  \n4023: 6.​\n4024: Prioritize functions for recovery based on \n4025: severity and time sensitivity  \n4026: 7.​\n4027: Document \n4028: findings \n4029: and \n4030: validate \n4031: with \n4032: stakeholders; feed results into BCP/DRP  \n4033:  \n4034: TYPES OF IMPACT ASSESSED  \n4035: ●​\n4036: Financial — lost revenue, increased costs, \n4037: penalties \n4038: ●​\n4039: Operational — halted production, service \n4040: failures, lost productivity \n4041: ●​\n4042: Legal/Regulatory — compliance breaches, \n4043: fines, legal obligations \n4044: ●​\n4045: Reputational — loss of trust, negative media, \n4046: brand damage \n4047: ●​\n4048: Contractual \n4049: — \n4050: SLA \n4051: breaches, \n4052: penalties, \n4053: contract termination \n4054: ●​\n4055: Strategic \n4056: — \n4057: delayed \n4058: launches, \n4059: missed \n4060: opportunities, competitive disadvantage \n4061:  \n4062:  \n4063:  \n4064:  \n4065:  \n4066: PAGE 21 \n4067: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n4068:  \n4069: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n4070: ITAS311-T \n4071: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n4072: \f \n4073:  \n4074:  \n4075: KEY TAKEAWAYS  \n4076: ●​\n4077: BIA \n4078: turns \n4079: \"what \n4080: if\" \n4081: scenarios \n4082: into \n4083: measurable, \n4084: prioritized, \n4085: and \n4086: actionable \n4087: information  \n4088: ●​\n4089: Provides \n4090: an \n4091: analytical \n4092: foundation \n4093: for \n4094: effective Business Continuity and Disaster \n4095: Recovery planning  \n4096: ●​\n4097: Helps allocate limited recovery resources to \n4098: the areas that matter most  \n4099: ●​\n4100: Not a one-time exercise — it should be \n4101: reviewed regularly as processes and priorities \n4102: change  \n4103: ●​\n4104: A \n4105: strong \n4106: BIA \n4107: helps reduce downtime, \n4108: financial loss, and reputational harm during \n4109: disruptions  \n4110:  \n4111: COMMON TOOLS & TECHNIQUES  \n4112: ●​\n4113: Structured \n4114: interviews \n4115: & \n4116: workshops \n4117: — \n4118: gather detailed information from department \n4119: heads and process owners  \n4120: ●​\n4121: BIA \n4122: questionnaires/surveys \n4123: — \n4124: collect \n4125: consistent \n4126: information \n4127: across \n4128: different \n4129: business units  \n4130: ●​\n4131: Dependency \n4132: mapping \n4133: — visualize how \n4134: processes, systems, people, and vendors \n4135: depend on each other  \n4136: ●​\n4137: Impact-over-time analysis — show how the \n4138: impact of a disruption increases over time  \n4139: ●​\n4140: Criticality ranking matrices — compare \n4141: processes and determine which should be \n4142: prioritized  \n4143: ●​\n4144: BCM software — standardize and manage \n4145: BIA \n4146: activities, \n4147: especially \n4148: in \n4149: larger \n4150: organizations  \n4151:  \n4152: D \n4153: PLAN REVIEW \n4154:  \n4155: WHY REVIEW THE PLAN?  \n4156: ●​\n4157: A continuity plan is only accurate the day it \n4158: is written. Organizations change, staff turn \n4159: over, systems evolve, and new threats \n4160: emerge — without regular review, the plan \n4161: quietly drifts out of step with reality  \n4162: ●​\n4163: PEOPLE CHANGE - Roles, contacts, and \n4164: responsibilities move on \n4165: ●​\n4166: SYSTEMS CHANGE - New platforms replace \n4167: what describes  \n4168: ●​\n4169: RISK CHANGE  - New threats emerge that \n4170: earlier plans never considered  \n4171:  \n4172: WHEN TO TRIGGER A REVIEW  \n4173: ●​\n4174: SCHEDULED INTERVAL  \n4175: ○​\n4176: A baseline review, typically annual, \n4177: regardless of whether anything else \n4178: has changed  \n4179: ●​\n4180: ORGANIZATIONAL \n4181: OR \n4182: TECHNOLOGY \n4183: CHANGE  \n4184: ○​\n4185: Restructures, \n4186: mergers, \n4187: new \n4188: systems, or office moves that alter \n4189: dependencies  \n4190: ●​\n4191: AFTER AN INCIDENT OR AUDIT FINDING  \n4192: ○​\n4193: Real \n4194: events \n4195: and \n4196: independent \n4197: findings \n4198: are \n4199: the \n4200: most \n4201: reliable \n4202: evidence a plan needs updating  \n4203:  \n4204: THE REVIEW PROCESS  \n4205: 1.​\n4206: Confirm Scope & Ownership  \n4207: -​\n4208: Check \n4209: that \n4210: named \n4211: owners \n4212: and \n4213: covered functions are still valid.  \n4214: 2.​\n4215: Validate Contacts & Dependencies  \n4216: -​\n4217: Update contact lists, suppliers, and \n4218: system dependencies.  \n4219: 3.​\n4220: Cross-Check Against the BIA  \n4221: -​\n4222: Ensure recovery priorities still match \n4223: current business impact.  \n4224: 4.​ Update Procedures  \n4225: -​\n4226: Revise \n4227: steps \n4228: to \n4229: reflect \n4230: current \n4231: people, systems, and processes  \n4232: 5.​\n4233: Sign-Off & Version Control  \n4234: -​\n4235: Formally approve and record the \n4236: plan as the current controlled version  \n4237:  \n4238: ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITY  \n4239: ●​\n4240: Senior Management  \n4241: ○​\n4242: Owns the plan overall and formally \n4243: approves it after review.  \n4244: ●​\n4245: BC Manager  \n4246: ○​\n4247: Coordinates the review, schedules \n4248: input, and tracks completion.  \n4249: ●​\n4250: Department Heads  \n4251: ○​\n4252: Validate that local procedures still \n4253: reflect how their teams actually work.  \n4254: ●​\n4255: Internal Audit  \n4256: ○​\n4257: Provides \n4258: independent \n4259: assurance \n4260: that the review was thorough and \n4261: objective.  \n4262:  \n4263: E \n4264: TESTING THE PLAN  \n4265:  \n4266: WHY TEST?  \n4267: ●​\n4268: Validates Assumptions  \n4269: ○​\n4270: Confirms \n4271: that \n4272: the \n4273: plan's \n4274: assumptions about people, systems, \n4275: and timing actually hold up  \n4276: ●​\n4277: Uncovers Gaps  \n4278: ○​\n4279: Surfaces missing steps, outdated \n4280: contacts, and unrealistic timelines \n4281: before a real event does.  \n4282:  \n4283:  \n4284:  \n4285:  \n4286: PAGE 22 \n4287: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n4288:  \n4289: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n4290: ITAS311-T \n4291: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n4292: \f \n4293:  \n4294: ●​\n4295: Trains Staff  \n4296: ○​\n4297: Builds familiarity and confidence so \n4298: people know exactly what to do \n4299: under pressure  \n4300: ●​\n4301: Satisfies Compliance  \n4302: ○​\n4303: Demonstrates \n4304: due \n4305: diligence \n4306: to \n4307: regulators, auditors, and insurers.  \n4308:  \n4309: TYPES OF EXERCISES  \n4310:  \n4311:  \n4312: THE TESTING CYCLE  \n4313: ●​\n4314: Plan - Document procedures and recovery \n4315: objectives  \n4316: ●​\n4317: Test - Exercise the plan under realistic \n4318: conditions  \n4319: ●​\n4320: Review - Results Capture what worked and \n4321: what did not  \n4322: ●​\n4323: Update - Plan Fold lessons learned back into \n4324: the plan  \n4325:  \n4326: KEY TAKEAWAYS  \n4327: ●​\n4328: Shared terminology (RTO, RPO, MTD, WRT) \n4329: keeps \n4330: every \n4331: team \n4332: speaking \n4333: the same \n4334: language. \n4335: ●​\n4336: Maximum Tolerable Downtime is the ceiling \n4337: every recovery target must fit beneath. \n4338: ●​\n4339: A Business Impact Analysis turns disruption \n4340: into numbers, and numbers into priorities. \n4341: ●​\n4342: Plans decay — scheduled and trigger-based \n4343: reviews keep them accurate. \n4344: ●​\n4345: Testing, from tabletop to full interruption, \n4346: proves the plan before a real event does \n4347:  \n4348: L8: BACKING UP DATA AND \n4349: APPLICATION \n4350:  \n4351: TOPIC OVERVIEW \n4352: A.​ TYPES OF BACKUPS \n4353: B.​ PREPARATION \n4354: C.​ IDENTIFICATION \n4355: D.​ THE THREE-PHASE RESPONSE MODEL \n4356: a.​\n4357: Notification \n4358: b.​\n4359: Response \n4360: c.​\n4361: Recovery \n4362: E.​ POST INCIDENT REVIEW \n4363: F.​\n4364: DOCUMENTATION \n4365: G.​ REPORTING \n4366: H.​ SUMMARY \n4367:  \n4368: A \n4369: TYPES OF BACKUPS \n4370:  \n4371: BACKUP \n4372: ●​\n4373: Backing up data means creating copies of \n4374: files and systems so they can be restored if \n4375: the originals are lost, damaged, or corrupted. \n4376: There are several common types of backups, \n4377: each \n4378: with \n4379: different \n4380: tradeoffs in speed, \n4381: storage \n4382: space, \n4383: and \n4384: recovery \n4385: time. \n4386: Understanding \n4387: these \n4388: types \n4389: helps \n4390: organizations choose the right strategy for \n4391: protecting their data. \n4392:  \n4393: 1. FULL BACKUP \n4394: ●​\n4395: slowest to create, fastest to restore, uses the \n4396: most storage  \n4397:  \n4398: 2. INCREMENTAL BACKUP \n4399: ●​\n4400: An incremental backup only copies data that \n4401: has changed since the last backup, whether \n4402: that was a full backup or another incremental \n4403: backup. This makes it much faster and more \n4404: storage-efficient than a full backup, but \n4405: restoring \n4406: data \n4407: can \n4408: take \n4409: longer \n4410: since \n4411: multiple backup sets are needed.  \n4412:  \n4413: 3. DIFFERENTIAL BACKUP \n4414: ●​\n4415: A differential backup copies all data that has \n4416: changed since the last full backup, not since \n4417: the last backup of any kind. Over time, \n4418: differential \n4419: backups \n4420: grow \n4421: larger \n4422: than \n4423: incremental ones, but they are faster to \n4424: restore because only two backup sets are \n4425: needed: the full backup and the latest \n4426: differential.  \n4427:  \n4428: 4. MIRROR BACKUP \n4429: ●​\n4430: A mirror backup creates an exact, real-time \n4431: copy of the source data, similar to a full \n4432: backup but continuously updated. Unlike \n4433: other backup types, a mirror backup does not \n4434: keep older versions of files. If a file is deleted \n4435: from the source, it is also removed from the \n4436: mirror, which can be risky if data loss goes \n4437: unnoticed.  \n4438:  \n4439: COMPARISON OF BACKUP TYPES \n4440: 1.​\n4441: Full backup \n4442: -​\n4443: slowest to create, fastest to restore, \n4444: uses the most storage  \n4445:  \n4446:  \n4447:  \n4448: PAGE 23 \n4449: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n4450:  \n4451: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n4452: ITAS311-T \n4453: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n4454: \f \n4455:  \n4456: 2.​\n4457: Incremental backup  \n4458: -​\n4459: fastest to create, slowest to restore, \n4460: uses the least storage  \n4461: 3.​\n4462: Differential backup  \n4463: -​\n4464: moderate \n4465: creation \n4466: speed, \n4467: faster \n4468: restore than incremental, moderate \n4469: storage use  \n4470: 4.​ Mirror backup  \n4471: -​\n4472: real-time copy, no version history, \n4473: fastest \n4474: access \n4475: but \n4476: no \n4477: rollback \n4478: protection  \n4479:  \n4480: B \n4481: PREPARATION \n4482:  \n4483: OVERVIEW \n4484: ●​\n4485: Preparation lays the foundation for the entire \n4486: incident response process, determining how \n4487: quickly and effectively an organization can \n4488: react once something actually goes wrong.  \n4489: ○​\n4490: Preparation = first phase of Incident \n4491: Handling \n4492: ○​\n4493: Goal: get the organization ready \n4494: before an incident happens \n4495: ○​\n4496: Reduces damage and response time \n4497: when an incident occurs \n4498: ○​\n4499: Involves \n4500: people, \n4501: processes, \n4502: and \n4503: technology readiness \n4504:  \n4505: POLICIES & PLANS \n4506: ●​\n4507: Without a documented policy and response \n4508: plan, teams waste critical time figuring out \n4509: who does what. Preparation removes that \n4510: guesswork before an incident ever occurs. \n4511: ○​\n4512: Establish \n4513: an \n4514: Incident \n4515: Response \n4516: Policy \n4517: ○​\n4518: Develop an Incident Response Plan \n4519: (IRP) \n4520: ○​\n4521: Define \n4522: roles \n4523: and responsibilities \n4524: (Incident Response Team) \n4525: ○​\n4526: Set \n4527: up \n4528: communication \n4529: and \n4530: escalation procedures \n4531:  \n4532: TOOLS & RESOURCES \n4533: ●​\n4534: Having detection tools, an up-to-date asset \n4535: inventory, and a ready forensic kit in place \n4536: beforehand \n4537: means \n4538: the \n4539: team \n4540: can \n4541: act \n4542: immediately instead of scrambling to gather \n4543: resources mid-crisis. \n4544: ○​\n4545: Deploy \n4546: security \n4547: tools \n4548: (IDS/IPS, \n4549: firewalls, antivirus, logging systems) \n4550: ○​\n4551: Maintain \n4552: updated \n4553: hardware/ \n4554: software inventory \n4555: ○​\n4556: Prepare forensic tools and \"jump kit\" \n4557: (forensic drives, cables, checklists) \n4558: ○​\n4559: Ensure backup systems are in place \n4560: and tested \n4561: TRAINING & TESTING \n4562: ●​\n4563: Preparation isn't something done once and \n4564: forgotten, it's an ongoing cycle of training \n4565: staff, running simulated drills, testing the \n4566: plan, and updating it based on what's \n4567: learned. \n4568: ○​\n4569: Conduct regular employee security \n4570: awareness training \n4571: ○​\n4572: Run \n4573: incident \n4574: response \n4575: drills/simulations (tabletop exercises) \n4576: ○​\n4577: Test communication channels and \n4578: contact lists \n4579: ○​\n4580: Review and update the IR plan \n4581: periodically \n4582:  \n4583: C \n4584: IDENTIFICATION \n4585:  \n4586: OVERVIEW \n4587: ●​\n4588: Identification is the stage where the team \n4589: separates true security incidents from false \n4590: alarms, determining the scope and severity \n4591: of what's actually happening. \n4592: ○​\n4593: Identification \n4594: = \n4595: detecting \n4596: and \n4597: confirming that an incident occurred \n4598: ○​\n4599: Determine \n4600: scope, \n\n(Showing lines 3901-4600 of 5328. Use offset=4601 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"deferrable ones \n●​\nQuantifies financial, operational, legal, and \nreputational impact \n●​\nJustifies budget for recovery investments \n●​\nPrevents over- or under-investing in the \nwrong priorities \n●​\nMay be required for regulatory compliance \n●​\nBuilds resilience — helps reduce recovery \ntime and unnecessary costs \n●​\nAligns \nbusiness, \nIT, and leadership on \npriorities \n ","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":"deferrable ones \n●​\nQuantifies financial, operational, legal, and \nreputational impact \n●​\nJustifies budget for recovery investments \n●​\nPrevents over- or under-investing in the \nwrong priorities \n●​\nMay be required for regulatory compliance \n●​\nBuilds resilience — helps reduce recovery \ntime and unnecessary costs \n●​\nAligns \nbusiness, \nIT, and leadership on \npriorities \n \nKEY OBJECTIVES \n●​\nIdentify critical business processes and their \ndependencies — people, systems, data, and \nvendors involved  \n●​\nDetermine how impact escalates over time, \nfrom \nminor \ndisruption \nto \nsevere \nor \nunacceptable impact  \n●​\nEstablish \nclear \nrecovery \npriorities \nso \nleadership \nknows \nwhich \nprocesses \nto \nprioritize  \n●​\nIdentify resource requirements for recovery \n— staffing, technology, facilities, and vendor \nsupport  \n●​\nSurface \nhidden \ninterdependencies \nand \nsingle points of failure across departments  \n●​\nProvide a shared understanding across the \norganization of what \"critical\" actually means  \n \nCORE METRICS \n●​\nRTO (Recovery Time Objective)  \n○​\nmaximum \nacceptable \ntime \na \nsystem/process can be unavailable \nbefore it must be restored  \n●​\nMTD (Maximum Tolerable Downtime)  \n○​\nmaximum time a process can be \nunavailable \nbefore \nthe \nimpact \nbecomes unacceptable  \n●​\nRPO (Recovery Point Objective)  \n○​\nmaximum acceptable amount of \ndata loss, measured in time  \n●​\nMBCO \n(Minimum \nBusiness \nContinuity \nObjective)  \n○​\nminimum service level the business \nmust maintain during a disruption  \n \nTHE BIA PROCESS — STEP BY STEP  \n1.​\nDefine \nscope \n— \nwhich \ndepartments, \nprocesses, and locations are covered  \n2.​\nIdentify critical business functions through \ninterviews, \nsurveys, \nand workshops with \nprocess owners  \n3.​\nMap dependencies — people, technology, \nfacilities, data, and third party vendors  \n4.​\nAssess impact at 7 increasing time intervals \nacross \nfinancial, \noperational, \nlegal, \nand \nreputational categories  \n5.​\nAssign RTO, RPO, and MTD for each critical \nprocess  \n6.​\nPrioritize functions for recovery based on \nseverity and time sensitivity  \n7.​\nDocument \nfindings \nand \nvalidate \nwith \nstakeholders; feed results into BCP/DRP  \n \nTYPES OF IMPACT ASSESSED  \n●​\nFinancial — lost revenue, increased costs, \npenalties \n●​\nOperational — halted production, service \nfailures, lost productivity \n●​\nLegal/Regulatory — compliance breaches, \nfines, legal obligations \n●​\nReputational — loss of trust, negative media, \nbrand damage \n●​\nContractual \n— \nSLA \nbreaches, \npenalties, \ncontract termination \n●​\nStrategic \n— \ndelayed \nlaunches, \nmissed \nopportunities, competitive disadvantage \n \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 21 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n \nKEY TAKEAWAYS  \n●​\nBIA \nturns \n\"what \nif\" \nscenarios \ninto \nmeasurable, \nprioritized, \nand \nactionable \ninformation  \n●​\nProvides \nan \nanalytical \nfoundation \nfor \neffective Business Continuity and Disaster \nRecovery planning  \n●​\nHelps allocate limited recovery resources to \nthe areas that matter most  \n●​\nNot a one-time exercise — it should be \nreviewed regularly as processes and priorities \nchange  \n●​\nA \nstrong \nBIA \nhelps reduce downtime, \nfinancial loss, and reputational harm during \ndisruptions  \n \nCOMMON TOOLS & TECHNIQUES  \n●​\nStructured \ninterviews \n& \nworkshops \n— \ngather detailed information from department \nheads and process owners  \n●​\nBIA \nquestionnaires/surveys \n— \ncollect \nconsistent \ninformation \nacross \ndifferent \nbusiness units  \n●​\nDependency \nmapping \n— visualize how \nprocesses, systems, people, and vendors \ndepend on each other  \n●​\nImpact-over-time analysis — show how the \nimpact of a disruption increases over time  \n●​\nCriticality ranking matrices — compare \nprocesses and determine which should be \nprioritized  \n●​\nBCM software — standardize and manage \nBIA \nactivities, \nespecially \nin \nlarger \norganizations  \n \nD \nPLAN REVIEW \n \nWHY REVIEW THE PLAN?  \n●​\nA continuity plan is only accurate the day it \nis written. Organizations change, staff turn \nover, systems evolve, and new threats \nemerge — without regular review, the plan \nquietly drifts out of step with reality  \n●​\nPEOPLE CHANGE - Roles, contacts, and \nresponsibilities move on \n●​\nSYSTEMS CHANGE - New platforms replace \nwhat describes  \n●​\nRISK CHANGE  - New threats emerge that \nearlier plans never considered  \n \nWHEN TO TRIGGER A REVIEW  \n●​\nSCHEDULED INTERVAL  \n○​\nA baseline review, typically annual, \nregardless of whether anything else \nhas changed  \n●​\nORGANIZATIONAL \nOR \nTECHNOLOGY \nCHANGE  \n○​\nRestructures, \nmergers, \nnew \nsystems, or office moves that alter \ndependencies  \n●​\nAFTER AN INCIDENT OR AUDIT FINDING  \n○​\nReal \nevents \nand \nindependent \nfindings \nare \nthe \nmost \nreliable \nevidence a plan needs updating  \n \nTHE REVIEW PROCESS  \n1.​\nConfirm Scope & Ownership  \n-​\nCheck \nthat \nnamed \nowners \nand \ncovered functions are still valid.  \n2.​\nValidate Contacts & Dependencies  \n-​\nUpdate contact lists, suppliers, and \nsystem dependencies.  \n3.​\nCross-Check Against the BIA  \n-​\nEnsure recovery priorities still match \ncurrent business impact.  \n4.​ Update Procedures  \n-​\nRevise \nsteps \nto \nreflect \ncurrent \npeople, systems, and processes  \n5.​\nSign-Off & Version Control  \n-​\nFormally approve and record the \nplan as the current controlled version  \n \nROLES AND RESPONSIBILITY  \n●​\nSenior Management  \n○​\nOwns the plan overall and formally \napproves it after review.  \n●​\nBC Manager  \n○​\nCoordinates the review, schedules \ninput, and tracks completion.  \n●​\nDepartment Heads  \n○​\nValidate that local procedures still \nreflect how their teams actually work.  \n●​\nInternal Audit  \n○​\nProvides \nindependent \nassurance \nthat the review was thorough and \nobjective.  \n \nE \nTESTING THE PLAN  \n \nWHY TEST?  \n●​\nValidates Assumptions  \n○​\nConfirms \nthat \nthe \nplan's \nassumptions about people, systems, \nand timing actually hold up  \n●​\nUncovers Gaps  \n○​\nSurfaces missing steps, outdated \ncontacts, and unrealistic timelines \nbefore a real event does.  \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 22 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nTrains Staff  \n○​\nBuilds familiarity and confidence so \npeople know exactly what to do \nunder pressure  \n●​\nSatisfies Compliance  \n○​\nDemonstrates \ndue \ndiligence \nto \nregulators, auditors, and insurers.  \n \nTYPES OF EXERCISES  \n \n \nTHE TESTING CYCLE  \n●​\nPlan - Document procedures and recovery \nobjectives  \n●​\nTest - Exercise the plan under realistic \nconditions  \n●​\nReview - Results Capture what worked and \nwhat did not  \n●​\nUpdate - Plan Fold lessons learned back into \nthe plan  \n \nKEY TAKEAWAYS  \n●​\nShared terminology (RTO, RPO, MTD, WRT) \nkeeps \nevery \nteam \nspeaking \nthe same \nlanguage. \n●​\nMaximum Tolerable Downtime is the ceiling \nevery recovery target must fit beneath. \n●​\nA Business Impact Analysis turns disruption \ninto numbers, and numbers into priorities. \n●​\nPlans decay — scheduled and trigger-based \nreviews keep them accurate. \n●​\nTesting, from tabletop to full interruption, \nproves the plan before a real event does \n \nL8: BACKING UP DATA AND \nAPPLICATION \n \nTOPIC OVERVIEW \nA.​ TYPES OF BACKUPS \nB.​ PREPARATION \nC.​ IDENTIFICATION \nD.​ THE THREE-PHASE RESPONSE MODEL \na.​\nNotification \nb.​\nResponse \nc.​\nRecovery \nE.​ POST INCIDENT REVIEW \nF.​\nDOCUMENTATION \nG.​ REPORTING \nH.​ SUMMARY \n \nA \nTYPES OF BACKUPS \n \nBACKUP \n●​\nBacking up data means creating copies of \nfiles and systems so they can be restored if \nthe originals are lost, damaged, or corrupted. \nThere are several common types of backups, \neach \nwith \ndifferent \ntradeoffs in speed, \nstorage \nspace, \nand \nrecovery \ntime. \nUnderstanding \nthese \ntypes \nhelps \norganizations choose the right strategy for \nprotecting their data. \n \n1. FULL BACKUP \n●​\nslowest to create, fastest to restore, uses the \nmost storage  \n \n2. INCREMENTAL BACKUP \n●​\nAn incremental backup only copies data that \nhas changed since the last backup, whether \nthat was a full backup or another incremental \nbackup. This makes it much faster and more \nstorage-efficient than a full backup, but \nrestoring \ndata \ncan \ntake \nlonger \nsince \nmultiple backup sets are needed.  \n \n3. DIFFERENTIAL BACKUP \n●​\nA differential backup copies all data that has \nchanged since the last full backup, not since \nthe last backup of any kind. Over time, \ndifferential \nbackups \ngrow \nlarger \nthan \nincremental ones, but they are faster to \nrestore because only two backup sets are \nneeded: the full backup and the latest \ndifferential.  \n \n4. MIRROR BACKUP \n●​\nA mirror backup creates an exact, real-time \ncopy of the source data, similar to a full \nbackup but continuously updated. Unlike \nother backup types, a mirror backup does not \nkeep older versions of files. If a file is deleted \nfrom the source, it is also removed from the \nmirror, which can be risky if data loss goes \nunnoticed.  \n \nCOMPARISON OF BACKUP TYPES \n1.​\nFull backup \n-​\nslowest to create, fastest to restore, \nuses the most storage  \n \n \n \nPAGE 23 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n2.​\nIncremental backup  \n-​\nfastest to create, slowest to restore, \nuses the least storage  \n3.​\nDifferential backup  \n-​\nmoderate \ncreation \nspeed, \nfaster \nrestore than incremental, moderate \nstorage use  \n4.​ Mirror backup  \n-​\nreal-time copy, no version history, \nfastest \naccess \nbut \nno \nrollback \nprotection  \n \nB \nPREPARATION \n \nOVERVIEW \n●​\nPreparation lays the foundation for the entire \nincident response process, determining how \nquickly and effectively an organization can \nreact once something actually goes wrong.  \n○​\nPreparation = first phase of Incident \nHandling \n○​\nGoal: get the organization ready \nbefore an incident happens \n○​\nReduces damage and response time \nwhen an incident occurs \n○​\nInvolves \npeople, \nprocesses, \nand \ntechnology readiness \n \nPOLICIES & PLANS \n●​\nWithout a documented policy and response \nplan, teams waste critical time figuring out \nwho does what. Preparation removes that \nguesswork before an incident ever occurs. \n○​\nEstablish \nan \nIncident \nResponse \nPolicy \n○​\nDevelop an Incident Response Plan \n(IRP) \n○​\nDefine \nroles \nand responsibilities \n(Incident Response Team) \n○​\nSet \nup \ncommunication \nand \nescalation procedures \n \nTOOLS & RESOURCES \n●​\nHaving detection tools, an up-to-date asset \ninventory, and a ready forensic kit in place \nbeforehand \nmeans \nthe \nteam \ncan \nact \nimmediately instead of scrambling to gather \nresources mid-crisis. \n○​\nDeploy \nsecurity \ntools \n(IDS/IPS, \nfirewalls, antivirus, logging systems) \n○​\nMaintain \nupdated \nhardware/ \nsoftware inventory \n○​\nPrepare forensic tools and \"jump kit\" \n(forensic drives, cables, checklists) \n○​\nEnsure backup systems are in place \nand tested \nTRAINING & TESTING \n●​\nPreparation isn't something done once and \nforgotten, it's an ongoing cycle of training \nstaff, running simulated drills, testing the \nplan, and updating it based on what's \nlearned. \n○​\nConduct regular employee security \nawareness training \n○​\nRun \nincident \nresponse \ndrills/simulations (tabletop exercises) \n○​\nTest communication channels and \ncontact lists \n○​\nReview and update the IR plan \nperiodically \n \nC \nIDENTIFICATION \n \nOVERVIEW \n●​\nIdentification is the stage where the team \nseparates true security incidents from false \nalarms, determining the scope and severity \nof what's actually happening. \n○​\nIdentification \n= \ndetecting \nand \nconfirming that an incident occurred \n○​\nDetermine \nscope, ","lineStart":3901,"lineEnd":4600,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":true}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822520201,"end":1789822520303}},"id":"prt_0b9bc4a51001OMLwwYD3F7MUzR","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc457b001ExxYE25gzhrHVf"}}
