{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789822521911,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"id":"prt_0b9bc52330015DZehY96PrOB4m","messageID":"msg_0b9bc4c68001OGCjgNYxuHqoq5","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822522482,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e15c9fd39e6b42fc8ec700e2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","limit":800,"offset":4601},"output":"<path>C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n4601: nature, \n4602: and \n4603: severity of the event \n4604: ○​\n4605: Distinguish a true incident from a \n4606: false alarm \n4607: ○​\n4608: Early, accurate identification limits \n4609: damage \n4610:  \n4611: DETECTION SOURCES \n4612: ●​\n4613: Signs of an incident can come from many \n4614: directions at once: automated alerts, log \n4615: data, employee reports, or simply unusual \n4616: behavior in system performance. \n4617: ○​\n4618: Alerts from IDS/IPS, antivirus, and \n4619: SIEM tools \n4620: ○​\n4621: Log \n4622: analysis \n4623: (system, \n4624: network, \n4625: application logs) \n4626: ○​\n4627: Reports from employees or end users \n4628: ○​\n4629: Anomalies \n4630: in \n4631: system/network \n4632: performance \n4633:  \n4634: VERIFICATION STEPS \n4635: ●​\n4636: Before any response begins, the team must \n4637: confirm the event is real, collect initial \n4638: evidence, classify how serious it is, and \n4639: document everything as it unfolds. \n4640: ○​\n4641: Confirm the event is a real security \n4642: incident \n4643: ○​\n4644: Gather initial evidence (timestamps, \n4645: affected systems, indicators) \n4646: ○​\n4647: Classify \n4648: the \n4649: incident \n4650: type \n4651: and \n4652: severity level \n4653:  \n4654: PAGE 24 \n4655: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n4656:  \n4657: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n4658: ITAS311-T \n4659: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n4660: \f \n4661:  \n4662: ○​\n4663: Document \n4664: findings \n4665: as \n4666: they're \n4667: discovered (chain of custody) \n4668:  \n4669: NEXT ACTIONS \n4670: ●​\n4671: Once an incident is confirmed, the team \n4672: notifies \n4673: the \n4674: right \n4675: people, \n4676: assigns \n4677: ownership, and preserves evidence. Setting \n4678: the stage for the response to move into \n4679: containment. \n4680: ○​\n4681: Notify the Incident Response Team \n4682: ○​\n4683: Assign an incident handler/owner \n4684: ○​\n4685: Preserve \n4686: evidence \n4687: for \n4688: further \n4689: analysis \n4690: ○​\n4691: Move to the Containment phase \n4692: once confirmed \n4693:  \n4694: D \n4695: THE THREE-PHASE RESPONSE MODEL \n4696:  \n4697: THE THREE-PHASE RESPONSE MODEL \n4698: ●​\n4699: Once \n4700: an \n4701: incident \n4702: is \n4703: detected, \n4704: three \n4705: coordinated phases determine how well an \n4706: organization survives it. \n4707: ●​\n4708: Notification \n4709: ○​\n4710: Alert the right people, at the right \n4711: time, through the right channels. \n4712: ●​\n4713: Response \n4714: ○​\n4715: Contain \n4716: the \n4717: incident, \n4718: stop \n4719: the \n4720: damage, and eliminate its root cause. \n4721: ●​\n4722: Recovery \n4723: ○​\n4724: Restore systems and data, verify \n4725: integrity, and resume operations. \n4726:  \n4727: D1 \n4728: NOTIFICATION \n4729:  \n4730: WHO SHOULD BE INFORMED? \n4731: Notification lists should be prepared before an \n4732: incident occurs, not written from scratch during one. \n4733: ●​\n4734: Internal IT & Security Teams \n4735: ○​\n4736: The incident response team, system \n4737: administrators, and network/security \n4738: staff who will investigate and act. \n4739: ●​\n4740: Management & Executives \n4741: ○​\n4742: Department \n4743: heads \n4744: and \n4745: senior \n4746: leadership \n4747: who \n4748: need \n4749: situational \n4750: awareness and must approve major \n4751: decisions. \n4752: ●​\n4753: Legal & Compliance Officers \n4754: ○​\n4755: To assess regulatory obligations, \n4756: liability \n4757: exposure, \n4758: and mandatory \n4759: disclosure requirements. \n4760: ●​\n4761: Affected Users & Customers \n4762: ○​\n4763: Anyone whose data, accounts, or \n4764: services were impacted, so they can \n4765: protect themselves. \n4766:  \n4767: ●​\n4768: Law Enforcement & Regulators \n4769: ○​\n4770: Required for certain incidents (e.g., \n4771: data breaches, fraud) under law or \n4772: industry regulation. \n4773:  \n4774: REPORTING & THE VALUE OF SPEED \n4775: ●​\n4776: Reporting the Incident \n4777: 1.​\n4778: Standardized Reports \n4779: -​\n4780: Use a structured report to \n4781: detail what happened, when, \n4782: affected systems, and initial \n4783: impact. \n4784: 2.​\n4785: Defined Communication \n4786: -​\n4787: Follow \n4788: pre-defined \n4789: communication \n4790: chains \n4791: to \n4792: reach the right people first \n4793: time. \n4794: 3.​\n4795: Audit Trail Logging \n4796: -​\n4797: Log every single step with \n4798: precise \n4799: timestamps \n4800: and \n4801: actions taken for later review. \n4802: 4.​ Factual Accuracy \n4803: -​\n4804: Keep reports strictly factual \n4805: and \n4806: updated \n4807: as \n4808: new \n4809: information emerges; avoid \n4810: speculation. \n4811: ●​\n4812: Why Timely Notification Matters \n4813: 1.​\n4814: Limits the Damage \n4815: -​\n4816: Early warning lets security \n4817: teams \n4818: quickly \n4819: isolate \n4820: the \n4821: threat \n4822: before \n4823: it \n4824: spreads \n4825: further. \n4826: 2.​\n4827: Meets Legal Deadlines \n4828: -​\n4829: Many \n4830: strict \n4831: breach-notification laws set \n4832: clear windows (e.g., 72 hours) \n4833: for compliance. \n4834: 3.​\n4835: Preserves Vital Trust \n4836: -​\n4837: Prompt, \n4838: honest \n4839: disclosure \n4840: protects \n4841: organizational \n4842: reputation more than delayed \n4843: info. \n4844: 4.​ Speeds Up Recovery \n4845: -​\n4846: The \n4847: sooner \n4848: the \n4849: right \n4850: stakeholders \n4851: are engaged, \n4852: the faster the recovery phase \n4853: can begin. \n4854:  \n4855: D2 \n4856: RESPONSE \n4857:  \n4858: CONTAINING THE INCIDENT \n4859: Containment stops an incident from spreading \n4860: while preserving evidence for investigation. \n4861:  \n4862:  \n4863:  \n4864:  \n4865: PAGE 25 \n4866: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n4867:  \n4868: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n4869: ITAS311-T \n4870: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n4871: \f \n4872:  \n4873: ●​\n4874: Isolate affected systems \n4875: ○​\n4876: Disconnect compromised devices \n4877: or network segments to stop lateral \n4878: movement. \n4879: ●​\n4880: Disable compromised accounts \n4881: ○​\n4882: Suspend or reset credentials that \n4883: may have been exploited by the \n4884: attacker. \n4885: ●​\n4886: Apply short-term fixes \n4887: ○​\n4888: Block malicious IPs, close exploited \n4889: ports, or apply emergency firewall \n4890: rules. \n4891: ●​\n4892: Preserve evidence \n4893: ○​\n4894: Capture logs, memory dumps, and \n4895: system states before making further \n4896: changes. \n4897:  \n4898: PREVENTING DAMAGE & REMOVING THE CAUSE \n4899: ●​\n4900: Preventing Further Damage \n4901: 1.​\n4902: Continuous Monitoring \n4903: -​\n4904: Monitor systems continuously \n4905: for \n4906: signs \n4907: the \n4908: incident \n4909: is \n4910: spreading or recurring. \n4911: 2.​\n4912: Patch Vulnerabilities \n4913: -​\n4914: Patch related vulnerabilities \n4915: elsewhere in the environment \n4916: before attackers exploit them. \n4917: 3.​\n4918: Segment Networks \n4919: -​\n4920: Strengthen access controls \n4921: and segment networks to \n4922: limit any future blast radius. \n4923: 4.​ Staff Communication \n4924: -​\n4925: Communicate interim safety \n4926: measures to staff and users \n4927: while remediation continues. \n4928: ●​\n4929: Removing the Cause \n4930: 1.​\n4931: Root Cause Identification \n4932: -​\n4933: Identify \n4934: the \n4935: root \n4936: cause: \n4937: malware, \n4938: misconfiguration, \n4939: credentials, \n4940: or \n4941: unpatched \n4942: flaw. \n4943: 2.​\n4944: Malware Eradication \n4945: -​\n4946: Eradicate \n4947: malicious \n4948: code, \n4949: backdoors, and unauthorized \n4950: accounts left behind. \n4951: 3.​\n4952: Close Vulnerabilities \n4953: -​\n4954: Close \n4955: the \n4956: specific \n4957: vulnerability that allowed the \n4958: incident to happen in the first \n4959: place. \n4960: 4.​ Eradication Verification \n4961: -​\n4962: Verify eradication with scans \n4963: and audits before declaring \n4964: the environment clean. \n4965:  \n4966:  \n4967: D3 \n4968: RECOVERY \n4969:  \n4970: RESTORING SYSTEMS & DATA FROM BACKUPS  \n4971: Recovery brings the business back online using \n4972: clean, verified backups — never data that may still \n4973: be compromised. \n4974: ●​\n4975: Identify a Clean Restore Point \n4976: ○​\n4977: Choose a backup taken before the \n4978: incident occurred, confirmed free of \n4979: malware or tampering. \n4980: ●​\n4981: Rebuild on Trusted Media \n4982: ○​\n4983: Reinstall \n4984: systems \n4985: from \n4986: verified, \n4987: uninfected \n4988: sources \n4989: rather \n4990: than \n4991: restoring over a compromised host. \n4992: ●​\n4993: Restore in Priority Order \n4994: ○​\n4995: Bring back critical business systems \n4996: first, based on the organization's \n4997: recovery priorities. \n4998:  \n4999: TESTING SYSTEMS & RETURNING TO NORMAL \n5000: ●​\n5001: Testing if Systems Work Properly \n5002: 1.​\n5003: Verify Functionality \n5004: -​\n5005: Confirm applications, services, \n5006: and data are accurate and \n5007: complete. \n5008: 2.​\n5009: Security Scans \n5010: -​\n5011: Run security scans to confirm \n5012: no trace of the original threat \n5013: remains. \n5014: 3.​\n5015: Performance Check \n5016: -​\n5017: Check \n5018: performance \n5019: under \n5020: normal load before declaring \n5021: systems production-ready. \n5022: 4.​ Obtain Sign-Off \n5023: -​\n5024: Get sign-off from IT, security, \n5025: and business owners before \n5026: going live. \n5027: ●​\n5028: Returning to Normal Operations \n5029: 1.​\n5030: Gradual Rollout \n5031: -​\n5032: Bring systems back online \n5033: gradually, monitoring closely \n5034: for renewed issues. \n5035: 2.​\n5036: Notify Stakeholders \n5037: -​\n5038: Inform \n5039: stakeholders \n5040: and \n5041: users \n5042: that \n5043: services \n5044: are \n5045: restored and safe to use. \n5046: 3.​\n5047: Resume Monitoring \n5048: -​\n5049: Resume normal monitoring \n5050: routines \n5051: rather \n5052: than \n5053: emergency-level \n5054: watchfulness. \n5055: 4.​ Lessons Learned \n5056: -​\n5057: Document lessons learned \n5058: and \n5059: update \n5060: the \n5061: incident \n5062: response plan accordingly. \n5063:  \n5064:  \n5065: PAGE 26 \n5066: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n5067:  \n5068: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n5069: ITAS311-T \n5070: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n5071: \f \n5072:  \n5073: E \n5074: POST INCIDENT REVIEW \n5075:  \n5076: POST INCIDENT REVIEW \n5077: ●​\n5078: What happened, and when \n5079: ●​\n5080: Were the documented procedures followed, \n5081: and were they adequate? \n5082: ●​\n5083: What slowed the recovery down? \n5084: ●​\n5085: What do we do  differently next time? \n5086:  \n5087: FIND THE REAL CAUSE, NOT JUST THE SYMPTOM \n5088: ●​\n5089: Symptom: \"The server went down.\"​\n5090: Real cause: nobody was watching the disk \n5091: space, and nobody had ever tested a restore.​\n5092: An easy method: keep asking \"why?\" until \n5093: you reach something you can actually fix. This \n5094: is called the 5 Whys. \n5095: ●​\n5096: Check if the backup really worked \n5097: This is where the restore gets judged. Two \n5098: questions: \n5099: ○​\n5100: How much data did we lose, and \n5101: could we afford to lose that much? \n5102: (This is the RPO.) \n5103: ○​\n5104: How long were we down, and was \n5105: that fast enough? (This is the RTO.) \n5106: ○​\n5107: A backup that turns out damaged or \n5108: three weeks old is itself a finding. \n5109: ●​\n5110: Every finding needs a person in charge and \n5111: a due date.  \n5112: ○​\n5113: Then update the response plan, the \n5114: backup policy, and the step-by-step \n5115: guides. \n5116: ○​\n5117: Numbers to track: how long it took \n5118: to notice the problem (MTTD), how \n5119: long it took to fix it (MTTR), total \n5120: downtime, data lost, and cost. \n5121:  \n5122: F \n5123: DOCUMENTATION \n5124:  \n5125: DOCUMENTATION \n5126: ●​\n5127: Documentation \n5128: starts the moment you \n5129: detect the incident  \n5130: ●​\n5131: You do not write it at the end.  \n5132: ○​\n5133: It is a running log. Responders write \n5134: down each step, with the time, while \n5135: they are still working on the problem.  \n5136:  \n5137: WHAT TO WRITE DOWN: \n5138: ●​\n5139: Incident ID: one number that connects all \n5140: the records together \n5141: ●​\n5142: Discovery: when it was found, how it was \n5143: found, and who found it \n5144: ●​\n5145: Scope: which systems, applications, and data \n5146: were affected \n5147: ●​\n5148: Severity: how serious it was rated, and who \n5149: rated it \n5150: ●​\n5151: Actions: every step taken, in order, with the \n5152: exact time \n5153: ●​\n5154: Notifications: who was told, when, and \n5155: through what channel \n5156: ●​\n5157: Evidence: copies of logs, file checksums, and \n5158: a record of who handled them \n5159: ●​\n5160: Backup used: which backup was restored, its \n5161: date, and whether it was tested \n5162: ●​\n5163: Write facts only. Never write guesses. \n5164: ○​\n5165: The log is evidence. It can be used in \n5166: court, in an insurance claim, or in a \n5167: government \n5168: audit. \n5169: Keep \n5170: access \n5171: restricted, and keep the log for as \n5172: long as your policy requires.  \n5173:  \n5174: G \n5175: REPORTING \n5176:  \n5177: ONE INCIDENT, FIVE DIFFERENT READERS  \n5178: ●​\n5179: Technical team: the full timeline, the signs of \n5180: the attack, the real cause, and the fixes still to \n5181: be done \n5182: ●​\n5183: Management: a short summary of 1 to 2 \n5184: pages. Cost, downtime, business impact, and \n5185: what the team needs to stop it from \n5186: happening again \n5187: ●​\n5188: Legal team and government regulator: the \n5189: official breach notice, filed before the legal \n5190: deadline \n5191: ●​\n5192: Affected \n5193: users: \n5194: plain \n5195: language. \n5196: What \n5197: happened, what data of theirs was exposed, \n5198: and what they should do now \n5199: ●​\n5200: Law enforcement: only when a crime was \n5201: committed, such as fraud, extortion, or theft \n5202: of data \n5203:  \n5204: WHAT GOES INSIDE THE WRITTEN REPORT \n5205: ●​\n5206: Impact assessment  \n5207: ○​\n5208: is the section managers read twice. \n5209: It is how you justify the budget for \n5210: the fixes you are asking for.  \n5211: 1.​\n5212: Executive summary: written last, read first \n5213: 2.​\n5214: Incident overview: what it was, and how \n5215: serious \n5216: 3.​\n5217: Timeline: from detection to closing, with \n5218: times \n5219: 4.​\n5220: Systems and data affected: what was hit, \n5221: and how much \n5222: 5.​\n5223: Root cause: the real reason, not the symptom \n5224: 6.​\n5225: Response and recovery: what the team did, \n5226: including which backup was used \n5227: 7.​\n5228: Impact assessment: money lost, downtime, \n5229: data lost, and damage to reputation \n5230: 8.​\n5231: Lessons learned: what worked, and what did \n5232: not \n5233: 9.​\n5234: Recommendations: each one with a person \n5235: in charge and a due date \n5236:  \n5237: PAGE 27 \n5238: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n5239:  \n5240: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n5241: ITAS311-T \n5242: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n5243: \f \n5244:  \n5245: 10.​ Appendices: logs, screenshots, and other \n5246: evidence \n5247:  \n5248: REQUIRED BY LAW \n5249: ●​\n5250: Republic Act 10173, the Data Privacy Act of \n5251: 2012. NPC Circular 16-03.  \n5252: ○​\n5253: 72 hours: notify the National Privacy \n5254: Commission and the people whose \n5255: data was exposed. The clock starts \n5256: when you know, or have good reason \n5257: to believe, that a breach happened. It \n5258: does \n5259: not \n5260: start \n5261: when \n5262: your \n5263: investigation is finished. \n5264: ○​\n5265: 5 days: submit the full breach report, \n5266: counted from the day you discovered \n5267: the breach. Asking the NPC for an \n5268: exemption does not stop the clock. \n5269: ●​\n5270: The notice must state what happened, what \n5271: personal data was involved, what you did to \n5272: reduce the harm, and who to contact, such \n5273: as the Data Protection Officer. \n5274: ●​\n5275: This does get enforced. H&M Philippines was \n5276: ordered by the NPC to explain why it missed \n5277: the 72-hour deadline after a payment card \n5278: incident in 2018. \n5279:  \n5280: G \n5281: SUMMARY \n5282:  \n5283: KEY TAKEAWAYS \n5284: Follow up, Documentation, Reporting \n5285: ●​\n5286: Follow-up \n5287: turns \n5288: an \n5289: outage \n5290: into \n5291: an \n5292: improvement.  \n5293: ○​\n5294: Find \n5295: the \n5296: real \n5297: cause, \n5298: not \n5299: the \n5300: symptom. Give every fix a person in \n5301: charge and a due date. Confirm the \n5302: backup met the RPO and the RTO. \n5303: ●​\n5304: Documentation \n5305: is \n5306: written \n5307: during \n5308: the \n5309: incident, not after it.  \n5310: ○​\n5311: It is a running log of facts, with \n5312: times. It counts as evidence, so keep \n5313: it factual and keep access restricted. \n5314: ●​\n5315: Reporting changes with the reader.  \n5316: ○​\n5317: Five audiences, five levels of detail. \n5318: When personal data is involved, the \n5319: deadline is 72 hours by law. \n5320:  \n5321:  \n5322: PAGE 28 \n5323: UniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n5324:  \n5325: INFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \n5326: ITAS311-T \n5327: 3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n5328: \f\n\n(End of file - total 5328 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"nature, \nand \nseverity of the event \n○​\nDistinguish a true incident from a \nfalse alarm \n○​\nEarly, accurate identification limits \ndamage \n \nDETECTION SOURCES \n●​\nSigns of an incident can come from many \ndirections at once: automated alerts, log \ndata, employee reports, or simply unusual \nbehavior in system performance. \n○​\nAlerts from IDS/IPS, antivirus, and \nSIEM tools \n○​","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"C:\\xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","text":"nature, \nand \nseverity of the event \n○​\nDistinguish a true incident from a \nfalse alarm \n○​\nEarly, accurate identification limits \ndamage \n \nDETECTION SOURCES \n●​\nSigns of an incident can come from many \ndirections at once: automated alerts, log \ndata, employee reports, or simply unusual \nbehavior in system performance. \n○​\nAlerts from IDS/IPS, antivirus, and \nSIEM tools \n○​\nLog \nanalysis \n(system, \nnetwork, \napplication logs) \n○​\nReports from employees or end users \n○​\nAnomalies \nin \nsystem/network \nperformance \n \nVERIFICATION STEPS \n●​\nBefore any response begins, the team must \nconfirm the event is real, collect initial \nevidence, classify how serious it is, and \ndocument everything as it unfolds. \n○​\nConfirm the event is a real security \nincident \n○​\nGather initial evidence (timestamps, \naffected systems, indicators) \n○​\nClassify \nthe \nincident \ntype \nand \nseverity level \n \nPAGE 24 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n○​\nDocument \nfindings \nas \nthey're \ndiscovered (chain of custody) \n \nNEXT ACTIONS \n●​\nOnce an incident is confirmed, the team \nnotifies \nthe \nright \npeople, \nassigns \nownership, and preserves evidence. Setting \nthe stage for the response to move into \ncontainment. \n○​\nNotify the Incident Response Team \n○​\nAssign an incident handler/owner \n○​\nPreserve \nevidence \nfor \nfurther \nanalysis \n○​\nMove to the Containment phase \nonce confirmed \n \nD \nTHE THREE-PHASE RESPONSE MODEL \n \nTHE THREE-PHASE RESPONSE MODEL \n●​\nOnce \nan \nincident \nis \ndetected, \nthree \ncoordinated phases determine how well an \norganization survives it. \n●​\nNotification \n○​\nAlert the right people, at the right \ntime, through the right channels. \n●​\nResponse \n○​\nContain \nthe \nincident, \nstop \nthe \ndamage, and eliminate its root cause. \n●​\nRecovery \n○​\nRestore systems and data, verify \nintegrity, and resume operations. \n \nD1 \nNOTIFICATION \n \nWHO SHOULD BE INFORMED? \nNotification lists should be prepared before an \nincident occurs, not written from scratch during one. \n●​\nInternal IT & Security Teams \n○​\nThe incident response team, system \nadministrators, and network/security \nstaff who will investigate and act. \n●​\nManagement & Executives \n○​\nDepartment \nheads \nand \nsenior \nleadership \nwho \nneed \nsituational \nawareness and must approve major \ndecisions. \n●​\nLegal & Compliance Officers \n○​\nTo assess regulatory obligations, \nliability \nexposure, \nand mandatory \ndisclosure requirements. \n●​\nAffected Users & Customers \n○​\nAnyone whose data, accounts, or \nservices were impacted, so they can \nprotect themselves. \n \n●​\nLaw Enforcement & Regulators \n○​\nRequired for certain incidents (e.g., \ndata breaches, fraud) under law or \nindustry regulation. \n \nREPORTING & THE VALUE OF SPEED \n●​\nReporting the Incident \n1.​\nStandardized Reports \n-​\nUse a structured report to \ndetail what happened, when, \naffected systems, and initial \nimpact. \n2.​\nDefined Communication \n-​\nFollow \npre-defined \ncommunication \nchains \nto \nreach the right people first \ntime. \n3.​\nAudit Trail Logging \n-​\nLog every single step with \nprecise \ntimestamps \nand \nactions taken for later review. \n4.​ Factual Accuracy \n-​\nKeep reports strictly factual \nand \nupdated \nas \nnew \ninformation emerges; avoid \nspeculation. \n●​\nWhy Timely Notification Matters \n1.​\nLimits the Damage \n-​\nEarly warning lets security \nteams \nquickly \nisolate \nthe \nthreat \nbefore \nit \nspreads \nfurther. \n2.​\nMeets Legal Deadlines \n-​\nMany \nstrict \nbreach-notification laws set \nclear windows (e.g., 72 hours) \nfor compliance. \n3.​\nPreserves Vital Trust \n-​\nPrompt, \nhonest \ndisclosure \nprotects \norganizational \nreputation more than delayed \ninfo. \n4.​ Speeds Up Recovery \n-​\nThe \nsooner \nthe \nright \nstakeholders \nare engaged, \nthe faster the recovery phase \ncan begin. \n \nD2 \nRESPONSE \n \nCONTAINING THE INCIDENT \nContainment stops an incident from spreading \nwhile preserving evidence for investigation. \n \n \n \n \nPAGE 25 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n●​\nIsolate affected systems \n○​\nDisconnect compromised devices \nor network segments to stop lateral \nmovement. \n●​\nDisable compromised accounts \n○​\nSuspend or reset credentials that \nmay have been exploited by the \nattacker. \n●​\nApply short-term fixes \n○​\nBlock malicious IPs, close exploited \nports, or apply emergency firewall \nrules. \n●​\nPreserve evidence \n○​\nCapture logs, memory dumps, and \nsystem states before making further \nchanges. \n \nPREVENTING DAMAGE & REMOVING THE CAUSE \n●​\nPreventing Further Damage \n1.​\nContinuous Monitoring \n-​\nMonitor systems continuously \nfor \nsigns \nthe \nincident \nis \nspreading or recurring. \n2.​\nPatch Vulnerabilities \n-​\nPatch related vulnerabilities \nelsewhere in the environment \nbefore attackers exploit them. \n3.​\nSegment Networks \n-​\nStrengthen access controls \nand segment networks to \nlimit any future blast radius. \n4.​ Staff Communication \n-​\nCommunicate interim safety \nmeasures to staff and users \nwhile remediation continues. \n●​\nRemoving the Cause \n1.​\nRoot Cause Identification \n-​\nIdentify \nthe \nroot \ncause: \nmalware, \nmisconfiguration, \ncredentials, \nor \nunpatched \nflaw. \n2.​\nMalware Eradication \n-​\nEradicate \nmalicious \ncode, \nbackdoors, and unauthorized \naccounts left behind. \n3.​\nClose Vulnerabilities \n-​\nClose \nthe \nspecific \nvulnerability that allowed the \nincident to happen in the first \nplace. \n4.​ Eradication Verification \n-​\nVerify eradication with scans \nand audits before declaring \nthe environment clean. \n \n \nD3 \nRECOVERY \n \nRESTORING SYSTEMS & DATA FROM BACKUPS  \nRecovery brings the business back online using \nclean, verified backups — never data that may still \nbe compromised. \n●​\nIdentify a Clean Restore Point \n○​\nChoose a backup taken before the \nincident occurred, confirmed free of \nmalware or tampering. \n●​\nRebuild on Trusted Media \n○​\nReinstall \nsystems \nfrom \nverified, \nuninfected \nsources \nrather \nthan \nrestoring over a compromised host. \n●​\nRestore in Priority Order \n○​\nBring back critical business systems \nfirst, based on the organization's \nrecovery priorities. \n \nTESTING SYSTEMS & RETURNING TO NORMAL \n●​\nTesting if Systems Work Properly \n1.​\nVerify Functionality \n-​\nConfirm applications, services, \nand data are accurate and \ncomplete. \n2.​\nSecurity Scans \n-​\nRun security scans to confirm \nno trace of the original threat \nremains. \n3.​\nPerformance Check \n-​\nCheck \nperformance \nunder \nnormal load before declaring \nsystems production-ready. \n4.​ Obtain Sign-Off \n-​\nGet sign-off from IT, security, \nand business owners before \ngoing live. \n●​\nReturning to Normal Operations \n1.​\nGradual Rollout \n-​\nBring systems back online \ngradually, monitoring closely \nfor renewed issues. \n2.​\nNotify Stakeholders \n-​\nInform \nstakeholders \nand \nusers \nthat \nservices \nare \nrestored and safe to use. \n3.​\nResume Monitoring \n-​\nResume normal monitoring \nroutines \nrather \nthan \nemergency-level \nwatchfulness. \n4.​ Lessons Learned \n-​\nDocument lessons learned \nand \nupdate \nthe \nincident \nresponse plan accordingly. \n \n \nPAGE 26 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \nE \nPOST INCIDENT REVIEW \n \nPOST INCIDENT REVIEW \n●​\nWhat happened, and when \n●​\nWere the documented procedures followed, \nand were they adequate? \n●​\nWhat slowed the recovery down? \n●​\nWhat do we do  differently next time? \n \nFIND THE REAL CAUSE, NOT JUST THE SYMPTOM \n●​\nSymptom: \"The server went down.\"​\nReal cause: nobody was watching the disk \nspace, and nobody had ever tested a restore.​\nAn easy method: keep asking \"why?\" until \nyou reach something you can actually fix. This \nis called the 5 Whys. \n●​\nCheck if the backup really worked \nThis is where the restore gets judged. Two \nquestions: \n○​\nHow much data did we lose, and \ncould we afford to lose that much? \n(This is the RPO.) \n○​\nHow long were we down, and was \nthat fast enough? (This is the RTO.) \n○​\nA backup that turns out damaged or \nthree weeks old is itself a finding. \n●​\nEvery finding needs a person in charge and \na due date.  \n○​\nThen update the response plan, the \nbackup policy, and the step-by-step \nguides. \n○​\nNumbers to track: how long it took \nto notice the problem (MTTD), how \nlong it took to fix it (MTTR), total \ndowntime, data lost, and cost. \n \nF \nDOCUMENTATION \n \nDOCUMENTATION \n●​\nDocumentation \nstarts the moment you \ndetect the incident  \n●​\nYou do not write it at the end.  \n○​\nIt is a running log. Responders write \ndown each step, with the time, while \nthey are still working on the problem.  \n \nWHAT TO WRITE DOWN: \n●​\nIncident ID: one number that connects all \nthe records together \n●​\nDiscovery: when it was found, how it was \nfound, and who found it \n●​\nScope: which systems, applications, and data \nwere affected \n●​\nSeverity: how serious it was rated, and who \nrated it \n●​\nActions: every step taken, in order, with the \nexact time \n●​\nNotifications: who was told, when, and \nthrough what channel \n●​\nEvidence: copies of logs, file checksums, and \na record of who handled them \n●​\nBackup used: which backup was restored, its \ndate, and whether it was tested \n●​\nWrite facts only. Never write guesses. \n○​\nThe log is evidence. It can be used in \ncourt, in an insurance claim, or in a \ngovernment \naudit. \nKeep \naccess \nrestricted, and keep the log for as \nlong as your policy requires.  \n \nG \nREPORTING \n \nONE INCIDENT, FIVE DIFFERENT READERS  \n●​\nTechnical team: the full timeline, the signs of \nthe attack, the real cause, and the fixes still to \nbe done \n●​\nManagement: a short summary of 1 to 2 \npages. Cost, downtime, business impact, and \nwhat the team needs to stop it from \nhappening again \n●​\nLegal team and government regulator: the \nofficial breach notice, filed before the legal \ndeadline \n●​\nAffected \nusers: \nplain \nlanguage. \nWhat \nhappened, what data of theirs was exposed, \nand what they should do now \n●​\nLaw enforcement: only when a crime was \ncommitted, such as fraud, extortion, or theft \nof data \n \nWHAT GOES INSIDE THE WRITTEN REPORT \n●​\nImpact assessment  \n○​\nis the section managers read twice. \nIt is how you justify the budget for \nthe fixes you are asking for.  \n1.​\nExecutive summary: written last, read first \n2.​\nIncident overview: what it was, and how \nserious \n3.​\nTimeline: from detection to closing, with \ntimes \n4.​\nSystems and data affected: what was hit, \nand how much \n5.​\nRoot cause: the real reason, not the symptom \n6.​\nResponse and recovery: what the team did, \nincluding which backup was used \n7.​\nImpact assessment: money lost, downtime, \ndata lost, and damage to reputation \n8.​\nLessons learned: what worked, and what did \nnot \n9.​\nRecommendations: each one with a person \nin charge and a due date \n \nPAGE 27 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f \n \n10.​ Appendices: logs, screenshots, and other \nevidence \n \nREQUIRED BY LAW \n●​\nRepublic Act 10173, the Data Privacy Act of \n2012. NPC Circular 16-03.  \n○​\n72 hours: notify the National Privacy \nCommission and the people whose \ndata was exposed. The clock starts \nwhen you know, or have good reason \nto believe, that a breach happened. It \ndoes \nnot \nstart \nwhen \nyour \ninvestigation is finished. \n○​\n5 days: submit the full breach report, \ncounted from the day you discovered \nthe breach. Asking the NPC for an \nexemption does not stop the clock. \n●​\nThe notice must state what happened, what \npersonal data was involved, what you did to \nreduce the harm, and who to contact, such \nas the Data Protection Officer. \n●​\nThis does get enforced. H&M Philippines was \nordered by the NPC to explain why it missed \nthe 72-hour deadline after a payment card \nincident in 2018. \n \nG \nSUMMARY \n \nKEY TAKEAWAYS \nFollow up, Documentation, Reporting \n●​\nFollow-up \nturns \nan \noutage \ninto \nan \nimprovement.  \n○​\nFind \nthe \nreal \ncause, \nnot \nthe \nsymptom. Give every fix a person in \ncharge and a due date. Confirm the \nbackup met the RPO and the RTO. \n●​\nDocumentation \nis \nwritten \nduring \nthe \nincident, not after it.  \n○​\nIt is a running log of facts, with \ntimes. It counts as evidence, so keep \nit factual and keep access restricted. \n●​\nReporting changes with the reader.  \n○​\nFive audiences, five levels of detail. \nWhen personal data is involved, the \ndeadline is 72 hours by law. \n \n \nPAGE 28 \nUniverseFactory - BSIT-S-3A \n \nINFORMATION ASSURANCE AND SECURITY 2 \nITAS311-T \n3RD YEAR 1ST SEM – PROF. POPS MADRIAGA – MONDAY | 8:00AM-11:45AM \n\f","lineStart":4601,"lineEnd":5328,"totalLines":5328,"truncated":false}},"title":"xampp\\htdocs\\automation\\side-project\\data\\exams\\enaxqoqxb74s4g\\source.txt","time":{"start":1789822522424,"end":1789822522476}},"id":"prt_0b9bc52ae001TrWIYGTDO2OUSw","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9bc4c68001OGCjgNYxuHqoq5"}}
