{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789822960707,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_a8310fb7041d43bfafdfbea3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"[\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q01\",\n  \"question\": \"What do security logs record?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Approved financial transactions made by management.\",\n    \"The physical location of every employee each day.\",\n    \"Activities occurring on computers, networks, applications, and security devices.\",\n    \"Only the successful logins on a single host.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Security logs record activities occurring on computers, networks, applications, and security devices.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q02\",\n  \"question\": \"Which is listed as something security logs help organizations do?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Verify security controls.\",\n    \"Replace security monitoring tools.\",\n    \"Auto-generate user credentials.\",\n    \"Assign network permissions directly.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Logs help organizations detect unauthorized access, investigate incidents, verify security controls, and support audits and compliance.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q03\",\n  \"question\": \"Which set of questions does the reference list as important for logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Why, how, how much, and how often.\",\n    \"Who, why, which tool, and which vendor.\",\n    \"What, why, how, and how much.\",\n    \"Who, what, when, and where.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"The important log questions are who performed the action, what happened, when it happened, and where it originated.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q04\",\n  \"question\": \"Which event belongs under authentication logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Access to a restricted file.\",\n    \"A multi-factor authentication event.\",\n    \"A firewall connection.\",\n    \"An operating-system error.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Authentication logs capture successful and failed logins, password changes, multi-factor authentication events, and account lockouts.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q05\",\n  \"question\": \"Which event belongs under authorization logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A failed login attempt.\",\n    \"A software installation.\",\n    \"An administrative action.\",\n    \"A VPN connection.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Authorization logs cover access to restricted files, privilege changes, and administrative actions.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q06\",\n  \"question\": \"Which event belongs under network logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A password change.\",\n    \"A network scan.\",\n    \"A configuration change.\",\n    \"An application failure.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Network logs capture firewall connections, blocked traffic, VPN activity, and network scans.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q07\",\n  \"question\": \"Which event belongs under system and application logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Software installations.\",\n    \"Account lockouts.\",\n    \"Blocked traffic.\",\n    \"Privilege changes.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"System and application logs cover operating-system errors, software installations, configuration changes, and application failures.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q08\",\n  \"question\": \"Which set does NIST identify as important information for IDPS events?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Alert ID, vendor name, and sensor model.\",\n    \"Username, department, and manager.\",\n    \"Packet size, port number, and payload.\",\n    \"Timestamp, event type, event source, and reporting system.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"NIST identifies the timestamp, event type, event source, and sensor or reporting system as important information for IDPS events.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q09\",\n  \"question\": \"How does an IDS generally respond to detected activity?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"It blocks the activity automatically.\",\n    \"It generates alerts rather than blocking activity.\",\n    \"It quarantines the network segment.\",\n    \"It resets affected user accounts.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"An IDS generally generates alerts rather than blocking activity automatically.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q10\",\n  \"question\": \"Which of these can an IDS monitor?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Employee salary records.\",\n    \"Vendor service contracts.\",\n    \"Authentication attempts.\",\n    \"Physical access cards.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"An IDS can monitor network traffic, system activity, user behavior, application events, and authentication attempts.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q11\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement distinguishes NIDS from HIDS?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"NIDS monitors network traffic; HIDS monitors individual computers.\",\n    \"HIDS monitors network traffic; NIDS monitors servers.\",\n    \"NIDS only alerts; HIDS only blocks.\",\n    \"HIDS requires a SIEM; NIDS does not.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"A network-based IDS (NIDS) monitors network traffic, while a host-based IDS (HIDS) monitors individual computers or servers.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q12\",\n  \"question\": \"Which item appears in IDS log information per the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Processor temperature reading.\",\n    \"Employee badge number.\",\n    \"Router serial number.\",\n    \"Detection rule or signature.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"IDS log information includes the alert identification number, date and time, source and destination IPs, ports and protocol, detection rule or signature, attack type, severity, affected host, sensor, and response taken.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q13\",\n  \"question\": \"Which detection method combines multiple events to reveal a larger attack?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Protocol analysis.\",\n    \"Event correlation.\",\n    \"Signature-based detection.\",\n    \"Anomaly-based detection.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Event correlation combines multiple events to identify a larger attack.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q14\",\n  \"question\": \"Which action is part of verifying that security controls work as intended?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Confirming that logging is enabled on important devices.\",\n    \"Disabling logging on nonessential devices.\",\n    \"Removing timestamps from log entries.\",\n    \"Approving the final audit report.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Verification includes reviewing configurations, confirming logging is enabled, performing an authorized test intrusion, confirming the alert, and validating timestamps.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q15\",\n  \"question\": \"Where should security control testing be performed, per the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"On live production systems without notice.\",\n    \"In the vendor's data center only.\",\n    \"During the annual report review.\",\n    \"In an authorized, controlled environment.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Security control testing should be authorized and performed in a controlled environment.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q16\",\n  \"question\": \"Which practice is recommended for protecting logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Storing logs only on the systems that generate them.\",\n    \"Giving all administrators edit access to logs.\",\n    \"Storing logs in a centralized platform or SIEM.\",\n    \"Deleting logs after every incident.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Logs should be stored in a centralized log-management platform or SIEM and separated from the systems generating them.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q17\",\n  \"question\": \"How should logs be protected during transmission and storage?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"By encryption.\",\n    \"By compression.\",\n    \"By duplication.\",\n    \"By isolation.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Logs should be encrypted during transmission and storage.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q18\",\n  \"question\": \"Why should log storage be separated from the systems generating the logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"To reduce the cost of storage hardware.\",\n    \"To speed up log file writing.\",\n    \"To simplify software licensing.\",\n    \"To protect logs if a generating system is compromised.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Separate log storage keeps the logs safe if the systems that generate them are compromised, and helps monitor attempts to modify or delete them.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q19\",\n  \"question\": \"Which technique is recommended to protect the integrity of logs?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Renaming log files on a schedule.\",\n    \"Using hashes or append-only storage.\",\n    \"Compressing logs into archives.\",\n    \"Rotating log file locations.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Hashes, digital signatures, or append-only storage are used to protect log integrity.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q20\",\n  \"question\": \"Which function is part of log management per the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Authorizing user accounts.\",\n    \"Scheduling software updates.\",\n    \"Disposing of log data.\",\n    \"Assigning file permissions.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Log management includes generating, transmitting, storing, accessing, and disposing of log data.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q21\",\n  \"question\": \"Which item should security teams investigate during log review?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Printer maintenance records.\",\n    \"Meeting room bookings.\",\n    \"Software license counts.\",\n    \"Unexpected administrator activity.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Teams should investigate repeated failed logins, privilege escalation, disabled security services, malware detection, unexpected administrator activity, and large or unusual data transfers.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q22\",\n  \"question\": \"When suspicious activity is identified, what is the first action in the listed sequence?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Validate the alert.\",\n    \"Contain the activity.\",\n    \"Preserve evidence.\",\n    \"Improve security controls.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"The sequence starts by validating the alert, then identifying affected systems, preserving evidence, containing, investigating and documenting, and finally improving controls.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q23\",\n  \"question\": \"Signature-based analysis is also known as what?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Specification-based detection.\",\n    \"Anomaly detection.\",\n    \"Misuse detection.\",\n    \"Stateful analysis.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Signature-based analysis is also known as misuse detection because it scans for signatures that match recorded threats.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q24\",\n  \"question\": \"What does anomaly-based detection flag as a potential attack?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Any match to a known signature.\",\n    \"Any deviation from an established baseline of normal behavior.\",\n    \"Any improper use of a network protocol.\",\n    \"Any combination of two unrelated events.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Anomaly-based detection builds a baseline profile of standard operations and logs any deviation from it as a potential attack.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q25\",\n  \"question\": \"Stateful protocol analysis is also known as what?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Rule-based detection.\",\n    \"Misuse detection.\",\n    \"Behavior profiling.\",\n    \"Specification-based analysis.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Stateful protocol analysis, also called specification-based analysis, compares network activity against vendor profiles of how protocols should structurally behave.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q26\",\n  \"question\": \"What is a documented limitation of signature-based analysis?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Blindness to new zero-day vulnerabilities.\",\n    \"A high rate of false positives.\",\n    \"Heavy system resource consumption.\",\n    \"Dependence on vendor protocol profiles.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Signature-based analysis is entirely blind to new, zero-day vulnerabilities and morphing malware, though it is highly accurate for known threats.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q27\",\n  \"question\": \"What is a documented limitation of anomaly-based analysis?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"It cannot detect novel attacks.\",\n    \"It cannot build a baseline.\",\n    \"It produces a high rate of false positives.\",\n    \"It must track network pairings constantly.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Anomaly-based analysis causes a high rate of false positives from legitimate unusual user spikes or system changes.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q28\",\n  \"question\": \"What is a documented limitation of stateful protocol analysis?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"It fails to catch protocol manipulation.\",\n    \"It consumes heavy system resources.\",\n    \"It relies on learned history.\",\n    \"It ignores protocol state changes.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Stateful protocol analysis consumes heavy system resources because it continuously tracks state conditions across layers.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q29\",\n  \"question\": \"Which supplementary technique alerts defenders when pre-set operational limits are broken?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Threshold monitoring.\",\n    \"Resource profiling.\",\n    \"Executable profiling.\",\n    \"File integrity monitoring.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Threshold monitoring alerts defenders when pre-set operational limits are broken, such as a limit on failed login attempts.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q30\",\n  \"question\": \"Which supplementary technique tracks CPU, RAM, and bandwidth to find background malware?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Executable profiling.\",\n    \"Threshold monitoring.\",\n    \"Signature matching.\",\n    \"Resource profiling.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Resource profiling tracks system-wide metrics like CPU, RAM, and bandwidth to find sneaky background malware.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q31\",\n  \"question\": \"Which supplementary technique analyzes how software programs invoke system calls and read memory?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Resource profiling.\",\n    \"Executable profiling.\",\n    \"Threshold monitoring.\",\n    \"Anomaly profiling.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Executable profiling continuously analyzes how active programs invoke system calls and read system memory.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q32\",\n  \"question\": \"Which activity is part of how a HIDS works?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Controlling which devices join the network.\",\n    \"Encrypting the host's storage volume.\",\n    \"Comparing current file states against baseline snapshots.\",\n    \"Blocking all external IP addresses.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"A HIDS performs file integrity monitoring by comparing current states of critical files and configurations against baseline snapshots.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q33\",\n  \"question\": \"Which data does the HIDS agent collect directly from the host?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Kernel activities and authentication records.\",\n    \"Network switch configuration files.\",\n    \"Firewall rule sets.\",\n    \"Cloud billing summaries.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"The HIDS agent gathers system logs, application event logs, authentication records, and kernel activities directly from the host.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q34\",\n  \"question\": \"What is layered defense, also called defense in depth?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"One control repeated at several points in a system.\",\n    \"A backup strategy that duplicates one server several times.\",\n    \"A policy requiring two approvals per change.\",\n    \"Multiple security controls at different levels of an information system.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Layered defense uses multiple security controls at different levels so that if one layer fails, another can detect, prevent, or limit the attack.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q35\",\n  \"question\": \"Firewalls, segmentation, and NAC are examples in which security layer?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Data security.\",\n    \"Network security.\",\n    \"Application security.\",\n    \"Physical security.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Network security protects network connections and traffic with firewalls, segmentation, and NAC.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q36\",\n  \"question\": \"What does Network Access Control (NAC) determine?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Which servers need software patches.\",\n    \"Which log sources send data.\",\n    \"Which users and devices may connect to network resources.\",\n    \"Which ports may be opened by applications.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"NAC controls which users and devices are allowed to connect to and use network resources.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q37\",\n  \"question\": \"Which is a possible NAC decision?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Defer.\",\n    \"Approve.\",\n    \"Notify.\",\n    \"Restrict.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Possible NAC decisions are allow, deny, restrict, and quarantine.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q38\",\n  \"question\": \"What is the general sequence followed in the NAC process?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Identification, authentication, assessment, authorization, monitoring.\",\n    \"Assessment, identification, monitoring, authorization, authentication.\",\n    \"Authentication, monitoring, identification, assessment, authorization.\",\n    \"Authorization, authentication, assessment, identification, monitoring.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"NAC generally follows identification, authentication, security assessment, authorization, and monitoring.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q39\",\n  \"question\": \"What is the primary purpose of host isolation?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Repairing damaged hardware.\",\n    \"Containment.\",\n    \"Installing security updates.\",\n    \"Reconfiguring the network.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Host isolation separates a suspected compromised host from other systems, and its primary purpose is containment.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch2-q40\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement defines system hardening?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Physically securing server rooms from intruders.\",\n    \"Replacing old hardware with newer models.\",\n    \"Reducing vulnerabilities by configuring systems per security best practices.\",\n    \"Backing up data to removable media.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"System hardening reduces or eliminates vulnerabilities by configuring the operating system, applications, network services, and user accounts according to security best practices.\"\n}\n]","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\iasexam\\c2q.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\iasexam\\c2q.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\iasexam\\c2q.json","time":{"start":1789822960688,"end":1789822960704}},"id":"prt_0b9c2a80e001Ja4hOGgrxMwH5L","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9c2a0a5001y0vDAGJ4QepVGN"}}
