{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789823013146,"sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_228260cf3f2f40468ac0bcd9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"[\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q01\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement defines Business Continuity Management (BCM)?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A framework of policies, processes, and capabilities that prepare a business for disruptive incidents.\",\n    \"A documented set of procedures followed only during a single disruption.\",\n    \"A technical plan for restoring servers after an outage.\",\n    \"A financial reserve maintained for unexpected expenses.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"BCM is an organization-wide framework of policies, processes, and capabilities that prepare a business to prevent, respond to, and recover from disruptive incidents.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q02\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement defines a Business Continuity Plan (BCP)?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A framework of policies for managing everyday operations.\",\n    \"Documented procedures for keeping critical functions running during and right after a disruption.\",\n    \"A plan for restoring data after a system failure.\",\n    \"A schedule for reviewing the continuity program annually.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"A BCP is the documented set of procedures an organization follows to keep critical functions running during, and immediately after, a disruption.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q03\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement defines Disaster Recovery (DR)?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"An organization-wide continuity framework.\",\n    \"A set of procedures for keeping functions running during a disruption.\",\n    \"A technical, IT-focused effort to restore systems, data, and infrastructure after an outage.\",\n    \"A policy governing employee emergency responses.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Disaster Recovery is a technical, IT-focused subset of continuity planning concerned with restoring systems, data, and infrastructure after an outage.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q04\",\n  \"question\": \"What does the Recovery Time Objective (RTO) measure?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"The maximum acceptable amount of data loss.\",\n    \"The time needed to verify restored data.\",\n    \"The absolute limit of disruption before survival is threatened.\",\n    \"The maximum acceptable time to restore a business function after a disruption.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"RTO is the maximum acceptable time to restore a business function after a disruption occurs.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q05\",\n  \"question\": \"What does the Recovery Point Objective (RPO) define?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"The maximum acceptable data loss as a point in time for restoration.\",\n    \"The time needed to resume normal processing after restoration.\",\n    \"The maximum downtime the business can survive.\",\n    \"The minimum service level during a disruption.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"RPO is the maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured as the point in time to which data must be restored.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q06\",\n  \"question\": \"What does Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) represent?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"The preferred target for restoring services.\",\n    \"The absolute limit of disruption before the organization's survival is threatened.\",\n    \"The average downtime across all past outages.\",\n    \"The budget for continuity investments.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"MTD is the absolute limit of disruption a function can withstand before survival of the business is threatened.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q07\",\n  \"question\": \"What does Work Recovery Time (WRT) cover?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"The time to restore systems after an outage.\",\n    \"Time lost while waiting for vendor support.\",\n    \"The time needed after restoration to verify data and resume normal processing.\",\n    \"The time between detection and notification.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"WRT is the time needed after systems are restored to verify data and resume normal processing.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q08\",\n  \"question\": \"How does the reference order incident, crisis, and disaster?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"By the amount of media coverage received.\",\n    \"By the number of departments involved.\",\n    \"By the duration of the response effort.\",\n    \"By escalating levels of severity.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Incident, crisis, and disaster are escalating levels of severity: an incident is minor, a crisis threatens objectives and reputation, and a disaster causes major, prolonged damage.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q09\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement describes resilience?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"The ability to absorb and adapt to disruption without losing function.\",\n    \"The capacity to restore data from backups.\",\n    \"The speed of a network connection.\",\n    \"The number of backup sites available.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Resilience is the ability to absorb and adapt to disruption without losing function.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q10\",\n  \"question\": \"What is a single point of failure (SPOF)?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A device that fails during peak load.\",\n    \"A component whose failure stops the entire process.\",\n    \"A server that has no backup.\",\n    \"A process that depends on one vendor.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"A SPOF is any single component whose failure stops the entire process.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q11\",\n  \"question\": \"What does continuity of operations emphasize?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Eventual restoration of services only.\",\n    \"Reducing the cost of disruptions.\",\n    \"Sustained delivery of critical services throughout a disruption.\",\n    \"Timely notification of stakeholders.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Continuity of operations is the sustained delivery of critical services and functions throughout a disruption, not merely their eventual restoration.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q12\",\n  \"question\": \"Why is MTD described as a boundary rather than a recovery target?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"It is the average downtime across incidents.\",\n    \"It is adjusted after every test.\",\n    \"It is optional for non-critical functions.\",\n    \"It is the outer limit that recovery strategies must stay within.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"MTD is not a target to aim for; it is the outer boundary every recovery strategy must stay inside before consequences become unacceptable.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q13\",\n  \"question\": \"Which factor drives Maximum Tolerable Downtime, per the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Financial impact such as lost revenue and penalties.\",\n    \"The number of backup locations.\",\n    \"The size of the continuity team.\",\n    \"The type of operating system deployed.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"MTD is driven by financial impact, reputational damage, regulatory and legal obligations, and operational dependencies.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q14\",\n  \"question\": \"Why must MTD differ by business function, according to the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Because functions use different software.\",\n    \"Because some functions, like payroll, tolerate more downtime than others like trading.\",\n    \"Because each function has a separate budget.\",\n    \"Because every function reports to different executives.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"MTD differs by function; for example, payroll tolerates more downtime than trading.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q15\",\n  \"question\": \"How does the cost of downtime behave as time passes?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"It stays constant until the MTD is reached.\",\n    \"It declines as buffers absorb the impact.\",\n    \"It accelerates and compounds over time.\",\n    \"It resets after each recovery milestone.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Impact rarely rises in a straight line; it accelerates, with each additional day compounding financial, legal, and reputational cost.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q16\",\n  \"question\": \"How do RPO, RTO, and WRT relate to the MTD?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"They each set separate limits unrelated to MTD.\",\n    \"They are measured after normal operations resume.\",\n    \"They replace the need for an MTD.\",\n    \"Their combined values must fit inside the MTD boundary.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Together, RPO plus RTO plus WRT must fit comfortably inside the MTD boundary.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q17\",\n  \"question\": \"Which is the first step to determine acceptable downtime?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Identify critical processes the organization cannot operate without.\",\n    \"Derive RTO and RPO targets.\",\n    \"Set the MTD threshold.\",\n    \"Model impact over time.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"The first step is to identify the critical processes, the functions the organization cannot operate without.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q18\",\n  \"question\": \"Which is the final step in determining acceptable downtime?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Consult stakeholders and regulators.\",\n    \"Derive RTO and RPO targets safely inside the ceiling.\",\n    \"Quantify impact over time.\",\n    \"Identify critical processes.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"After setting the MTD threshold, the final step is working backward to set RTO and RPO targets safely inside that ceiling.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q19\",\n  \"question\": \"Which statement defines a Business Impact Analysis (BIA)?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A method for ranking project risks by likelihood.\",\n    \"A financial audit of recovery investments.\",\n    \"A process to identify and evaluate the effects of a disruption on critical operations.\",\n    \"A review of the continuity plan's version history.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"A BIA is a process to identify and evaluate the effects of a disruption to critical business operations.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q20\",\n  \"question\": \"Which comparison distinguishes a BIA from a risk assessment?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"BIA asks what could go wrong; risk assessment asks how fast to recover.\",\n    \"Both answer the same question in different terms.\",\n    \"Risk assessment measures impact; BIA measures likelihood.\",\n    \"BIA asks the impact and required recovery speed; risk assessment asks what could go wrong.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"Risk assessment asks what could go wrong, while BIA asks what the impact is and how quickly recovery must happen.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q21\",\n  \"question\": \"Which metric sets the minimum service level the business must maintain during a disruption?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"MBCO.\",\n    \"RTO.\",\n    \"WRT.\",\n    \"RPO.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"The Minimum Business Continuity Objective (MBCO) is the minimum service level the business must maintain during a disruption.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q22\",\n  \"question\": \"Which impact type covers SLA breaches, penalties, and contract termination?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Reputational.\",\n    \"Contractual.\",\n    \"Operational.\",\n    \"Strategic.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Contractual impact covers SLA breaches, penalties, and contract termination.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q23\",\n  \"question\": \"How many increasing time intervals does the BIA process use to assess impact?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Three.\",\n    \"Four.\",\n    \"Five.\",\n    \"Seven.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"The BIA steps assess impact at seven increasing time intervals across the impact categories.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q24\",\n  \"question\": \"Which tool is listed for carrying out a BIA?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Structured interviews and workshops.\",\n    \"Network vulnerability scans.\",\n    \"Server backup logs.\",\n    \"Firewall rule reviews.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"Common BIA tools include structured interviews and workshops, questionnaires or surveys, dependency mapping, impact-over-time analysis, criticality ranking matrices, and BCM software.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q25\",\n  \"question\": \"Why must a continuity plan be regularly reviewed?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Because testing is too expensive.\",\n    \"Because regulators require annual updates.\",\n    \"Because plans drift out of step as people, systems, and risks change.\",\n    \"Because it is written only once.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"A continuity plan is only accurate the day it is written; people, systems, and risks change, so the plan must be reviewed to stay current.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q26\",\n  \"question\": \"Which event should trigger a plan review, per the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"A change in the weather forecast.\",\n    \"A merger or organizational restructure.\",\n    \"A quarterly marketing report.\",\n    \"A staff training session.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Reviews are triggered by scheduled intervals, organizational or technology changes such as restructures and mergers, and after an incident or audit finding.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q27\",\n  \"question\": \"Who provides independent assurance that the plan review was thorough and objective?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Senior management.\",\n    \"The BC manager.\",\n    \"Internal audit.\",\n    \"Department heads.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 2,\n  \"explanation\": \"Internal audit provides independent assurance that the review was thorough and objective.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q28\",\n  \"question\": \"Which reason explains why continuity plans should be tested?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"To assign new contacts.\",\n    \"To validate assumptions and uncover gaps before a real event.\",\n    \"To approve the annual budget.\",\n    \"To update the BIA questionnaire.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 1,\n  \"explanation\": \"Testing validates assumptions, uncovers missing steps or outdated contacts, trains staff, and satisfies compliance.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q29\",\n  \"question\": \"Which step completes the testing cycle described in the reference?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Plan.\",\n    \"Test.\",\n    \"Review.\",\n    \"Update the plan with lessons learned.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 3,\n  \"explanation\": \"The testing cycle is Plan, Test, Review results, then Update the plan by folding lessons learned back into it.\"\n},\n{\n  \"id\": \"ch4-q30\",\n  \"question\": \"Which key takeaway states that every recovery target must remain beneath the MTD?\",\n  \"choices\": [\n    \"Maximum Tolerable Downtime is the ceiling each recovery target must fit beneath.\",\n    \"RTO is the ceiling each target must exceed.\",\n    \"RPO determines the budget for recovery.\",\n    \"WRT replaces the MTD in testing.\"\n  ],\n  \"correctAnswer\": 0,\n  \"explanation\": \"A core takeaway is that Maximum Tolerable Downtime is the ceiling every recovery target (RTO, RPO, WRT) must fit beneath.\"\n}\n]","filePath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\iasexam\\c4q.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"C:\\Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\iasexam\\c4q.json","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"Users\\kelly\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\opencode\\iasexam\\c4q.json","time":{"start":1789823013120,"end":1789823013143}},"id":"prt_0b9c36213001b62FVu4WI4URwT","sessionID":"ses_f464447b3ffe6otWPhKUxoCp1k","messageID":"msg_0b9c35896001RpzfYn554PCm5G"}}
